Conventions used in this Manual

@rem <pre>
Courier
@rem </pre>
 - Screen displays and data from the computer will be displayed in the 
Courier typeface when available in the current document format.

Italics
 - Directories and filenames are displayed in italics. Key sequences also 
use italics.

Bold
 - Program names relating to Zeus are displayed in Bold.

@rem <pre>
 <-'
@rem </pre>
 - Indicates that the line continues despite the page width break.


So What is Zeus?

Zeus is a Bulletin Board System (BBS) program. It allows people to call 
into a BBS on your computer (via a modem, or telnet over the Internet) 
which you design, to share messages, files, play games, converse together 
and whatever your imagination can think of for your users.

@endnode


@node "Manual.Install" "2 - Installation"

Chapter 2 - Installing Zeus for the first time


2.0.1 Zeus requirements

Zeus requires:

 - Any Amiga computer with Kickstart 2.04 installed.
 - A hard drive with 5 megabytes free.
 - Two megabytes of RAM or more.

 - ARexx must be installed.


2.0.2 Extracting the Zeus disks

Zeus is supplied either on three distribution disks, and a third-party 
utility supplement disk, or on CD-ROM.  If you have obtained floppy disk 
images via an electronic medium they will be archived using the DMS packer. 
To install Zeus from these files, you must get a copy of DMS (available on 
AmiNet), and extract each Zeus file onto four separate floppy disks.

This can be achieved with the following commands:

@rem <PRE>
 <Insert Disk 1 in df0:>
 1> dms write Z1.DMS to df0:

 <Insert Disk 2 in df0:>
 2> dms write Z2.DMS to df0:

 <Insert Disk 3 in df0:>
 3> dms write Z3.DMS to df0:

 <Insert Disk 4 in df0:>
 4> dms write Z4.dms to df0:
@rem </PRE>

This is not required if you have Zeus on CD-ROM.


2.0.3 Installing Zeus

Floppy Disks: Once you have the three Zeus distribution disks unpacked, you 
can begin to install Zeus. Installation is performed using the standard 
Commodore Installer program.

 - Insert Zeus Distribution Disk 1 into any drive.
 - On the Workbench screen, double-click on the ZDIST icon.
 - Double click on the "Install" icon.
 - Follow the installation instructions.
 - When you are prompted to select a directory to install Zeus into, select 
a partition with the required disk space, and select "Make new Drawer". 
Call this new drawer "Zeus". Select "Proceed".
 - Follow the installation instructions.

CD-ROM: As above, but click on the Install icon which is on the CD-ROM.

If all went well, the following lines will have been added to your "S:User-
Startup" file, which is executed upon bootup.

@rem <PRE>
Assign Zeus:      <Zeus-Directory>
Assign RIPICONS:  Zeus:RIP/Icons
Assign RIPFONTS:  Zeus:RIP/Fonts
Assign Logs:      Zeus:Logs
Assign Doors:     <Door-Path>
Assign Libs:      Zeus:Libs ADD
Assign Devs:      Zeus:Devs ADD
SetEnv FileEditor c:ED %s
@rem </PRE>

Where <Zeus-Directory> is the directory you chose to install Zeus into. The 
<Door-Path> is the directory you chose in the Installer to store the doors, 
which Doors: will be assigned to. After installation is complete, please 
reboot your machine.



2.0.4 Libraries

Zeus libraries are not stored in LIBS:. This is because there are quite a 
few of them, and some door emulation libraries may conflict with other 
programs. The line "Assign Libs: Zeus:Libs ADD" will cause the search path 
for system libraries to be widened to both the Libs: directory and the 
Zeus:Libs/ Directory.

In addition to the libraries provided with the Zeus Distribution disks, you 
must have the following libraries installed in LIBS:

@rem <PRE>
        rexxsyslib.library (Kickstart 2.04 or above)
        reqtools.library   (V37 or above)
@rem </PRE>

For file transfers on the BBS the following libraries are required and 
supplied on Zeus Distribution Disk #4 as "LIBS.LHA"

@rem <PRE>
        xprxmodem.library
        xprymodem.library
        xprzmodem.library
        xprzedzap.library
@rem </PRE>

To share the serial port with other applications at the same time you also 
need the owndevunit.library, also supplied in this archive. See the section 
in this manual on Owndevunit.library.


2.0.5 FileEditor ENV Variable

The ENV variable, "FileEditor" stores the command line to execute your 
local editor. This defaults to the Commodore "ED" program, stored in the C: 
directory as provided with all versions of Workbench.

The "%s" is expanded to the name of the file to be edited. Other possible 
values for "ENV:FileEditor" can be set by using the following lines instead 
of the one written in S:User-Startup:

CygnusEd@{uu}

@rem <PRE>
 SetEnv FileEditor "CED -keepio %s"
@rem </PRE>

GoldEd@{uu}

@rem <PRE>
 SetEnv FileEditor "Ed %s Y"
@rem </PRE>

Amiga Default ED@{uu}

@rem <PRE>
 SetEnv FileEditor "Ed %s"
@rem </PRE>


2.1.0 After installation

After the successful installation of Zeus, a few points must be checked 
before starting it up for the first time.


2.1.1 ARexx?

Is ARexx installed? If it is not, the following lines must be added to your 
"S:User-Startup" startup file before Zeus:

@rem <PRE>
Sys:System/RexxMast >NIL:
Sys:RexxC/rxlib rexxsupport.library 0 -30 0
@rem </PRE>

It is also possible to start ARexx by placing the RexxMast program in the 
WBStartUp directory, but this method can complicate matters (it involves 
RexxMast opening a window to tell you it has started, and this can 
sometimes stop Workbench changing ScreenMode).


2.2.0 First time usage

After your installation has completed, you must first reset your Amiga 
before Zeus may be loaded.

Once your Amiga has rebooted, open up the Zeus window from the Workbench, 
which will be installed in the path you selected in the Installer program.

@{"Zeus Drawer Picture" Link "ZeusDrawer.GIF/main"}

The following drawers will be shown:

@rem <PRE>
 Bin/           - CLI based binary utilities for Zeus
 Config/        - Configuration files for Zeus and utilities)
 Conversion/    - Conversion utilities from other BBS programs
 Developers/    - Developer information for Zeus
 Docs/          - Documentation files
 Doors/         - Online door programs
 Logs/          - Text log files
 FileTypes/     - Icon files for your own projects
@rem </PRE>

@{"File Types Picture" Link "FileTypes.gif/main"}

@rem <PRE>
 Libs/          - Zeus System Library files
 Prefs/         - GUI Configuration utilities
@rem </PRE>

@{"Preferences Picture" Link "Preferences.gif/main"}

@rem <PRE>
 Rexx/          - Offline ARexx scripts
 Shortcuts/     - Batch scripts for simple BBS operations
 Scripts/       - Online BBS menus and scripts
 Text/          - Online BBS texts
 Utils/         - GUI utilities for Zeus
@rem </PRE>

@{"Utilities Picture" Link "Utilities.gif/main"}



Also, the Zeus executable and a "ClickForColors" icon will be shown. The 
latter will reset your main Workbench screen to use "MagicWB" colours, the 
colour scheme of the Zeus icons.

The main Zeus icon will boot up the BBS in full, but before we click on 
this, enter the Prefs drawer and double-click on "StartUp".

@{"StartUp Picture" Link "StartUp.gif/main"}

This is the program startup GUI. It defines all the programs that will be 
started after the main Zeus program has been loaded up. Each program in the 
list can be assigned a "Stack size", "Priority" and "Delay".  Delay 
specifies the time to wait in seconds before executing the next program in 
the list.

The list will contain:

@rem <PRE>
Zeus:Bin/FileControl
Zeus File Server- controls all aspects of the internal file database
Zeus:Bin/MsgControl
Zeus Message Server- controls all aspects of the internal message database.
Zeus:Bin/BBSLine 0 Local.zeus
Sets up the local line task.
Zeus:Bin/BBSLine 1 Remote.zeus
Sets up the first serial line task (defaults to serial.device).
Zeus:Bin/BBSLine 2
task (defaults to znm.device).
Zeus:Bin/Watchers/ANSI 0 JUMPY ICONIFY
Loads up the local line watcher in iconify mode.
Zeus:Bin/Watchers/ANSI 1 JUMPY ICONIFY
Loads up the first remote line watcher in iconify mode.
;Zeus:Bin/Watchers/RIP 0 NTSC
Not loaded. RIP server (local)
;Zeus:Bin/Watchers/RIP 1 NTSC
Not loaded. RIP server (remote)
;Zeus:Bin/Connector 1 ShowGui
Not loaded. Remote line answerer.
Zeus:Utils/ChatStatus
ChatStatus icon on Control screen.
Zeus:Utils/Control
Zeus Control Window/Screen.
Zeus:Utils/Voyeur
Lines Watcher
Zeus:Bin/ZeusDock
Program launcher.
@rem </PRE>


Let's add a new entry:

Click Add. Change the (New) string to

@rem <PRE>
Zeus:Bin/PostMan USER "<Your User Name>" EXECUTE "rx Zeus:Rexx/Logon.rexx"
@rem </PRE>

Hit Up once to move this entry before the ZeusDock program.

Hit Save. Then press Quit.

This has set up the PostMan utility, which creates an icon on your Control 
window informing you whether you have mail waiting.


2.2.1 PostMan

The previous steps set up the PostMan utility, which creates an icon on 
your Control window informing you whether you have mail waiting or not. 
Clicking on the icon will run the line specified with the "EXECUTE" 
argument (an ARexx script is provided with Zeus which places the line at 
your login point). You can also specify a command to execute when new mail 
is received using the "MAILCMD" argument.

@{"No Mail Waiting" Link "PostManNoMail.gif/main"}

No Mail Waiting

@{"1 Message Waiting" Link "PostManMail.gif/main"}

1 Message Waiting

@rem <PRE>
Usage: PostMan EXECUTE/A,USER,MAILCMD,XPOS/N,YPOS/N,FORUSER=FOR/K

   EXECUTE  - Command to execute upon clicking on icon
   USER     - Name of user to check
   MAILCMD  - Command to execute upon receiving mail
   XPOS     - X Position to place icon
   YPOS     - Y Position to place icon
   FORUSER  - Display this name under the icon in Control
              to enable use of more than one PostMan
@rem </PRE>

FORUSER: Specifying this on the command line changes the text under the 
icon on Control to reflect the name given here: eg: "SysOp: 1" and 
"CoSysOp: 3".  The idea is to allow two or more PostMan's to run and to be 
able to tell which PostMan shows who's message count.


PostMan playing a sample with the DSound program a sample when new mail is 
received:

@rem <PRE>
Zeus:Bin/PostMan EXECUTE "rx Zeus:Rexx/Logon/Logon.rexx" USER "Nick Loman" 
MAILCMD "Doors:ChatPage/DSound Doors:ChatPage/8svx/Alert"
@rem </PRE>

PostMan running it's window at X=500, Y=200:

@rem <PRE>
Zeus:Bin/PostMan EXECUTE "rx Zeus:Rexx/Logon/Logon.rexx" USER "Nick Loman" 
XPOS 500 YPOS 200
@rem </PRE>



2.2.2 Loading Zeus

Now, let's load up Zeus. Zeus can be loaded from the CLI or from the 
Workbench. We recommend you add the following entry at the bottom of your 
S:user-startup file (after Zeus's automatically added section):

@rem <PRE>
run <>NIL: Zeus:Zeus
@rem </PRE>

Running Zeus from the CLI is recommended as the pathing information from 
the CLI is maintained, and any programs launched from within the BBS will 
respect these settings.

Double click on the Workbench icon.

@{"Zeus Loading" Link "ZeusLoading.gif/main"}

The "ZEUS" loading screen will immediately appear, and show the progress of 
the startup. Zeus first reads the configuration, loads the user files and 
then starts executing the programs in the Program startup list.



2.2.3 The Control Window

Soon after Zeus reports that it is RUNNING PROGS, the Control Window should 
pop up on a public screen named "ZEUS". This Control Window allows you to 
access the preference and utility programs from the menus, as well as your 
own configuration programs. The Control Window may open on your WB screen 
by turning off the Control->Prefs->Open Screen option from the menus.

@{"Control Window" Link "ControlWindow.gif/main"}

The Control Window also operates as an icon dock. Zeus programs can be 
iconified onto this window, and the built-in Dock program, named ZeusDock, 
may place icons on this window. These icons may launch programs when 
pressed (see section 2.2.7 Dock, further on).

The Line Watcher, named Voyeur, will also be loaded. This program monitors 
the status of all the lines on the BBS and provides shortcuts to several 
regularly used node functions. If you wish Voyeur to appear already 
iconified when it loads, see the next section 2.2.4 Voyeur on changing the 
command line parameters (which you alter via the Prefs/Startup program).



2.2.4 Line Watcher

The Line Watcher is called Zeus:Utils/Voyeur. It is loaded as part as the 
default program startup and creates a window which looks like this:

@{"Voyeur Window" Link "Voyeur.gif/main"}

The larger window contains a list of lines, providing information about the 
callers currently connected to the BBS. The smaller window has a dock of 
six icons which perform important line functions. You may select a line by 
clicking in the list box or typing the line number on the keyboard. Once a 
line is selected, clicking on one of the icons will perform a function on 
that line.

Icon Description
 Keboard Shortcut
 Description

Jack in the Box
 P
 Pops up the current watcher for this line so you can see exactly what the 
user is doing. If there is no watcher currently loaded then it will execute 
the script: Zeus:Rexx/Voyeur/PopUpWatcher.rexx. You should edit this script 
if you wish to use an alternative to the standard ANSI watcher, named 
Zeus:Bin/Watchers/ANSI.

Boot
 B
 Kicks the user off the BBS.

Confused User
 D
 Executes Zeus:Rexx/Voyeur/UserDetails.rexx with the current line and 
public screen Voyeur is open on as arguments. This script comes 
preconfigured to load the Utils/UserDetails program.

Surgeon
 E
 Executes Zeus:Rexx/Voyeur/EditUser.rexx with the current line number and 
public screen Voyeur is open on as arguments. This script comes 
preconfigured to load the Utils/UserEditor program.

Radio Pylons
 S
 Pops up a requester to send a "node message" to the user online. Messages 
will be sent verbatim to the user's terminal and will not say who they have 
been sent from.

Iconify
 I
 Iconifies Voyeur on the Control window.


Start Up Options

Voyeur also has command line arguments you may provide it when not starting 
the program from Workbench, such as from the Zeus Startup of Shell.

@rem <PRE>
Zeus Bulletin Board System (c)1992-1999 Zeus Developments
Zeus Line Snooper - Written by Nick Loman.

Usage: Voyeur XPOS/N,YPOS/N,TOOLXPOS/N,TOOLYPOS/N,ICONIFY/S,USAGE/S

   XPOS      - X position of line list window
   YPOS      - Y position of line list window
   TOOLXPOS  - X position of toolbar window
   TOOLYPOS  - Y position of toolbar window
   ICONIFY   - Start Voyeur in Iconify mode
   USAGE     - Display this usage text
@rem </PRE>

For example, to start Voyeur iconified on the Control dock, specify 
"Iconify" as one of the arguments to the program.


The Control Window may also be run on a screen and configured in many 
different ways:

@{"Default Zeus Control Window configuration" Link "VoyeurDefault.gif/main"}
Default Zeus Control Window configuration

@{"A reconfigured Control Window" Link "VoyeurRecon.gif/main"}
A reconfigured Control Window





2.2.5 The MenuEditor

NB: The MenuEditor is not for creating menus for the BBS proper, but merely 
for configuring the SysOp's interface. For more information on configuring 
the BBS see the chapter on Editing your BBS.

The menu bars of the Control window/screen may be configured to launch your 
favourite programs or Zeus utilities. In addition, other Zeus programs also 
allow you to edit their menus.

 These programs are:

@rem <PRE>
  Zeus:Utils/Control
  Zeus:Utils/FileEditor
  Zeus:Utils/UserEditor
  Zeus:Bin/ZMailer
  Zeus:Bin/Watchers/ANSI and Zeus:Bin/Watchers/RIP
@rem </PRE>

Each program stores it's menu file in a different location within 
Zeus:Config/. Shortcuts to let you edit these menus have been created on 
the Control Screen, under the menu Miscellaneous->Config:

Program
 Menu Option
 File in Zeus:Config/

Control Screen
 Miscellaneous->Config->Control Menus
 Control/Menus.Dat

FileEditor
 Miscellaneous->Config->FileEditor Menus
 File/FileEditorMenus.Dat

UserEditor
 Miscellaneous->Config->UserEditor Menus
 Zeus/UserEditorMenus.Dat

Mailer
 Miscellaneous->Config->Mailer Menus
 Msg/MailerMenu.Dat

Watchers
 Miscellaneous->Config->Watcher Menus
 Watchers/Menus.Dat

@{"Menu Editor Picture" Link "MenuEditor.gif/main"}

Many Zeus programs which allow you to edit their menus which have an item 
in the program defined section of their menus called "Edit Menus..." which 
will allow you to call the Menu Editor directly from that program. To then 
active menu changes, select "Reload Menus" in the specific program.


Let's add a new entry to Control shut down Zeus.

Hit the "Add" button under the "Menus" listview. Change (New) to "Misc".
Hit "Add" under the "Items" menu. Change (New) to "Quit Zeus". Change the 
following entries:

@rem <PRE>
   CommKey: X
      Type: Execute
     Stack: 4096
Executable: Newshell FROM Zeus:Shortcuts/BBS/ShutdownZeus
@rem </PRE>

Hit the close window. Select "Save". Now, from the "Control" menu on the 
Control Screen, select "Reload Menus" from the Prefs items.

Your "Quit Zeus" entry will now be listed on the menus.



2.2.6 Configuring the Control Window

The Control Window can also serve as a public screen. From the "Prefs" item 
in the "Control", select "Open Screen". This will open up a public screen, 
using the same properties as your Workbench screen.

All prefs and utility programs will open on the Zeus Screen, which is named 
"ZEUS" if it is active.

Various other properties of the public screen can be controlled from the 
"Prefs" menu:

Screen Mode
 - Brings up a screen-mode requester. All aspects of the screen mode can be 
selected from here.

Font
 - Brings up a font requester. The default screen font can be selected 
here.

Palette
 - Brings up a palette requester.

Backdrop
 - Brings up a file requester- you can choose an IFF file to be tiled on 
the Control Window. We have supplied a nice marble pattern in 
"Zeus:Config/MarbleDark" from the MagicWB archive.

Default Screen
 - if this is on all windows will open on this screen instead of the 
Workbench screen.

Pop Screen
 - Zeus screen will be brought to the front whenever a window opens on it. 
Note, if you specify "BACKGROUND" in the programs Icon ToolTypes or 
Arguments, and select this option (or select it in any other program as 
it's actually a system option in AmigaOS) then Control will pop to front 
when its own Control window opens. Therefore, this option is not compatible 
with opening Control as the back-most screen.

Borderless
 - Removes the window border providing a more attractive look.

The "Save Config" option will write out these configuration changes to 
disk.


Start Up Options

Control also has options available when loading it, specified either 
through the program's icon ToolTypes or via command line arguments:

ICONIFY     -- Open Control iconified on Workbench.
BACKGROUND  -- Open the Control screen behind all other screens.
BG          -- As BACKGROUND.
NODATATYPES -- On AmigaOS 3.0 and higher: Control will only load IFF images 
which are not colour-remapped.  This is faster than using datatypes, but 
less featured.



2.2.7 Dock

The Dock utility adds icons to the Control Screen which, upon clicking will 
execute a program. The Dock is configured using the Prefs/DockCfgprogram:

@{"Dock Picture" Link "Dock.gif/main"}

Each item in the listview represents an icon stored within the directory 
Zeus:Config/Icons. All icon files end in .info but this extension is 
omitted in the list. For each item, various items of data may be 
configured.

Execute
 The command to execute upon clicking the icon.

Text
 - The text to display underneath the icon. This field may be left blank.

Stack
 - The stack size to start the program with. This setting should be 4096 by 
default or higher if need be.

Priority
 - The priority to start the program at. Programs requiring little CPU time 
should be placed at a high priority (0-10), programs which drain the CPU 
resources should be placed at a lower priority (-10-0).

Floating
 - The icon will be made "floating", and will find the next suitable place 
to locate itself. Setting this flag on will ghost the X Co and Y Co 
gadgets.

X Co
 - Specifies the X position on the Control screen to place the icon.

Y Co
 - Specifies the Y position on the Control screen to place the icon.

Find Pos
 - Pops up a bar which you may drag around the screen to find the exact 
position you wish the icon to be placed. The "X Co" and "Y Co" gadgets will 
be updated as it is moved.



2.2.8 Logging into the BBS

Logging into the BBS can be acheived locally from "Line 0". Clicking on the 
"Line 0" icon from the Control Window will deiconify the watcher. Each 
ANSI/RIP watcher is a public screen, named "ANSI.<Line Number>" or 
"RIP.<Line Number>"

The Zeus ANSI Watcher will display various information on the screen:

@{"Watcher Picture" Link "Watcher.gif/main"}

This screen is displayed when Zeus is waiting for a call. Hitting [RETURN] 
will login to the system.

Before you logon, you may wish to do some configuration. The ANSI watcher 
will clone the screen resolution from the Workbench screen, but this is 
often not the best resolution in order to get optimum speed and memory 
efficiency. To change the resolution hit the "Screen Mode" button. We 
recommend the following settings:

@rem <PRE>
 Europe or PAL Standard Countries:

 PAL:High Res   Width: 640 Height: 256
 Colours:16
@rem </PRE>

or

@rem <PRE>
 North America or NTSC Standard Countries:

 NTSC:High Res  Width: 640 Height: 200
 Colours:16
@rem </PRE>

These screen resolutions are more suited to BBS usage. However, any screen 
resolution may be used with Zeus.

You will probably also wish to select an IBM-style font. Press "Font" and 
from the list select IBM.font size 8. Any size font may be used with Zeus, 
but 8-point fonts are the most common for BBS usage.

The Palette is set up for standard Workbench colours on the last callers 
screen, and the 16-colour ANSI palette while watching. Hitting the 
"Palette" button will allow you to change both these palettes.

Press the "Keys" button. This will bring up the macro window. From here, 
you can configure Function Key commands to be executed and text to be 
pushed onto the input in the current BBS session. Currently set up keys 
are:

F1
 - Start Chat with User

F7
 - Edit Personal Attributes of User

F8
 - Edit Account of User

F9
 - Edit File Accesses for User

F10
 - Edit Message Accesses for User

S1
 - (Shift-F1) Log user in LOGS Logger.<LineNumber>

S10
 - Boot user offline.



Let's add a new entry. In the F2 string gadget type your name followed by 
"\r\n". Then change the "Exec" cyclebox next to the string gadget to 
"Text". This will make this entry a text output string.

Close the window using the close window gadget.

Again, selecting "Save Config" from the Watcher menu will cause the 
configuration changes to be output to disk.

Hit [RETURN] to log in.

You will be prompted for your name. Hit F2 to output your name. Login as a 
new user, and complete the new user registration.


2.2.9 Upgrading the online user's account

Hit F8 to load the UserEditor and bring up your account window. Click the 
"Sel" button next the the "Access" gadget to bring up a list of access 
levels. Double click on "255- SysOp" entry to give yourself SysOp access on 
the BBS.Close this window, and select "Save User" from the main menu. Hit 
Quit to close the UserEditor, or ESC.

NB: The ESC key works as a substitute for the close window gadget on all 
GUI preference and utility programs supplied with Zeus.


2.2.10 Using the BBS

You are now logged into the example BBS setup supplied with Zeus. Wander 
around the BBS and try the menu items. To logout use the 'L' option from 
the main menu. This setup is based on an actual live BBS. You will probably 
want your BBS to look different to this. We will discuss configuring the 
look and feel of your BBS in the chapter Editing your BBS.


You have now completed the initial installation of your BBS, and created an 
account for yourself. You'll now need to configure some "Lines" for your 
BBS which is the topic of Chapter 3:

@rem <PRE>
 @{" 3 - Lines                             " Link "Lines" }
@rem </PRE>


@endnode


@node "Manual.Lines" "3 - Lines"

Chapter 3 - Configuring Your Lines


3.0.1 What are lines?

Each Zeus Bulletin Board can have one or more lines. A line is some kind of 
interface to provide access to the Bulletin Board. Zeus supports an 
unlimited number of lines, which are configured in the utility 
"Prefs/LineEditor", or the "Lines" option from the Prefs menu on the 
Control Screen. There are two types of line in Zeus:

@rem <PRE>
           Ÿ Local      This type of line has no networking capability and 
is accessible from the local computer only. It is traditionally used for 
the SysOp to logon to the Bulletin Board, although more than one local line 
may be defined.
           Ÿ Serial     A serial line is used for attaching modems, 
nullmodem cables, ISDN adapters, telnet devices and other similar AmigaDOS 
shared devices to your Zeus Bulletin Board System.
@rem </PRE>


3.1.1 Setting up lines

Lines are set up with the program "Prefs/Lines", or the Lines menu option 
from the Zeus Control Window.

@{"Lines Editor Picture" Link "LinesEditor.gif/main"}

The main Lines screen shows all the configured lines on the system. There 
are also six buttons:

Add@{uu}
 - This button will add a new line entry. A new window will open to prompt 
for the configuration details of the line.

Delete@{uu}
 - This button will delete a line entry. All the line numbers numbers below 
the new deletion will be shifted up by one.

Up@{uu}
 - Change the position of the currently selected line by one, upwards.

Down@{uu}
 - Change the position of the currently selected line by one, downwards.

Save@{uu}
 - Save the Line configuration out to disk. The Lines configuration is 
stored in the file "Zeus:Config/Zeus/Lines.Dat"

Quit@{uu}
 - Close the Line Editor.

In addition, double clicking on a serial line will allow you to edit its 
configuration settings.

Now double click on Line 1.

@{"Lines Configuration Picture" Link "LinesConfig.gif/main"}


A new window will appear with various gadgets and buttons. This window 
allows you to customise the line.

Device@{uu}
 - The Amiga serial device to drive this line. Example entries would be 
"serial.device", which is used for the Amiga's internal serial port or 
"znm.device" - Zeus' own nullmodem device.

Unit@{uu}
 - The Unit number of the device. This setting is used with multi-serial 
cards like the ASDG dual serial card. The first serial device would be 0, 
the second 1 and so on. The internal serial port on the standard Amiga is 
Unit 0.

Buffer@{uu}
 - The internal buffer Zeus should use. Higher values use more memory, but 
allow higher throughput on transfers. Faster modems should have higher 
settings. Values from 2048-65536 are commonplace, in multiples of 2k.

Baud@{uu}
 - Speed to run the serial port at. This should be a reflection of the 
speed of your modems total throughput potential. The Amiga serial device 
should not be run at speeds greater than 19,200 baud without an 
accelerator.

                Example Entries:

@rem <PRE>
                Modem                   Baud

                1200                    1200
                2400 (v22b)             2400
                2400 + MNP 5            4800
                2400 + V42b             9600
                9600 + V42b             19200
                14400 + V42b            38400
                16800 + V42b            38400
                28800 + V42b            57600
                33600 + V42b            57600
                56k/ISDN                115200
@rem </PRE>

The Amiga's built in serial port is typically okay at speeds upto 57600 
with a 68030 CPU, but 68000 machines work reliably only upto 19200.  115200 
might be reliably achieved only with 3rd party serial ports.  In any case, 
a 3rd party serial port card is recommended for best performance.

Break@{uu}
 - Break delay. This value need not be changed in most cases, a value of 
250000 should suffice.

Use DSR@{uu}
 - This option should be selected if you are using hardware handshaking 
(RTS/CTS) with a 7WIRE cable.

Run Script@{uu}
 - This option when off will stop Zeus loading up the line script for the 
device. This should be set to ON in almost every case.

Check Carrier@{uu}
 - This option specifies that Zeus should detect whether the user is 
online, and quit the session if they drop carrier. It should be set ON in 
most cases.

Rad Boogie@{uu}
 - Disable some checking to boost serial performance with the Amiga 
internal serial.device. This option may help improve modem performance at 
faster baud rates.

Float Baud@{uu}
 - Ticking this box will cause the baudrate to "float" according to the 
login baud rate. For example, a line locked at 9600 receiving a caller at 
2400 would be shift the baud ratedown to 2400 for the duration of the call. 
This switch is generally only useful for old modems.

Data Len@{uu}
 - Length of data bits. Bulletin boards typically use a value of 8 bits per 
data byte.

Handshake@{uu}
   RTS/CTS -  most efficient handshaking for modems using a locked baud 
rate. Requires a 7-wire serial cable. Use this if you are not sure.
   XON/XOFF - less efficient handshaking, works with a standard 2, 3, 7 
serial cable (RECV, SEND, GROUND).

Stop Bits@{uu}
 - Number of bits to use to indicate the end of a data byte. Bulletin 
Boards almost always use a value of 1.

Parity NONE/EVEN/ODD@{uu}
 - Bulletin boards typically use a setting of NONE.


3.1.2 Device Compatibility

There are various serial device replacements available which help speed up
serial throughput on the internal serial device. They are all available on 
the
AmiNet in the comm/misc directory.

Name
 Archive
 Notes

8n1.device
 8n1.lha
 Contains bugs which may cause crashing when using ZMailer. There are newer 
versions, such are New_8n1.lha, which should work okay normally but 
possibly still crash when using OwnDevUnit.library (see later). 8n1.device 
is often the fastest option.

BaudBandit.Device
 baudbandit1.4b.lha
 Speeds up throughput considerably.

artser.device
 ArtSer37_6.lha
 Speeds up throughput.


3.1.3 Line Access

Each line on the BBS may have a separate list of access restrictions. 
Hitting the Access button from the Device Line window will bring up the 
following window.

@{"Lines Access Picture" Link "LinesAccess.gif/main"}

This window allows you to specify ranges of times to restrict access to the 
line. There are several types of line restrictions:

Restriction@{uu}

This flag prevents logons. "Normal" allows all logons through, but baud and 
access restrictions are still applicable, "No Logons" prevents users 
getting onto the BBS, useful if you are doing important configuration. 
"Mail Only" only allows mail calls, useful for Zone Mail Hour (ZMH) 
periods. "No Answer" will prevent the phone from being answered when used 
in conjunction with ZMailer or Connector, which is ideal for running non-24 
hour BBSs.

Min Baud@{uu}
 The minimum baudrate that users may use during this period. Useful for 
restricting users with slower speed modems during busy periods.

Max Baud@{uu}
 The maximum baudrate that users may use during this period.

Min Access@{uu}
 The minimum access level a user must have to logon  This is useful for 
restricting access to a line during subscriber-only hours for example.

Max Access@{uu}
 The maximum access level a user must have to logon.

Dial Back@{uu}
 This is a special feature for those who run a BBS on a voice line, or for 
SysOps who wish to use their voice line to get access to their BBS without 
disturbing the main line.  Also known as "Ring Back", this enables a 
special mode in ZMailer or Connector (but only these) such that if the 
phone rings, it won't be answered straight away.  Instead, the calling user 
must ring once, wait more than 10 seconds but less than 20 then call again. 
 This time, Zeus will answer the phone.
 As you can see, since a normal voice caller would just call once and wait 
until the line was answered, it allows you to run a BBS on a voice line if 
your users (or yourself) remember to `dial back'.


3.1.4 Strings

This window allows you to specify the commands used by Zeus:Utils/Connector 
and Zeus:Bin/ZMailer when talking to the modem. These strings accept Modem 
Control Codes.

Modem Control Codes

@{"Lines Codes Picture" Link "LinesCodes.gif/main"}

These boxes may contain the following characters which translate to serial 
operations:

@rem <PRE>
 Character        Serial Operation

    ~             short delay
    ^             drop DTR, wait a while, raise DTR again
    |             send a carriage return character
    \             escape character, send next character as is
@rem </PRE>

You can normally find the correct strings for these boxes in your modems 
manual, although the defaults used by Zeus should be fine for all modern 
modems.

Init Cmd@{uu}
 Initialisation command for the modem.

Answer Cmd@{uu}
 Command to send when a RING result is returned from the modem.

Hangup Cmd@{uu}
 Command to hangup the modem. Usually contains '^' character.

Ring@{uu}
 String returned by the modem when a ring is detected.

Connect@{uu}
 String returned by the modem when a successful data connect has been 
established.

No Connect@{uu}
 String returned by the modem when an answer command has been sent and no 
connection has been established.



3.1.5 Connecting the lines

An interface must be provided to sit in between Zeus and the serial device. 
In the case of a modem device, the phone must be answered and then the BBS 
must be started. If you are running a front-door mailer for FidoNet 
connections, Zeus must be "spawned" if it is a human caller.

Each line may be started in a different way. There are four programs which 
handle the task of answering the phone in different circumstances:

The Connector@{uu}
 A simple phone answerer. Does not provide any FidoNet facilities.

Zeus Mailer@{uu}
 Zeus' own "front-door" mailer. Similar to the TrapDoor program it is used 
to provide BBS and FidoNet connections on a single line.

NetStart@{uu}
 A BBS spawner to allow telnet connections to the BBS.

MailerStart@{uu}
 A BBS spawner run by third-party "front-door" mailer programs such as 
TrapDoor.


3.2.1 The Connector

The Connector is a utility that simply answers the phone when it detects a 
"RING" command and loads the BBS upon the modem reporting a "CONNECT". The 
strings Connector sends to the modem are defined in Prefs/LineEditor, in 
the "Strings" window for the applicable line number.


@rem <PRE>
Usage: Utils/Connector 
LINE/A,SHOWGUI/S,QUERY/S,NOOPEN/S,OWNDEV/S,WXPOS/N,WYPOS/N.
@rem </PRE>

Connector must be called with the "LINE" argument, which indicates which 
line the program should open on. The Connector will open in "iconified" 
mode by default, and will bring up an icon on the Control Window. This icon 
is stored in Zeus:Config/Icons/Connector.info.

LINE@{uu}
 - Line number to start the Connector on.

SHOWGUI@{uu}
 - Show the Control GUI (defaults to iconified)

QUERY@{uu}
 - Report current modem status on GUI.

NOOPEN@{uu}
 - Do not open the serial device.

OWNDEV@{uu}
 - Obtain "exclusive access" on the serial device. Connector defaults to 
obtaining "shared" access, meaning other serial programs can open the 
device at the same time.

WXPOS@{uu}
 - Pixel co-ordinates of the top (X) corner to open the Connector GUIon.

WYPOS@{uu}
 - Pixel co-ordinates of the top (Y) corner to open the Connector GUI on.


Connector can also be configured via Workbench by its ToolTypes:

Workbench icon tooltypes:

LINE=<Line Number>@{uu}

SHOWGUI@{uu}

QUERY@{uu}

NOOPEN@{uu}

OWNDEV@{uu}

WXPOS=<number>@{uu}

WYPOS=<number>@{uu}

XPOS=<number>@{uu}

YPOS=<number>@{uu}

These are used in the same way as the shell arguments.


The Connector Window

@{"Connector Picture" Link "Connector.gif/main"}

The Connector GUI has the following gadgets and areas:

Init@{uu}
 Sends the initialisation command to the modem.

HangUp@{uu}
 Boots the user offline.

Iconify@{uu}
 Iconifies the window.

Quit@{uu}
 Quits The Connector.

Query@{uu}
 Turns query mode on and off. When query mode is activated, The Connector 
will ask the serial device what it's status is every second. The `lights' 
at the bottom of the window will be updated to show what is going on. The 
letters stand for:

 CD@{uu}
  Carrier detect.

 DSR@{uu}
  Data set ready.

 DTR@{uu}
  Data terminal ready.

 RTS@{uu}
  Whether RTS is high or low.

 CTS@{uu}
  Whether CTS is high or low.

 Status@{uu}
  The current status of the Connector.

Text display@{uu}
 The most recent text sent/received from the modem while in command mode 
(BBS I/O does not go through this window).


Connector ARexx

The Connector supports the following ARexx commands, which can be addressed 
at it's port "CONNECTOR.<line>", where <line> is the line number Connector 
is running on.

INIT
 Equivalent to pressing the Init button. Returns 0 if successful or 10 if 
there is a caller online.

HANGUP
 Equivalent to pressing the HangUp button. Returns 0 if successful or 10 if 
there is no caller online.

QUIT
 Quits the Connector

QUERY <ON|OFF>
 Turns Query mode on or off.

SMODEM <Command>
 Sends the given command to the modem. Returns 0 if successful or 10 if 
there is a caller online.

ONLINE
 Returns the state of the line. 0 if there is no caller or 5 if there is.

OPENDEV
 Opens the serial device. Returns 0 if successful, 5 is device was already 
open or 10 if device failed to open.

CLOSEDEV
 Closes the serial device. Returns 0 if successful or 5 if device was 
already closed.


@rem <PRE>
/* An example script to hang up the line and quit the Connector */

Options Results

Address 'CONNECTOR.1' /* Talk to the Connector */

HANGUP

QUIT
@rem </PRE>


Other example scripts using these ARexx commands can be found in the 
Zeus:Rexx/Connector/ drawer, and a fuller guide to the connectors ARexx 
features can be found in the Zeus ARexx Reference Guide, under @{"Connector" link rexx.guide/Connector}.


3.3.0 ZMailer

ZMailer is Zeus' own "front-door". As well as answering the phone when a 
call is received, it detects whether the user is a human caller or a 
FidoNet mail session. If it is a user then the BBS is spawned, otherwise 
the FidoNet mail session is initiated, the remote system performs a 
handshake and mail files are transferred.

When a user calls a Zeus BBS with the Mailer online they encounter a screen 
like this:


@rem <pre>
Welcome to Zeus BBS

You have connnected on Line 1

Press ESCAPE twice to enter the BBS
@rem </pre>


A mailer dialling in will jump straight to the handshake, after it has 
detected a special string.

Configuring ZMailer to receive calls

Configuration of ZMailer is done through the Prefs/Mailer utility, 
accessable from the Connection->Config->Mailer item from the Control menu. 
Upon loading the following window will be displayed:

@{"ZMailer Preferences Picture" Link "ZMailerPrefs.gif/main"}

On the right of the window a list of all the serial devices configured in 
the Line Configuration Editor will be displayed. For each serial device you 
have configured there is a separate Mailer configuration. To configure the 
mailer configuration for line 1 (in this example "serial.device", 0) you 
would select the "001: serial.device, 0" entry in the listview.

The five buttons in the top portion of the window control different aspects 
of ZMailer's behaviour:

Addresses@{uu}
 Defines the primary FidoNet addresses and AKA addresses used for this 
line.

Modem@{uu}
 Defines values relating to the modem configuration for dialling out and 
receiving calls as well as timeout values and delays.

Strings@{uu}
 Defines strings relating to the information the user receives when the 
Mailer is in certain states (Mail only etc.), and defines strings sent to 
the modem when answering and dialling out.

Session@{uu}
 Defines FidoNet protocols to be used when dialling out and receiving calls 
from
other FidoNet sites. Also allows various miscellaneous options to be set.

External@{uu}
 Defines programs to be run after specific events occur (fax calls, file 
requests etc.)


Addresses

@{"ZMailer Addresses Picture" Link "ZMailerAddresses.gif/main"}

This window allows you to enter your FidoNet addresses and AKA's ("Also 
Known As"). You should enter your primary address first (the entry at the 
top of the window) and all your other addresses below. Mail waiting for a 
particular address will only be received if your address is specified in 
this window.


Modem

@{"ZMailer Modem Picture" Link "ZMailerModem.gif/main"}

Dial Timeout@{uu}
 The number of seconds to wait before aborting an unconnected outgoing 
call.

Drop BBS Secs@{uu}
 The number of seconds to wait for a handshake before spawning the BBS.

Min Baud@{uu}
 If an incoming connect is lower than this baud rate, ZMailer will hang-up. 
 This can also be specified in a more configurable way using the Access 
section of the Lines configuration.

Reset Secs@{uu}
 Send the initialisation string to the modem every `x' seconds.

Serial Buff@{uu}
 ZMailer's internal serial buffer. If you get serial buffer overflows try 
increasing this value. Usually at least 16384 bytes.

Char Delay@{uu}
 Some modems can't handle data being sent to them at full speed while in 
command mode. This field sets the time (in 1/50th of a second) ZMailer 
should wait after sending each character. Set to 0 if your modem can handle 
it!

Redial Delay@{uu}
 Number of seconds delay between redials to busy systems.

Redial Retries@{uu}
 Number of redials to attempt on an outgoing call.

Ring Count@{uu}
 The number of rings to answer after when receiving a call.

Ringing Count@{uu}
 Number of rings before dropping carrier on an outgoing call.

EMSI Buff@{uu}
 Size of buffer for storing EMSI information. Default to 4096.

Long Delay@{uu}
 Sets the time (in 1/50th of a second) ZMailer will delay on a tilde (~) 
character when sending a modem command. Also sets the time that DTR will be 
droped for a carat (^) character. Set to 25.


Strings

@{"ZMailer Strings Picture" Link "ZMailerStrings.gif/main"}

The strings in the bottom half of this window may contain Modem Control 
Codes (see Section Modem Control Codes).

Banner@{uu}
 A line of text always displayed to the user when they connect. The `|' 
character is replaced by a newline.  If the first character of this text is 
a `<', then a textfile is displayed, e.g. putting `<Zeus:Text/Banner.txt' 
would display Zeus:Text/Banner.txt on connect.

BBS Message@{uu}
 Another line of text giving instructions of how to login to the BBS. The 
`<' character may be used to specify a text file.

Mail Only@{uu}
 A line of text to display when the BBS is in "mail only" mode, as set by 
the Prefs/LineEditor utility. The `<' character may be used to specify a 
text file.

BBS Chars@{uu}
 Upto twenty characters which may be used as an alternative to the ESCAPE 
key to enter the BBS. This option is useful for user's with terminals which 
do not support the ESCAPE key. A `.' is the usual setting.

Pre Dial@{uu}
 The string to send to the modem before the phone number to dial. This is 
usually the AT command to dial out (ATDT).

Fax Connect@{uu}
 The string returned by the modem when a FAX connect has been recognised. 
Specifying `FAX' would be a possible value if your modem returned `CONNET 
FAX'.

Voice Connect@{uu}
 The string returned by the modem when a VOICE connect has been recognised.

Post Dial@{uu}
 A string to output after the phone number to dial. This is usually a | 
character indicating to send a newline. The format of dialling out is `Pre 
Dial', followed by the phone number, followed by `Post Dial'.

Data Connect@{uu}
 If your modem returns a string to let you know it is receiving a data call 
rather than a FAX or VOICE one, enter that string here (usually "DATA")

Data Online@{uu}
 If ZMailer receives the string defined in Data Connect, it will send this 
command to return the modem to online mode. Usually "ATO|".


Session

The Session Window allows you to control the configuration used by ZMailer 
when connecting with other systems and transferring mail.

@{"ZMailer Session Picture" Link "ZMailerSession.gif/main"}

Allow Refusing@{uu}
 Allow remote systems to refuse mail you are offering them. ZMailer systems 
dialling out can acheive this using the Forbid and Keep options.

Beep on Connect@{uu}
 When a call is received, ZMailer will flash the screen and beep (as 
defined by the Workbench preferences).

Auto UnIconify@{uu}
 Uniconifies the ZMailer window automatically upon connection.

Use Call List@{uu}
 The record of a remote system polling for mail is stored in the "Last 
Callers list", and can be read by Last Call utilities and on the Watcher 
front-end screen.

Use Line-x.Log@{uu}
 Write out logging information to the line log rather than a dedicated 
ZMailer log.

Log Caller ID@{uu}
 Any caller ID information reported by the modem will be stored in the log.

Accounting@{uu}
 Enable traplist accounting procedures. See the traplist documentation for 
more details on how accounting works in the NODELIST.LHA file stored on 
Zeus Disk #4.

Use Watcher Scrn.@{uu}
 Pop up the ZMailer window on the applicable watcher screen for this line, 
rather than the Control screen/window.

Forbid@{uu}
 Select what type of mail/files you do not want to be sent. In an EMSI 
handshake ZMailer will ask the remote system not to send:

    NONE: (default)
    XMAIL: Compressed mail and file attaches
    FREQ: File requests
    ALL: Both of the above

Keep@{uu}
 This flag disallows the sending of files and mail to another system 
dialling.

    ALL: Do not send any files out.
    XMAIL:Do not send out compressed mail and file attaches.
    NONE: Send all files out. (default)

PickUp@{uu}
 Selects which addresses to receive for when dialling out.

    ALL: Pick up mail and files for all addresses. (default)
    PRIMARY: Pick up mail and files for the primary address only.
    NONE: Don't pick up any mail or files.

After Sesn@{uu}
 Determines when the After Session string as defined in "Strings" should be 
called:

    ALWAYS: After every call (BBS or mailer) (default)
    MAILER: After a mailer call only.
    SUCCESS: After a successful mailer call only.
    GOT DATA: After a successful mailer call where data was received.
    NEVER: Never call After Session.

Handshaking Boxes@{uu}
 Determines which handshaking options ZMailer should offer when dialling 
out or receiving calls. Defaults to all handshaking options available. 
These flags should only be changed when you are having difficulties 
connecting to other mailers.

Protocol Boxes@{uu}
 Determines which protocol options ZMailer should offer when sending and 
receiving files.Large buffer Hydra protocols are not recommended unless 
using extremely fast modems (i.e. ISDN).


Externals

The Externals window allows you to configure "External" programs to be run 
when special events occur.

@{"ZMailer Externals Picture" Link "ZMailerExternals.gif/main"}

All strings in this configuration window may contain special "control 
codes" used by ZMailer. These codes are the same as TrapDoor and GMS for 
the sake of compatability:

@{"ZMailer Codes" link "ZMailerCodes"}


After Session@{uu}
 A command to be run after a connection. See the "After Sesn." 
Configuration tag in the Session section for more information about when 
this command is run. To run ZMP in import mode on the Zeus screen the 
following command will work:

@rem <pre>
run <>CON:////ZMP/SCREENZEUS Zeus:Bin/ZMP Import Export Tick
@rem </pre>

File Request@{uu}
 A command to execute when a file request is received. See the chapter on 
File Requests for example configuration lines.

Fax Handler@{uu}
 A command to execute when a fax request is received. ZMailer determines a 
fax request from the settings in the Modem window. To spawn GPfax from Zeus 
you can use the following command:

@rem <pre>
Execute Zeus:Config/ReceiveFax
@rem </pre>

Where ReceiveFax is the following script:

@rem <pre>
Wait 1
rx "address rexx_gpfax openserial"
rx "address rexx_gpfax fconreceive"
Wait 3
rx "address rexx_gpfax initmodem"
Wait 3
rx "address rexx_gpfax closeserial"
Zeus:Bin/PostMsg FROM FaxReceiver TO "SysOp" AREA 1 TEXTFILE 
Zeus:Text/GotFax SUBJECT "You have received  a FAX!!"
Wait 5
@rem </pre>

Voice Handler@{uu}
 A command to execute when a voice request is received. ZMailer determines 
a voice request from the settings in the Modem window.

File Requests@{uu}

ZMailer supports File Requests (FREQs). File Requests allow FidoNet sites 
to download files directly from your file base without logging into the BBS 
manually. To enable FidoNet sites to access your file base you must set up 
a FREQServer. There are many of these available for the Amiga, but only one 
which supports the Zeus file catalogue directly. This is called GoldFREQ 
and can be found on the aminet as comm/fido/GFreq29Zeus.lha.

How FREQs work

Requesting a file from another FidoNet system is simple. Create a file 
called "z.n.n.p.REQ" in your outbound directory, where z.n.n.p is the zone, 
node, net and point of the destination system separated by periods. Inside 
this file you should put the names of the files to request line by line, 
e.g.

@rem </pre>
1> type OUTBOUND:2.442.107.0.REQ
FILES
GFreq28Zeus.LZX
@rem </pre>

This file would request the complete list list of 2:442/107.0 along with 
the latest version of GFreq. "FILES" is known as a magic name due to the 
name you request is not necessarily the filename you will receive, eg. 
FILES may be substituted for Filelist.lha in the remote file base.

To send this file request off you must load ZMailer and tell it to poll 
2:442/107.0. After the session handshake has been completed the files you 
have requested will be returned (during the same session), usually along 
with a netmail informing you of the results of your request.

What does the FREQserver do?

A FREQserver is the program that reads in these .REQ files after they have 
been received by the mailer and creates the list of files to be sent back 
which the mailer reads and duly sends. The FREQserver also decides whether 
the system calling has access to the files requested (files can be limited 
in a similar way to the file sub-system ratio system in some FREQservers).

Setting up a FREQserver is different for each package, but we have provided 
some common lines to place in your "File Request" Configuration string in 
the ZMailer configuration.

GoldFreq

@rem <pre>
GoldFreq %i %o %O %B "%S" %n c=<path/cfgfile>
@rem </pre>

Cloning ZMailer Line Configurations

You do not have to reconfigure each serial line if the settings are 
essentially the same. You can Copy a line configuration definition by 
selecting the line to copy, pressing the Copy button and selecting a line 
number to copy to the configuration to.

How ZMailer receives files

When ZMailer is sent a file by the other side of a connection, it will only 
download the file if it is not a duplicate of a file already in the inbound 
directory.  A duplicate is a file which has not only the same name, but the 
same file size and date.  This is to ensure that a changed or updated file 
is still downloaded.
There are options for specifically refusing certain types of files too, 
which was discussed earlier under the title of "Forbid options".


3.4.0 MailerStart

MailerStart is used by external "front-door" mailers such as Trapdoor, GMS 
and JamMail to spawn a Zeus BBS session when they detect a "human" call, as 
opposed to a mail call.

MailerStart is stored in Zeus:Bin/System/

CLI Usage:

@rem <pre>
MailerStart LINE/N/A,LOCKEDBAUD/N/A,REALBAUD/N/A,STRING/F
@rem </pre>

Information for configuring MailerStart with your preferred mailer is 
supplied here. Please refer to your mailer instructions for more 
information on setting it up to receive FidoNet calls.

JamMail

Configure JamMail for a standard BBS, and use this BBS launch command:

1. Command: zeus
2. TextFile: ""
3. Execute: Zeus:Bin/System/MailerStart $(line) $(baudlocked) $(baud)
4. PostBBS:
5. Check Error: Enabled

GMS

Where <x> is the line number to install.

Add the following to GMS:Config/Mailer.<x>/MailerSettings

@rem <pre>
BBSMode=Spawn
BBSCommand="Zeus:Bin/System/MailerStart <x> %b %B"
BBSCommandStack=20000
@rem </pre>

TrapDoor

Where <x> is the line number to install..

Add the following to your trapdoor configuration, typically stored in 
"MAIL:Trapdoor.cfg".

@rem <PRE>
BBSCOMMAND "Zeus:Bin/System/MailerStart <x> %b %B %C"

BBSINOUT ""

BBSMODE SPAWN
@rem </PRE>



3.5.0 NetStart

NetStart allows you, in conjuction with AmiTCP, Miami or another TCP/IP 
stack and TnServ (freely available on AmiNet), to have people logon to your 
Zeus system by telnet.

1) Install AmiTCP & TnServ
2) Edit your TnServ banner to something like:

@rem <PRE>
---

Welcome to this great BBS!

To access BBS, login as 'bbs' with password of 'bbs'

---
@rem </PRE>

3) Add the following line to your TnServ passwd file:

@rem <PRE>
---

bbs,bbs,1,1,(User),RAM:,Zeus:Bin/System/NetStart

---
@rem </PRE>

4) Decide how many telnet lines you wish to support and with the 
'Prefs/Lines' program, generate that many serial lines. You do not need to 
worry about any of the settings but you must set the Device: to 
fakesr.device.  NetStart takes care of the rest of the magic.

When all your TelNet lines are busy, NetStart will notify callers of this 
by sending them the file NoLineFree.txt from your configured Texts 
directory.



3.6.0 The Owndevunit.library

The owndevunit.library is a shared library which keeps track of which 
program is currently using the serial device. It allows many programs to 
use the serial.device at the same time, and "lock" it when it needs to use 
it.  Simply make the library available and it will be used.

What the library does, is temporarily put offline programs such as Connect 
or ZMailer when something else wishes to dial out.  For example, when 
calling an Internet provider (with a modem), you need to free the serial 
port for the TCP/IP stack to use.  Without Owndevunit this would mean 
quitting Connector or ZMailer; with Owndevunit the Zeus programs will (if 
no there is no current call) release the serial device for as long the 
Internet call lasts, then `retake' the serial port and continue as normal 
when the Internet call ends.

NB: This does not mean that more than one program can use the serial device 
at the same time (to make calls etc.), concurrent programs reading and 
writing to the serial port would cause unwanted problems, and is something 
Owndevunit is designed to avoid!



3.6.1 Testing your BBS using a local terminal

When running communications software there is often a need to test it out 
over a serial link to see if the data from one program is being properly 
processed by another. As a Zeus BBS operator you may want to see how your 
ANSI/RIP screens look on the terminal packages your users will be using to 
call your system, or to check your scripts concerned with IEMSI, file 
transfers, or other serial only data processes.

The usual way to create a serial link is to have two machines connected by 
modems, running over a telephone line, or a null-modem cable which plugs 
into the serial ports of both machines. Not only are these both hardware 
solutions, which can be a pain in itself to setup, they also require two 
machines which not everyone posesses.

The Zeus "Null-Modem" device is a software solution to this problem which 
combines the features of both systems while only using ONE machine. As an 
added feature, you can also run multiple null-modem connections at the same 
time!

Each unit of the device acts as if it has a modem attached which can 
respond to AT commands, answer calls and dial out, although you may wonder 
where the unit would connect to! The trick is that units are paired 
together so when a program opens unit zero, it will connect with the 
program using unit one, unit two will connect with unit three and so on as 
this illustration shows.

@{"ZNM Diagram" Link "ZNMDiagram.gif/main"}

Any communication programs that allows you to select which serial device to 
use should work. Just change them to use znm.device and select a free unit 
pair.

The 'modem' part of the device only responds to a few AT commands:

AT@{uu}
 Returns 'OK'

ATA@{uu}
 Answer an incoming call.  If the other unit is not trying to dial, NO 
CARRIER will be returned.

ATD<Number>@{uu}
 Tells the modem to try and connect with the other unit. If the other unit 
does not answer, BUSY will be returned. Anything can be supplied for 
<Number>.



3.6.2 Adding a Line

Once you have created the line definition and decided on a method of 
connecting the line, you must add an entry to the Startup configuration to 
start up a new BBSLine task automatically. To start up a new remote line #3 
with Zeus the following line should be added to your startup configuration:

@rem <PRE>
Zeus:Bin/BBSLine 3 Remote.zeus
@rem </PRE>

@endnode


@node ZMailerCodes "ZMailer Codes"

These codes can be used in all of ZMailer's "External Commands" settings.  
They include commands the same as TrapDoor, Gotcha and GMS for the sake of 
compatibility, as well as including some Zeus specific ones.

 %a - aka of the remote system (one aka per %a)
 %A - all akas of the remote system (space separated)
 %b - baudrate (between computer and modem)
 %B - baudrate (of the actual connection)
 %C - full CONNECT message from the modem
 %s - serial device name
 %u - serial device unit number
 %f - serial device flags
 %r - unique random number (timestamp)
 %l - name of logfile
 %L - number of the current line
 %Z - zone number of the other system
 %N - net number of the other system
 %F - fido node number of the other system
 %P - point number of the other system
 %n - complete FidoNet address of the other system
 %S - name of Sysop of other system
 %R - name of the ARexx port of this Mailer
 %p - name of the public screen
 %I - name of inbound directory
 %O - name of outbound directory
 %d - direction 1 if inbound call, -1 if outbound call
 %% - %

 For the FREQUEST keyword, the following sequences are allowed in addition 
to the above:

 %i - name of the .REQ file (input file)
 %o - name of the .RLO file (output file)

@endnode

@node "Manual.General" "4 - General Configuration"

Chaper 4 - General Configuration



4.0.1 General

Before attempting to set the General System Configuration of your BBS, you 
should first have read and complete Chapters 2 and 3, installation of the 
program and setting up the Lines.



4.1.1 System

The System Configuration is for the configuration of the BBS itself, and 
the main settings that Zeus uses for paths and buffers.



4.1.2 Main System

The Zeus:Prefs/System program loads up the System Configuration. This is 
accessable through the menu entry BBS->Config->System. There are nine 
buttons on the main screen:

@{"System Picture" Link "System.gif/main"}

System Settings@{uu}
 This button calls up the "System Variables" window, where general 
information about the BBS and various buffer sizes are configured.

Languages@{uu}
 This button calls up the "Languages" window, allowing you to add new 
languages to the BBS.

Paths@{uu}
 This button calls up the "Paths" window, where you may edit the locations 
Zeus searches for files in.

Menu Sets@{uu}
 The "Menu Sets" window. Allows you to maintain the different menu sets you 
might support on your BBS.

Ban List@{uu}
 The "Ban List" windows allows you to ban certain names, or name patterns 
from logging onto your BBS.

Computers@{uu}
 The "Computer List" window is a simple configuration to allow user's to 
enter what computer they are using when they logon.

Update@{uu}
 This button updates any configurations you have changed on the BBS. It 
performs the same function as "UpdateConfigs SYS", see Section Update 
Configs.



4.2.1 System Variables

@{"System Variables Picture" Link "SystemVars.gif/main"}

User index read buffer@{uu}
 This variable specifies the size of the internal buffer used to speed up 
user data access. The larger the number, the faster the seek time. The box 
next to it shows the approximate memory usage.

Carrier check interval@{uu}
 This variable specifies how often in seconds Zeus checks to see if the 
user has dropped carrier.

Inactivity timeout@{uu}
 The number of seconds user's may lay "idle" on the BBS before they are 
logged off automatically.

Days of last calls kept@{uu}
 The size of the "last calls list" in days. Higher values use more memory. 
This defines how many calls Zeus will remember for displaying on the ANSI 
screen, and to users in any Last Callers doors you have installed. Please 
note that this value is per node; if set to 14, then line 1 will remember 
the last 14 days of calls to line 1 only, line 2 will remember 14 days of 
calls to line 2, etc, and all of these are loaded into memory.

Count local calls@{uu}
 Should calls on "local nodes" be counted in the system graphs and 
statistics?

Buffered text files@{uu}
 The number of text files to be buffered. Zeus has a text buffering system 
to increase the speed of the BBS. However, for every text file buffered the 
size of the text file is used up in memory. If you are using text buffering 
it is wise to limit the number of files to buffer to around 20 to save 
memory.

 NB: When using buffered text files, if you edit a text file and it is 
currently stored in the buffer, the text file will not be updated on the 
BBS. You must either reload Zeus for the change to be noticable for a user, 
or run the command "Bin/TextPreload FLUSH" (See section 4.10.0, 
TextPreLoad).

Max text file size@{uu}
 Files above this size will not be buffered. Useful for saving memory when 
displaying text files of a large size which are not BBS menus using the 
ARexx Display command.

IEMSI Information@{uu}

This information is sent during an IEMSI handshake with a user to identify 
the system. It is also used by some scripts on the BBS to get the System 
Name and SysOp Name.

System Name@{uu}
 The name of your BBS, e.g. "Bob's BBS".

System Location@{uu}
 The location of your BBS, e.g. "Brighton, Sussex".

SysOp Name@{uu}
 Your name.



4.3.1 Languages

@{"System Languages Picture" Link "SystemLang.gif/main"}

Languages take the form of files of text stored in the Zeus:Config/Language 
directory. They are named "<language>.lang", where <language> is the name 
of the language. Every language file must contain the following tags for 
the use of Zeus: All other prompts in the default language file supplied 
with Zeus, Zeus:Config/Langauge/default.lang are configured within the 
scripts using the Prompt ARexx command and are not part of Zeus itself.

WAITRETURN@{uu}
 Sent when the return key must be pressed to continue.

WAITRETURNANIM@{uu}
 Sent when the return key must be pressed to continue, and the user has 
Animated Prompts on.  A speed can be specified for the animation using 
"\\\\s<speed>".  That is, 'double backslash' (since backslash is also a 
special character) 's' and the speed as a number.  20 gives approximately 
the normal speed, and a smaller number means less delay and faster 
animation.  Speed can be set at any point in the animation, any number of 
times, so you can have variable speed animation.  Please also see the @{"Read" link "Read"} 
ARexx command information.

YN@{uu}
 Sent when the user must press "Yes or No" to continue.

YNC@{uu}
 Sent when the user must press "Yes, No or Continuous" to continue.

DEFYES@{uu}
 Sent when the user must press "Yes or No" and the default option is Yes 
(i.e. pressing return would return yes).

DEFNO@{uu}
 Sent when the user must press "Yes or No" and the default option is Yes 
(i.e. pressing return would return no).

YESTXT@{uu}
 Text sent when the user selects "Yes" at one of the above prompts.

NOTXT@{uu}
 Text sent when the user selects "No" at one of the above prompts.

CONTTXT@{uu}
 Text sent when the user selects "Continuous" at one of the above prompts.

YESCHAR@{uu}
 One letter abbreviation for `Yes'

NOCHAR@{uu}
 One letter abbreviation for `No.

CONTCHAR@{uu}
 One letter abbreviation for `Continuous'

PRIVCHAR@{uu}
 The "secret" letter output character-for-character for each letter of a 
password.

YES@{uu}
 Displayed when boolean control codes are positive.

NO@{uu}
 Displayed when boolean control codes are negative.

ON@{uu}
 Displayed when active control codes are "on".

OFF@{uu}
 Displayed when active control codes are "off".

STRING@{uu}
 The default formatting for a control code of type "string". Should be set 
at %s in almost all cases.

NUMBER@{uu}
 The default formatting for a control code of type "number". Should be set 
at %ld (long number) in almost all cases.

TIME@{uu}
 The default formatting for a time string. Takes standard date codes, as 
documented in Chapter x.xx, Control Codes.  
*****************************************************

MORE@{uu}
 Text displayed when asking if user wishes to request more text.

MONTH1, MONTH2, ... MONTH12@{uu}
 The full name of months from January to December (1-12).

GENDER0@{uu}
 The string for Gender 0 (unknown)

GENDER1@{uu}
 The string for Gender 1 (male)

GENDER2@{uu}
 The string for Gender 2 (female)


The following are strings used in showing file lists:

FIRSTFILE@{uu}
 The header displayed when showing files in an area.

FILEFORMAT@{uu}
 The format of each file in a short file list. Control Codes %1400-%1420 
may be used here.

SCANAREAS@{uu}
 The string to output when scanning through areas. The area name currently 
being scanned can be retrieved from the %s control code.

STDINDENT@{uu}
 The default number of characters to indent the file description on 
consecutive lines.  i.e.:

@rem <pre>
Filename    123k Description line 1
                 description line 2
<---This space-->
@rem </pre>

STDFILENAME@{uu}
 The minimum number of characters used by the file name in the FileFormat 
tag.  This is effectively obsolete due to enhancements to the ControlCode 
mechanism.

@rem <pre>
Filename    123k Description line 1
                 description line 2
<---This-->
@rem </pre>


4.4.1 Banned Users

@{"System Banned Picture" Link "SystemBanned.gif/main"}

This window allows you to ban users from your BBS.  Although you can delete 
users from the user database, you cannot stop them immediately recreating 
an account. By adding their name to the ban list you have some protection 
from them being able to create a new account under the same name.

All AmigaDOS wildcards may be used in the string gadget.

Banning all user's with "SEX" in their name:@{uu}

Click the ADD button. Enter "#?SEX#?" in the string gadget.

Verifying your ban works:@{uu}

Enter "MR. SEXY" in the Test Name box. Click on "Test Current" and "Test 
String". Press Test. If you have entered the string correctly it will 
report that the User is banned.

Testing no user's are already banned with your banlist settings:@{uu}

To ensure you have not accidentally banned a user who is already using your 
BBS, click "Test All" and "Test Users". Press the "Test" button. The 
results of the check are displayed in the text box.



4.5.1 Computer Types

@{"System Computers Picture" Link "SystemComp.gif/main"}

The Computer Types window allows you to specify a list of computer types 
that the users may select when choosing what computer they own. The purpose 
of this list is simply to match the index number against a description for 
use when displaying user data.



4.6.1 Bulletins

Bulletins are just normal text files but are used for keeping users up to 
date on the latest news or door scores, etc.  The online interface to 
bulletins is such that new or updated bulletins created since a user's last 
call are marked as such when they logon so the user can immediately be told 
what's happened recently.

Zeus's database of Bulletins is kept upto date using the Zeus:Bin/BM 
(bulletin manager) program.  It can detect which files have been changed 
and update them accordingly.  The Bulletin Manager is discussed below in 
section 4.6.3.

Bulletins are modified in real time; that means that any changes you make 
via the Bulletin Editor or Manager will be seen immediately on the BBS.



4.6.3 Bulletin Editor

@{"System Bulletins Picture" Link "SystemBul.gif/main"}

The Bulletins Editor is a graphical program which allows you to edit the 
list of text files that the user may read. Each Bulletin may have various 
fields attributed to it:

Name@{uu}
 The title the user will see when listing the online bulletins. When you 
add a new bulletin using the file requester the filename will be added into 
this field - this may be changed.

Filename@{uu}
 The name of the file to display.

Key@{uu}
 A short name describing the bulletin of 9 characters or less. This "Key" 
is used for referencing the bulletin easily through ARexx, without needing 
to know the sequence of it in the list.

Created@{uu}
 The date the file was created. Pressing the adjacent "disk" icon will 
retrieve the date the file itself was last modified.

Expires@{uu}
 The date this bulletin will "expire". When a bulletin has expired it will 
be removed from the list. Clearing this string will ensure that the 
bulletin will never expire.

Low Access@{uu}
 Lowest access required to access this bulletin.

High Access@{uu}
 Highest access required to access this bulletin.

Renumber@{uu}
 Renumbers the bulletins in case one has expired.

Update@{uu}
 Updates the online configuration. Equivalent to Zeus:Bin/BM UPDATE

Save@{uu}
 Saves the configuration out to disk.



4.6.3 BulletinManager

@rem <PRE>
Usage: BM
ADD/S,REM=REMOVE/S,UPDATE/S,RNUM=RENUMBER/S,LIST/S,NUM=NUMBER/K/N,NAME/K,
KEY/K,FNAME/K,EXPIRE/S,XWEEKS/K/N,XDAYS/K/N,XMINS/K/N,ALL/S

   ADD      - Add a new bulletin
   REMOVE   - Remove bulletin
   UPDATE   - Set creation date from date stamp
   RENUMBER - Renumber all bulletins from 1 -> n
   LIST     - List all bulletins
   NUMBER   - Specify a single bulletin to
              REMOVE/UPDATE
   NAME     - Specify bulletin name for ADD
   KEY      - Specify bulletin KEY for
              ADD/REMOVE/UPDATE
   FNAME    - Specify bulletin filename for ADD
   EXPIRE   - Specify that bulletin does expire for
              ADD
   XWEEKS   - Number of weeks   till bulletin expires
              for ADD
   XDAYS    - Number of days     ""     ""       ""
               "" ""
   XMINS    - Number of minutes  ""     ""       ""
               "" ""
   ALL      - Affect all bulletins during UPDATE
@rem </PRE>

BulletinManager is a utility typically run from within the 
Zeus:Scripts/Remote.zeus script to update the bulletins after each call. 
This is necessary in case you have bulletins which are affected by the 
user's actions, for example the hi-score files created by some doors. The 
following line updates your bulletins from within an ARexx script:

@rem <PRE>
address command 'Zeus:Bin/BM UPDATE ALL'
@rem </PRE>

To add a new bulletin expiring in 2 weeks and 1 day:

@rem <PRE>
1> Zeus:Bin/BM ADD NAME "Global War HiScores" KEY "GWHIGH" FNAME    <-'
"Doors:GW2/HiScores.txt" EXPIRE XWEEKS 2 XDAYS 1
@rem </PRE>

To remove this bulletin manually:

@rem <PRE>
1> Zeus:Bin/BM REMOVE KEY "GWHIGH"
@rem </PRE>



4.7.0 Updating Configurations

Changing configurations manually by editing configuration files using a 
text editor, or simply copying new ones across will not be taken as the "on-
line" configuration automatically. You can "update" your configuration 
files in several ways:

  Shut down and reload Zeus

  Select "update online configuration" in some configuration editors

  Use Bin/UpdateConfigs



4.7.1 UpdateConfigs

Run UpdateConfigs with one or more arguments to specify the configuration 
files you wish Zeus to read in.

@rem <PRE>
Usage: UpdateConfigs
MA=MSGAREAS/S,FA=FILEAREAS/S,GM=GENERALMSG/S,ED=EDITORS/S,
XPR=XFERPROTOS/S,SIGS/S,GC=GLOBALCMDS/S,MS=MENUSETS/S,
LG=LANGUAGES/S,BAN=BANNEDUSERS/S,COMP=COMPUTERS/S,PATHS/S,
SYS=SYSTEMVARS/S,TM=TEMPLATES/S,GF=GENERALFILE/S,ARC=ARCHIVERS/S,
MAP=MAPPING/S,ALL/S

MA=MSGAREAS            Message areas
FA=FILEAREAS           File areas
GM=GENERALMSG          General message settings
ED=EDITORS             Editors
XPR=XFERPROTOS         File transfer protocols
SIGS                   Special interest groups
GC=GLOBALCMDS          Global commands
MS=MENUSETS            Menu sets
LG=LANGUAGES           Language definitions
BAN=BANNEDUSERS        Banned users
COMP=COMPUTERS         Computers
PATHS                  Paths
SYS=SYSTEMVARS         System settings
TM=TEMPLATES           Access Templates
ARC=ARCHIVERS          Archivers
GF=GENERALFILE         General file settings
MAP=MAPPING            Netmail/Internet name mapping

ALL                    ALL of the above
@rem </PRE>



4.7.2 Updating configs while users are online

You should not update the language configuration while users are on the 
BBS. Doing so may cause problems when using certain functions. For safety 
wait until they have logged off, or at least until they sitting idle on a 
menu.



4.8.0 System Graphs

These are simple graphing tools which you can use to view the general usage 
of your BBS over long periods of time.

Zeus:Utils/TimeStat shows how much time your system has been alive per hour 
(white graph) and how much of this time had users online (blue graph). The 
two averages (horizontal bars with numbers to the left) show the number of 
minutes your board has been alive (minutes per hour) and the number of 
minutes per hour users have been online. The text box at the bottom shows 
total active time and total online time.

@{"System Graphs 1 Picture" Link "SystemGraphs1.gif/main"}

@{"System Graphs 2 Picture" Link "SystemGraphs2.gif/main"}

Both statistics programs are font sensitive, scalable and can update in 
real time! The usage is:

@rem <PRE>
Zeus:Utils/CallerStat X/N,Y/N,W/N,H/N,FONT/K,FS/N,SCREEN/K,UPDATE/N

Zeus:Utils/TimeStat X/N,Y/N,W/N,H/N,FONT/K,FS/N,SCREEN/K,UPDATE/N

   X      - X position of the window
   Y      - Y position of the window
   W      - Width of the window
   H      - Height of the window
   FONT   - Name of font to use
   FS     - Font size
   SCREEN - Name of the public screen to open on (ZEUS
            by default)
   UPDATE - The stats will be updated every X number
            of seconds.
@rem </PRE>



4.9.0 Resetting the system settings

The file Zeus:Config/Zeus/SystemInfo.dat contains all the system call 
information. To reset the "number of calls" counter delete this file. The 
file Zeus:Config/LastCallers.dat stores the last callers data, delete this 
to reset the lists displayed by ANSI and Last Callers doors on the BBS.



4.10.0 TextPreLoad

As noted in 4.2.1, System Variables, Zeus will buffer Text files (the 
screens, menus and bulletins shown to users) in order to improve the 
performance of finding and displaying them.  The TextPreLoad command serves 
two functions:

1. It can flush out all buffered files, in order than changed files are 
shown as updated.

2. It can specifically buffer files ready for display.

@rem <PRE>
Zeus Bulletin Board System (c)1992-1996 Alex May & Nick Loman
Text File Pre-Loader - Written by Alex May

Usage: TextPreLoad FILES/M,FLUSH/S,ALL/S

   FILES - A list of text files to preload
   FLUSH - Flush all preloaded files
   ALL   - Recurse through directories
@rem </PRE>

To flush texts, use:

@rem <PRE>
TextPreLoad Flush
@rem </PRE>

To buffer a whole directory of files, you might use:

@rem <PRE>
TextPreLoad Files/ All
@rem </PRE>

Note that TextPreLoad automatically looks in your configured Texts 
directory - there is no need to specify this path unless the file is not in 
the Texts directory.



@endnode



@node "Manual.Users" "5 - Users"

Chaper 5 - Users



5.0.1 Users

The majority of user editing is done through Utils/UserEditor. This may be 
activated from the Users->User Editor item on the Control Window, or by 
pressing F7-F10 in an ANSI watcher.

When you are using your BBS remotely, you will need to use the online user 
editor available via the SysOp (* key) menu (only with the default supplied 
ScriptSets).



5.0.2 The UserEditor

The UserEditor main screen displays a list of all the user's on the BBS. If 
you are searching for a particular user you may jump to the first letter of 
their user name by hitting that particular key. Due to this feature, you 
should note that the underlined keyboard shortcuts for the window are 
accessed by holding shift.

@{"User Editor Picture" Link "UserEditor.gif/main"}

Button
 Description

Personal@{uu}
 Shows the selected user's personal settings.

Terminal@{uu}
 Shows the selected user's account and terminal settings.

Files@{uu}
 Shows the selected user's file account.

Messages@{uu}
 Shows the selected user's message account.

Iconify@{uu}
 Iconifies the window onto the Control Window.

Delete@{uu}
 Removes the selected user from the user database.

Save User@{uu}
 Saves changes to the user.

Add User@{uu}
 Adds a new user to the user database with standard flags and access.

Quit UED@{uu}
 Quits the UserEditor.


The bevel box in the top-right allows you to search through the user list 
using a particular filter:

Gadget
 Description

All Users@{uu}
 Shows the entire user list (default).

Access Level@{uu}
 Shows all user's with access level equal to the Input box.

Unvalidated@{uu}
 Shows all user's who have not been validated by the SysOp.

Address@{uu}
 Shows all user's who have an instance of the Input box string in their 
address, city or state.

Phone Numbers@{uu}
 Shows all user's who have an instance of the Input box string in their 
voice or data phone numbers.

Male@{uu}
 Shows all male users (ignores those with gender unknown).

Female@{uu}
 Shows all female users.

Show Name@{uu}
 Shows the user's names in the userlist.

Show Alias@{uu}
 Shows the user's aliases in the userlist.

Show Info@{uu}
 Shows some account information in the userlist.


The second bevel box controls the method in which the catalogue is searched 
and sorted:

Alphabetically@{uu}
 Sort alphabetically

Chronologically@{uu}
 Sort in relation to the date of their first call.

Forwards@{uu}
 Search forwards

Backwards@{uu}
 Search backwards


After selecting a user from the list you may edit and view four windows:



5.0.3 Personal Settings

@{"User Personal Picture" Link "UserPersonal.gif/main"}

This window shows the user data related to the user's personal data. All 
fields on this window are self-explanatory.



5.0.4 Terminal Settings

@{"User Terminal Picture" Link "UserTerminal.gif/main"}

The top gadget line is the most important part of the Terminal Settings 
window.Here you can change the access level of the user from the Template 
Configuration list.

This window shows the user data related to the user's terminal and account 
settings. The line of checkboxes on the right define the user's terminal 
settings. The line of gadgets on the left contain account information about 
the amount of time they have left, statistics and credit information. Some 
fields need some explanation:

Validated@{uu}
 Whether the SysOp has approved this user's application as a new user.

Log User@{uu}
 Dangerous option! Turning this flag off will stop the user's actions being 
logged to the line log.

No Restrict@{uu}
 The user may bypass any access or line restrictions on the node they have 
logged onto.

Protected@{uu}
 If this flag is on then the user will never be purged or expire.



5.0.5 File Access

@{"User File Access Picture" Link "UserFileAccess.gif/main"}

This window shows the file areas the user has access to, along with the 
access flags they possess. Individual access to file areas may be granted 
to users using the "Pick" button, and taken away using the "Del" button. 
File ratio limits, download and upload statistics, the user's current SIG, 
file area and transfer protocol are also displayed.



5.0.6 Message Access

@{"User Message Access Picture" Link "UserMsgAccess.gif/main"}

This window shows the message areas the user has access to, along with the 
access flags they possess. Again, individual message areas may be added to 
users using the "Pick" button, and taken away using the "Del" button. 
Message writing limits, current message SIG, current message area and 
current editor are also displayed here.



5.1.1 Utils/UserDetails

The Zeus:Utils/UserDetails program displays a window with information about 
a user.

@rem <pre>
Usage: UserDetails LINE/N,USERNAME,PUBSCREEN

 LINE      - Display information about using on Line n
 USERNAME  - Name of user to display.
 PUBSCREEN - Public screen name to open window on.
@rem </pre>

@{"User Details Picture" Link "UserDetails.gif/main"}



5.2.1 Rebuilding the User database

The Zeus:Bin/UserManager utility brings together several CLI only functions 
for processing Zeus' user base. It has been designed for both stand-alone 
and interactive use.

Functions available:

@rem <pre>
 1> UserManager REBUILD
@rem </pre>

The user file will be scanned and any users marked as deleted will 
befiltered out and their user directory and contents deleted. There is no 
way to recover deleted users after a rebuild.

@rem <pre>
 1> UserManager STRIP x [NOPROMPT]
@rem </pre>

Users who have not called for 'x' number of days will be deleted. You will 
be prompted before each user is deleted although this can be overidden with 
the NOPROMPT switch.

@rem <pre>
 1> UserManager UNDELETE [NOPROMPT]
@rem </pre>

Users who are deleted remain in the user file with their DELETED flag set 
until you perform a REBUILD. Using this function, you will be prompted as 
each deleted user is found and asked whether to restore them. By using the 
NOPROMPT switch, all deleted users will be restored without prompting you 
for confirmation.

@rem <pre>
 1> UserManager LIST pattern
@rem </pre>

All users who match the given 'pattern' (all AmigaDOS wildcards are valid) 
will be listed. The match is performed on the users name and alias.

@rem <pre>
 1> UserManager DELETE pattern [NOPROMPT]
@rem </pre>

All users who match the given 'pattern' (all AmigaDOS wildcards are valid) 
will be deleted. The match is performed on the users name and alias. You 
will be prompted for confirmation unless the NOPROMPT switch is used.

@rem <pre>
 1> UserManager DUPE
@rem </pre>

Checks for and deletes duplicate users. This is not really useful anymore 
as duplicate users should never appear. It was included to fix dupe 
problems during development.

@endnode


@node "Manual.Files" "Files"

Chapter 6 - File Subsystem

File Areas are configured using the "Prefs/FileConfig" program. Upon 
loading the main window will appear:

@{"File Config Picture" Link "FileConfig.gif/main"}


Four aspects of the file configuration can be controlled using this 
program:


6.1.0 General File Configuration

Selecting the "General" option will bring up a new window:

@{"File General Picture" Link "FileGeneral.gif/main"}


Various options of the file subsystem can be configured with this window:


Memory Usage@{uu}

Each line on the BBS uses indexing to speed up the file list searching. You 
can configure the size of these indexes. The size of indexes you use should 
be related to the amount of memory you have installed on your Amiga.


@rem <PRE>
Setting  Memory         Indexes   Usage
~~~~~~~  ~~~~~~         ~~~~~~~   ~~~~~
LOW      1 mb.or less   32        3k per index.

MEDIUM   1-2 mb.        128       13k per index.

HIGH     >2 mb.         256       26k per index.

OTHER    You may specify your own index usage settings in the string
gadget when using the "OTHER" setting.
@rem </PRE>


File Notes@{uu}

When a file is uploaded to the BBS, the file description given to the file 
is added to the AmigaDOS comment field. Due to restrictions in AmigaDOS, 
only the first 80 characters of the file description will be added. This 
option should be turned off when adding files from the CD-ROM to Zeus, as 
file notes may not be added to read-only devices.


Maximum Marks@{uu}

The maximum number of files a user may mark on the Bulletin Board. Every 
file marked takes up disk space, and a user marking '#?' on the BBS could 
fill up your hard drive very quickly if you have a few thousand files. Plus 
the time that might be required to mark all files could be considerable..


Temporary Path@{uu}

The path to copy files ready to be downloaded to. This path will only be 
used if "Copy Files to Temp" is set in the file area definition (discussed 
later).


Upload Path@{uu}

The base path to upload files to. A directory will be created within this 
path called Upload<x>/, where <x> is the line number uploading, e.g. 
specifying dh2:Uploads/ as your upload path will cause files uploaded on 
line 1 to be sent to dh2:Uploads/Upload1/ for processing. This directory 
shouldn't be present in the Zeus file area configuration.


Save@{uu}

Outputs the general file configuration to disk. The name of the general 
file configuration is "Zeus:Config/File/General.Dat" and is in binary 
format.


Quit@{uu}

Leaves the general file configuration window. If you have not saved the 
configuration you will be prompted to.



6.2.0 File Area List

File areas can be configured in the "Areas" window of the Prefs/FileConfig 
program..

@{"Files Areas Picture" Link "FileAreas.gif/main"}

This main window controls your file area list.

Add@{uu} - Add a new file area to the list. The file area list is sorted 
numerically and a new area will be inserted according to its number.

Edit@{uu} - Edit the file areas(s) currently marked. Single areas can be marked 
by double-clicking on the area you wish to edit.

Copy@{uu} - This will make duplicate copies of the currently marked file 
area(s).

Delete@{uu} - Remove the currently marked file area(s) from the file area list.

Number@{uu} - Renumber the currently marked file area(s). A requester will pop 
up allowing you to decide the base number to renumber from.

Save@{uu} - Outputs the file area list to disk.

Quit@{uu} - Leaves the area list window. If you have changed the file area 
configuration, a window will pop up asking what to do about the changes. 
"Save the configuration" saves the changes permenantly to disk, "Update 
online configuration" makes the changes immediatly available to users 
(note: if you update online, you should also save to disk since some Zeus 
programs might expect both the on disk and in memory configuration to be 
the same). "Update data files" calls the Zeus DataManager, which, if the 
file base is of fbzeus type, will change the file area indexes to reflect 
the change (otherwise, you'd need to rebuild the file indexes with 
FileManager then add the files back manually to that area).



6.2.1 The File Area Definition@{uu}

@{"File Area Definition Picture" Link "FileAreaDefinitions.iff/main"}


Selecting 'Add' or 'Edit' will bring up this window, allowing you to edit 
the attributes of each file area. Editing of the file area definitions can 
also be done through the Zeus ARexx port. See the commands @{"SetFileArea" link "SetFileArea"} and 
@{"GetFileArea" link "GetFileArea"} in the reference section of this 
manual.

Each field may also be accessed through control codes while a user is 
online.  See ControlCodes %1000 and onwards in the reference section of 
this manual.

Settings


Number@{uu} - The index number of this file area. No two file areas in the file 
area list may have the same number, and if you attempt to enter a duplicate 
number a requester will popup and make you rechoose. Please note Zeus does 
not support the use of an area 0 anywhere on the BBS.

Name@{uu} - A descriptive name for this file area. Up to 64 characters may be 
used, though for two column file area lists on the BBS to be formatted 
correctly, a maximum of 30 is recommended.

Path@{uu} - The main path for storing files belonging to this area in. This may 
be any valid AmigaDOS path of up to 104 characters long. A '/' or ':' 
character will be appended to this path if you do not supply one. E.g., 
entering dh2:aminet would be replaced with dh2:aminet/

Library@{uu} - Set a File Base library for this area. This allows programmers to 
write libraries which allow Zeus file bases to be stored in any format - 
for example, an FTP library would allow files to be stored on a remote FTP 
site, but look to be present on your BBS. File base libraries are libraries 
pre-fixed with "fb" - the default library being fbzeus.library. Select a 
new library using the file selector icon to the right - but only select 
fb#?.library's ! An incorrect one may crash File Config. If the library has 
a configuration window, "Opts" will show this.

Write access@{uu} - This cycle gadget allows the SysOp to allow or disallow 
write access to this path. CD-Rom drives for instance may not have R/W 
access as they are a read-only medium. This cycle gadget may have either a 
"R/W" or a "Read" setting.

Disk icon@{uu} - This icon will bring up a file requester allowing you to select 
the main path for this area easily.

Low@{uu} - The "override" minimum access level required to access this area. 
Template and SIG definitions take second priority to this setting. See 
section 8 for more informatioin on restricting access to users on the BBS.

High@{uu} - The "override" maximum access level required to access this area. A 
setting of 255 is common in almost every case.

Age@{uu} - The minimum age a user must be to enter this area. This setting is 
useful for restricting access to "over-18" file areaa.

Gender@{uu} - This cycle gadget allows you to prevent access to this file area 
for Male or Female members of the Bulletin Board.

U/L BPS@{uu} - This setting defines the minimum modem speed required to upload a 
file to this area. Typical settings are "1200" or "2400" to disallow the 
slower modem rates.

D/L BPS@{uu} - This setting defines the minimum modem speed required to download 
a file from this area. Some SysOps will only allow high-speed (over 9600 
bps) users to download from their file bases.


Multiple Paths@{uu} - If the files in this file area are stored over more than 
one path, for instance if you have more than one AmiNet CD and you wish to 
incorporate each directory on each CD into one directory on your BBS you 
should use the Multiple Paths option.

It is an extremely powerful part of Zeus's file management capabilities.  
Consider if you add another hard disk to your BBS.  You can create an 
identical set of directories on it as your original files drive, and set a 
multi-path for each area to each new directory.  When Zeus detects that the 
original hard disk is running out of space, it will automatically and 
invisibly start using the new hard disk to save files to.  This can be 
repeated for as many hard disks as your computer (or even network of 
computers) can hold, and by setting the Read Only option, you can 
transparently include CD-ROM directories, allowing for example to backup 
your file base to CD and still use the same areas for users to upload to.  
Further details on the use of MultiPaths can be found in @{"Chapter 15" link Manual.MultiPaths}, File 
Area MultiPaths, dedicated to the topic.

Clicking this button will bring up a new window.

@{"MultiPaths Picture" Link "MultiPaths.gif/main"}

An unlimited number of paths may be stored in this window.


String: - This entry may be changed with the Disk Icon (will bring up a 
file requester to allow you to select the path), or the string gadget 
itself in which you can enter the path directly.

RW - Read: - This specifies whether the path is Read/Write or Read only. In 
the case of a CD-ROM this should be set to "Read" for `read only', or can 
be used to stop Zeus saving files to a hard disk path, forcing files into 
another of the Multi-Path entries.

New: - Adds a new entry to the list. 

Del: - Deletes the currently selected item from the list.



Flags@{uu}


Allow Downloads@{uu} - Allow users to download files from this area. Typically 
set on.

Allow Uploads@{uu} - Allow users to upload files to this area.

View Files@{uu} - Allow users to view the files in this area.

Make Files Free@{uu} - All files in this area will default to "free access". 
This means that the user's download ratio will be unaffected when accessing 
files in this area.

DOS FileNames@{uu} - This setting truncates uploaded files to the MS-DOS 8.3 
character file format. Zeus file bases usually allow file names of up to 30 
characters which may be unacceptable to some MS-DOS machines. For instance, 
if this flag is set ON, the file "obnoxiouslylongfilename.zip" would be 
truncated to "obnoxiou.zip". Multi part files are also catered for with 
this algorithm, e.g. "obnoxious_part1.dms" and "obnoxious_part2.dms" would 
be truncated to "obnoxio1.dms" and "obnoxio2.dms".

Validation Required@{uu} - The SysOp must "hand-validate" using the 
Utils/FileEditor program each new upload to this area before users can 
download them. See section 8 for more information on allowing users to 
access unvalidated files.

All Files to Temp@{uu} - Files to be downloaded from this area will first be 
copied to the "Temporary Path" as specified in the "General File 
Configuration" window of Prefs/FileConfig. Use this when the storage the 
file is on will be too slow to send over the link the users might be using, 
or, if you have a multi-disk CD-ROM, this option will avoid any excessive 
switching between disks.



6.3.0 Transfer Protocols

The transfer protocols callers to the BBS may use to upload and download 
files may be configured by selecting "Xfers" from the main FileConfig 
window.

@{"File Protocols Picture" Link "FileProtocol.gif/main"}

Add@{uu}  - Adds a transfer protocol to the list.

Double Click@{uu} - Edits the transfer protocol you have selected.

Remove@{uu} - Removes the transfer protocol you have selected from the list.

Up@{uu} - Moves the transfer protocol up one place in the list.

Down@{uu} - Moves the transfer protocol down one place in the list.


Information about transfer protocols

Zeus comes supplied with two transfer protocols built-in. These are 
executable programs stored in "Zeus:Bin/System/" and are called "HydraXfer" 
(for Hydra bi-directional transfers) and "LocalXfer" (for simulating an 
upload from the local terminal). Other popular transfer protocols like 
"XModem", "YModem" and "ZModem" may be added using the "XPR" library 
system.

XPR libraries are standard Amiga libraries, commonly stored in LIBS:, which 
 handle the uploading and downloading of files. There are many different 
XPR protocol libraries available, but Zeus comes with the following ready 
configured.

The libraries themselves can be found on Zeus Disk #3, in the XPR.lha 
archive.

@rem <pre>
Protocol Name   Library                 Version Flags   DL Options      UL 
Options
XModem          xprymodem.library       2.20    -----   Z1 C1 B0 YS     Z1 
C1 B0 YS
XModem-1K       xprymodem.library       2.20    -----   Z1 C1 B1 YS     Z1 
C1 B1 YS
XModem-1K-G     xprymodem.library       2.20    -----   Z1 C1 B1 YG     Z1 
C1 B1 YG
XModem-CRC      xprymodem.library       2.20    -----   Z1 C1 B0 YS     Z1 
C1 B0 YS
YModem          xprymodem.library       2.20    BDU--   Z1 C1 B1 YB     Z1 
C1 B1 YB
YModem-G        xprymodem.library       2.20    BDU--   Z1 C1 B1 YG     Z1 
C1 B1 YG
ZModem          xprzmodem.library       2.0     BDU--   TN OR B16 F0 E10 AN 
DN SN RN    TN OR B16 F0 AN DN SN RN
@rem </pre>


Editing the file transfer protocol

@{"File Protocol Editing Picture" Link "FileProtocolEdit.gif/main"}

Name@{uu} - The name of the file transfer protocol as seen by the user.

Library@{uu} - Library name of XPR protocol, or the executable file to be run if 
in "Executable" mode.

D/L Options@{uu} - XPR options to be passed to the library for downloads. Refer 
to your XPR library documentation for more information about these 
settings.

U/L Options@{uu} - XPR options to be passed to the library for downloads.

Low Access@{uu} - The minimum access level required to access this protocol.

High Access@{uu} - The maximum access level required to access this protocol.

Batch files@{uu} - This protocol supports the sending or receiving of more than 
one file at once.

Auto download@{uu} - Flag to indicate whether the protocol does initiates 
transfers automatically.

Auto upload@{uu} - Flag to indicate that the protocol supports upload without 
prior entering of file name(s).

Executable@{uu} - Setting this flag to on means that the Library field is a 
program to run, rather than the name of an XPR library.

Bi-Directional@{uu} - Indicates whether the transfer supports uploading and 
downloading at the same time.



6.3.0 Adding files to the BBS

There are many ways to add files to your BBS. The easiest is using 
Zeus:Utils/FileEditor. It can also be accessed from the Files->File Editor 
entry in the Control menu.

6.3.1 The File Editor

Upon loading the File Editor the following window will be displayed:

@{"File Editor Picture" Link "FileEditor.gif/main"}

The large listview on the right doubles up as an area list and a file list. 
When the File Editor is first loaded the list of areas will appear, 
clicking on one of these will bring up a list of all the files in the 
area.Clicking on the "..parent" entry will bring you back to the area list.

The project menu from the menubar allows you to access some important 
general features of the File Editor:

About@{uu}
 Displays a small about request.

Iconify@{uu}
 Iconifies the File Editor on the Control Screen.

Edit Menus@{uu}
 Fires up the Prefs/MenuEditor program to allow you to edit the menu bar of 
the File Editor (see Section The MenuEditor).

Reload Menus@{uu}
 Reloads the menu configuration after editing with the "Edit Menus" 
function.

Quit@{uu}
 Quits the File Editor.


The second bevel box on the left contains "search controls". They allow you 
to filter the files displayed in a certain way:

Chrono@{uu}
 This mutually exclusive button allows you to choose between the files 
being displayed chronologically (i.e. by date), or alphabetically.

Forward/Backward@{uu}
 This set of buttons allows you to read the list forwards or backwards, 
e.g. if this was set on backwards in alphabetical mode the files would be 
listed from Z-A.

All/Validated/Unvalidated@{uu}
 This cycle gadget allows you to view files according to whether they have 
been validated by the SysOp, not validated yet or not to filter at all (all 
mode).

Clicking on a file in the list will display it's "file catalogue data". For 
every file which is on the BBS the file catalogue stores information about 
it:

@{"File Catalogue Picture" Link "FileList.gif/main"}

Number@{uu} - A unique number identifying the file in the file base. No two 
files may have the same number, and the number may not be changed.

Name@{uu} - The name of the file. Zeus does not allow file names to contain more 
than 31 characters, and the first character of the file may not be a 
character other than a letter or a number.

Downloads@{uu} - The number of times this file has been downloaded on the BBS.

Size@{uu} - The size of the file in bytes.

Uploaded By@{uu} - The name of the user who uploaded this file.

Upload Date@{uu} - The date this file was uploaded in the format "dd-mmm-yy hh 
mm ss"

Last Dl@{uu} - The last date this file was downloaded in the format "dd-mmm-yy 
hh mm ss".

Offline@{uu} - Is the physical file offline? This flag is not updated 
automatically, use the FileManager program in "OFFLINE" mode to set all the 
flags (see section on FileManager later).

Free@{uu} - Will this file debit the user's downloaded file counters?

Validated/ Unvalidated@{uu} - Has the SysOp decided that this file is fit to be 
on the BBS?


If you wish to edit any of this data you must press the Update Filebutton 
after you have made the appropriate changes.

Once you have selected a file area the file menu becomes available. From 
this menu you can manipulate the file base in various different ways:

Add File@{uu} - Allows you to add a file from your hard drive to the file base. 
This option firstly opens a file requester to let you select the file, the 
current directory will be the main path from the area definition. Then you 
may add a short description of upto 256 characters. To add a larger 
description later you should select the file and ue the Edit Description 
option from this menu.

Bulk Upload@{uu} - Allows you to add more than one file at the same time using a 
multi-select file requester, and lets you retrieve the file description 
either from the AmigaDOS file comment or by entering a global description 
for all files.

Delete File@{uu} - Deletes all the selected files in the current area. You will 
be prompted to decide whether you wish to delete the physical files as well 
as the file catalogue entries.

Move File@{uu} - Allows you to move all the selected files from one area to 
another, retaining their description and data. After selecting this option 
you should choose the area to move them by selecting another area.

Edit Description@{uu} - Loads your local editor up and lets you change the 
description of a file. To configure your local editor you can use the 
option Configure Editor from the Prefs menu within File Editor.

Bulk Validate@{uu} - Validates all unvalidated files within the area.

Unlisted Files@{uu} - Shows all the files within the search paths of this file 
area which have notbeen added to the file catalogue. When you are in 
unlisted files mode you can automatically bulk upload the entire list by 
selecting the Bulk Upload menu option.

Find File@{uu} - Prompts you for a name and jumps directly to that file. 
AmigaDOS wildcards are not used, the name must be exact.

Select All@{uu} - Marks every file in the current area.

Deselect All@{uu} - Unmarks every file in the current area.

Next Suitable Area@{uu} - Jumps to the next area with "suitable" files in. If 
you have selected a search mask such as Unvalidated files only then it will 
skip file areas with only validated files in.

Previous Suitable Area@{uu} - Jumps to the previous area with "suitable" files 
in.


The "Scripts" menu is entirely configurable through the MenuEditor program. 
It comes with three menu items pre configured:

Rebuild Catalogues@{uu} - A hotlink to the RebuildCatalogues shortcut (see 
Section Shortcuts)

Renumber Catalogues@{uu} - A hotlink to the RenumberCatalogues shortcut.

Extract FILE_ID.DIZ@{uu} - This script uses the FileEditor ARexx port to extract 
the FILE_ID.DIZ files from the archives marked and add it in as the new 
description. The script called is "Zeus:Rexx/FileEditor/ExtractDIZ.zeus" 
and uses the Archivers Configuration to extract the files fom the archive 
correctly.


Adding AmiNet CD's to the file catalogue

The Bin/ZAmiNet utility has been provided to allow you to upload files from 
your AmiNet CD's to the BBS. In order to upload files from the "FILES.BBS" 
or "INDEX" description files you must have already created the areas on 
your BBS. This can be acheived very easily using the 
Zeus:Rexx/FileAreas/AddCDAreas.rexx script.

Adding Areas

Create a list of all the areas you wish to upload from the AmiNet CD to a 
file,
each area separated by a new line and call it "Areas.txt", e.g.:

@rem <PRE>
1> type Areas.txt

comm/bbs
comm/dlg
comm/tcp
dev/c
dev/e
util/misc
@rem </PRE>

Run the following command, where "CD0:Aminet/" is the first part of the 
path
used to locate files in this directory. For example, for "comm/bbs" we 
would
search fo the files in "CD0:Aminet/comm/bbs". "200" is the number you wish 
to
start numbering the areas from. In this example "comm/bbs" will be area 
200,
"comm/dlg" area 201 etc.

@rem <PRE>
1> rx Zeus:Rexx/FileAreas/AddAreas Areas.txt CD0:AmiNet/ 200
@rem </PRE>


Adding Files from the CD

To upload the files from these areas we would run Bin/ZAmiNet in the 
following mode:

@rem <PRE>
Zeus:Bin/ZAminet AREAS 200-205 INDEX
@rem </PRE>

This specifies that we wish to upload areas 200-205 (this is a "range" 
argument), and we wish to retrieve the file descriptions from the "INDEX" 
file located in the path, CD0:Aminet/comm/bbs/INDEX for example.

If your AmiNet CD does not have the INDEX format but instead uses the 
FILES.BBS format then the following line could be used:

@rem <PRE>
Zeus:Bin/ZAminet AREAS 200-205 INDEX
@rem </PRE>


Adding a large number of files.

The Bin/FileManager utility is provided to make uploading many files to the 
file catalogue easy. Running in "ADDNEW ALL" will cause it to scan all the 
file areas searching for files that are in the area's physical path(s) but 
not in the file catalogue. Once it has found one it will ask you if you 
wish to upload it to the file catalogue, and prompt for a description. If 
you wish to add the files using the description provided in the AmigaDOS 
file comment then add the argument "FILENOTE".

To upload all new files in <AreaNumber>:

@rem <PRE>
Bin/FileManager ADDNEW AREAS <AreaNumber>
@rem </PRE>

To upload all new files on the BBS using the FileNote description:

@rem <PRE>
Bin/FileManager ADDNEW ALL FILENOTE
@rem </PRE>


Tidying up your file base

If you have file catalogue entries with no matching physical file due to a
partial file loss then you can delete all the file catalogue entries using 
the
FileManager with the "EXISTS" argument. This will search for the matching
physical file in the area's path(s) for each file catalogue entry it finds 
and
prompt you if a mismatch is found. You can opt to delete all files using 
the `a'
key when the first mismatch is found.

To delete all non-present file catalogue entries:

@rem <PRE>
Bin/FileManager EXISTS ALL
@rem </PRE>

To delete all non-present file catalogue entries in a particular area

@rem <PRE>
Bin/FileManager EXISTS AREAS <Area>
@rem </PRE>


FileManager in General

FileManager has a number of uses to which it can be put to use, 
particularly when run automatically from a Cron or script.

@rem <PRE>
Zeus Bulletin Board System (c)1992-1999 Zeus Developments
CLI File Catalogue Manager - written by Nick Loman/Neil Williams

Usage: FileManager 
EXISTS/S,ADDNEW/S,OFFLINE/S,SEARCH/K,UPDATE/S,ALL/S,AREAS/K,FILENOTE/S,NOQUERY/S

      EXISTS   -- Delete files which do not physically exist.
      ADDNEW   -- Add new files in path to catalogue.
      OFFLINE  -- Make files on/offline on existance.
      SEARCH   -- Find a file matching an AmigaDOS wildcard.
      UPDATE   -- Update's file size and file note for files.

      ALL      -- Process all file areas.
      AREAS    -- Process specific areas (ranges acceptable).

      FILENOTE -- Use FileNote from file, do not prompt with ADDNEW.
      NOQUERY  -- Always delete when using EXISTS
@rem </PRE>


"Exists" and "AddNew" have already been discussed above.

Offline@{uu}

The "Offline" option checks the path(s) of file areas for files which are 
not currently visible to Zeus. If they are not found, they are marked in 
the file catalogue as "offline", and are temporarily unavailable for 
download to users.

Offline is generally used when you have CD-ROMs in your file base.  When 
you remove or insert a CD, run FileControl over the appropriate areas (or 
ALL) and Zeus will update the offline flags.  MultiPaths are, of course, 
perfectly valid - the files marked offline can be mixed in an area with 
other files which are not marked offline at that time.

Search@{uu}

Search is a special option.  It allows you to give an AmigaDOS wildcard for 
a file name or file description which is searched for in the area range (or 
ALL) you define. Any matching files are shown in the shell together with 
the area they are in.

For example:

@rem <PRE>
FileControl Search #?GoldFreq#? Areas 20-60
@rem </PRE>

will search areas 20 to 60 for files mentioning GoldFreq.

Since the output is sent the Shell (via "stdio"), you can redirect it:

@rem <PRE>
FileControl Search #?GoldFreq#? Areas 20-60 >T:Search.txt
@rem </PRE>

will write the search results to the file T:Search.txt.

Update@{uu}

In the general usage of your file base it may happen that file sizes and 
file comments no longer match what Zeus thinks they are and should be.  For 
example, if your online `file lists' (lists of files on your BBS for users 
to download) are merely copied directly over the old versions, the sizes 
may be wrong and the file note will be blank.

Running "FileManager Update" causes Zeus to check each file in turn: if the 
real file size has changed, Zeus will update the file catalogue to that.  
If the file's comment does not match the file description known to Zeus, 
the file comment will be set.



@endnode



@node "Manual.Messages" "7 - Message Sub-system"

Chapter 7 - The Message Sub-system@{uu}

The Message Subsystem is based around the Prefs/MsgConfig configuration 
utility. Here you can set up all your message areas in which local, 
FidoNet, Internet E-Mail and UseNet messages may be saved.

Loading up Prefs/MsgConfig (Messages->Config->Areas Configuration from the 
Control Window will bring up the following window:

@{"Message Config Picture" Link "MsgConfig.gif/main"}


Hitting General will bring up the:

General Message Configuration

@{"General Message Config Picture" Link "MsgGeneral.gif/main"}

This window lets you configure the colours for the message texts. Each 
value may be between 0-15, 0-7 are low intensity colours, 8-15 are high 
intensity colours. A description of the colour will popup on the left after 
entering a value.


Message Area List

@{"Message Areas Picture" Link "MsgAreas.gif/main"}

Add@{uu} - Add a new message area to the list. The message area list is sorted 
numerically and a new area will be inserted according to its number.

Edit@{uu} - Edit the message areas(s) currently marked. Single areas can be 
marked by double-clicking on the area you wish to edit.

Copy@{uu} - This will make duplicate copies of the currently marked message 
area(s).

Delete@{uu} - Remove the currently marked message area(s) from the file area 
list.

Number@{uu} - Renumber the currently marked message area(s). A requester will 
pop up allowing you to decide the base number to renumber from.

Save@{uu} - Outputs the file area list to disk.

Quit@{uu} - Leaves the area list window. If you have changed the file area 
configuration, a window will pop up asking what to do about the changes. 
"Save the configuration" saves the changes permenantly to disk, "Update 
online configuration" makes the changes immediatly available to users 
(note: if you update online, you should also save to disk since some Zeus 
programs might expect both the on disk and in memory configuration to be 
the same). "Update data files" calls the Zeus DataManager, which, if the 
message base is of mbzeus type, will rename the message area indexes to 
reflect the change (otherwise, you'd need to manually rename MsgDirectory-
>#?.<oldnumber> to MsgDirectory->#?.<newnumber>).


Message Area Configuration

@{"Message Area Picture" Link "MsgArea.gif/main"}

Number@{uu} - The unique number of this message area. `0' is not a valid entry.

Area Name@{uu} - The descriptive name of this area.

TagName@{uu} - The FidoNet tagname of this area. Not applicable for Local Bases.

Origin@{uu} - Pops up the "origin window". Not applicable for Local Bases.

Executable@{uu} - Pops up the "executable window".

Library@{uu} - Pops up the "message base library window"

Address@{uu} - Pops up the "FidoNet address window". Not applicable for Local 
Bases.

Low Access@{uu} - The minimum access level required to access area.

High Access@{uu} - The maximum access level required to access area.

Age@{uu} - The minimum age the user must be to access the area.

Gender@{uu} - The gender the user must be to access area.

Max Msgs@{uu} - The maximum number of messages that will be kept in the area 
after the trim function has been called.

Private Base@{uu} - Messages in this base are made private by default?

Hi-Ascii@{uu} - This base supports "hi-ascii" (8 bit) characters.

Quoting@{uu} - This base allows user's to "quote" messages back when writing 
replies.

Aliases@{uu} - This base allows the use of aliases in the From: field of 
messages. Not permitted in most FidoNet bases.

Del Own Msgs@{uu} - User's may delete messages they have written in this base.

Show Kludges@{uu} - Show FidoNet kludge lines when displaying messages.

Show Seen-Bys@{uu} - Show FidoNet SEEN-BY lines when displaying messages.

Show Paths@{uu} - Show FidoNet PATH lines when displaying messages.

Moderator@{uu} - Message base has moderator status.

Text Styles@{uu} - "Text styles" are displayed in this area.  These are the 
*bold*, /italic/ emphasis markers that may be used in FidoNet.

ANSI Base@{uu} - This flag is an ANSI base and messages are sent verbatim.

Basetype@{uu}

Local: Area is not networked.

Echomail: Area is a FidoNet style area.

Netmail: Area is a FidoNet Netmail area.

UUCP: Area is a UseNet base.

Email: Area is an Internet e-mail base.

Bad: Area is for bad messages.

Origins

@{"Message Origins Picture" Link "MsgOrigins.gif/main"}

This window allows you to add Origin lines and assign an origin line to the 
message area. You can also opt to assign a "random" origin line from the 
list to messages written. The Origin configuration is stored in 
Zeus:Config/Msg/Origins.Txt and is in text format - origins stored one per 
line.

Origins should not be greater than 65 characters long or they may cause the 
message length to overflow.

Text Processing

@{"Message Processing Picture" Link "MsgProcessing.gif/main"}

This window allows you to assign "text processors" to the message area 
currently being edited. From here you may also add and delete text 
processors.


How to add a Text Processor

This can be a script or a program which is run after you write a message on 
the BBS, which modifies the text to be written in some way.

There are various programs which could be used. The most common ones are 
quote-generators or text translators which serve simply to add a touch of 
humour into your message bases (Gibber, Kraut, ValSpeak, Jive).

There is also potential for a spell-check program, a message text analyser, 
or a program in the same vain.


How to use Text Processing

The string gadget in the "Select Text Processor..." window is for the input 
of a
line to be executed when a message is written in the area. The code `%s' is
replaced by the name of the file containing the text.


Text Translators

Some text translators are included with Zeus, and are stored in the 
Zeus:Bin/ directory.

To use any of these translators, we have to use some simple scripts.

To use Jive, the Exe field should look like this:

"C:Execute Zeus:Bin/Jive.Scr %s"

This requires us to have the script, Jive.Scr in the Zeus:Bin/ directory. 
The included one looks like this:

@rem <pre>
.key filename
.bra {
.ket }

Copy {filename} t:TempJive
Delete {filename}
Zeus:Bin/Jive <t:TempJive >{filename}
Delete t:TempJive
@rem </pre>


Message Base Libraries

Up until this point all the message areas we have covered have had used the 
mbzeus.library as their "Message Base Library". A "Message Base Library" is 
a collection of routines that handle message database functions at a low 
level, e.g. Loading, saving, deleting and scanning messages. Zeus' own 
Message Base Library is called mbzeus.library and is a particularly 
optimised and fast standard of it's own. It stores all the messages in a 
message area in two files, once for the header and one for the text. This 
provides fast access speeds and minimum disk fragmenting with high usage.

The only disadvantage with Zeus' own message format is that it is only 
compatible with utilities that suppport it, such as Zeus' own FidoNet mail 
processor ZMP.

Zeus is able to interface with other message base formats through the 
"Message Base Library" system. Message areas assigned to a message base 
library other than mbzeus.library work in exactly the same way from the 
point of view of the user, but are stored physically in a quite different 
format.

Currently there are only two message base libraries available for Zeus.

 - mbzeus.library
 - mbstarmsg.library

In general, the mbzeus.library should be sufficient for your needs. 
However, if you wish to use FidoNet utilities relying on the FTS-1 format 
message base format then you must use the "mbstarmsg.library". This is also 
known by the name "Plain" in the MailManager software.


mbstarmsg.library

The mbstarmsg.library stores it's data files in quite a different way to 
the mbzeus.library. For every single message in an area a separate message 
called "x.MSG" is created in the directory "MAILDIR:<tagname>/", where 
x.msg is the number of the message, MAILDIR: is your mail directory (as set 
up in Prefs/System->Paths) and <tagname> is the Tagname of the area (as set 
up in the Message Configuration).

A physical set-up using mbstarmsg.library may look like this:

@rem <PRE>
 MAIL:
      StarMsg.idx
      AMIGA/
            20.msg
            21.msg
            22.msg
            23.msg
            24.msg
            25.msg
      ZEUS_BETA/
            1.msg
            2.msg
            3.msg
@rem </PRE>

The "StarMsg.idx" file keeps a record of the "Message Pointers" (see 
Section Message Pointers).


Using mbstarmsg.library with other Mail Processors@{uu}

If you are not using ZMP on your message bases, or will be using other 
utilities which will create/delete messages in your mbstarmsg.library you 
need to inform the mbstarmsg.library that changes have been made for them 
to show up on the BBS. The Bin/mbManager utility plays a part in this (see 
Mainting your Message Base below for detailed information about mbManager).

You should set up an automatic event using a CRON to run mbManager in 
"UPDATE ALL" mode will detect new messages written on the BBS and change 
the pointers as appropriate. In addition to the message pointers being 
incorrect, using other mail processors will result in no Waiting Mail files 
being created for each user to inform them that new messages have arrived 
for them. To create Waiting Mail files you should run mbManager in 
"WAITINGMAIL ALL" mode. Areas which do not use the mbstarmsg.library will 
be ignored in this mode.


Maintaining your Message Base

The most common maintenance function to be performed upon the message base 
is to trim it down when more messages than are desired have been imported. 
The Message Area Configuration tag "Max Msgs" defines the number of 
messages that should be kept. In order that no messages are lost when the 
base is not read often and more than 200 messages have been imported the 
base will keep expanding until you "trim" the base.

Trimming your Message Base@{uu}

The "trim" function is accessed through the Bin/mbManager program.

@rem <PRE>
Usage: mbManager

UP=UPDATE/S,RS=RESCAN/S,M=MAINT/S,T=TRIM/S,INITIALISE/S,WM=WAITINGMAIL/S,AREA/N/K,RANGE/K,ALL/S

   UPDATE      Check High and Low pointers are correct
   RESCAN      Scan area for High and Low message
               pointers

   MAINT       Perform a health check on message
               area(s)

   TRIM        Trims message area(s) till it contains
               'MaxMsgs'

   INITIALISE  Delete all messages and reset message
               pointers

   WAITINGMAIL Create waiting mail data information
               from new messages

   AREA        Specify one area to act upon

   RANGE       Specify a range of areas to act upon

   ALL         Act upon all message areas
@rem </pre>

Running the following command line will "trim" all your bases:

@rem <pre>
1> mbManager TRIM ALL
@rem </pre>

The trim command has the effect of deleting old mail, and as such the 
user's waiting mail files may be affected. To clean-up the waiting mail 
files you should use the "Bin/MsgMaint" program:

@rem <pre>
Zeus Bulletin Board System (c)1992-1999 Zeus Developments

Message Subsystem datafile manager - written by Nick Loman
Usage: MsgMaint H=HIGHPTR/K/S,W=WAITMAIL/K/S,AREA/N/K,RANGE/K,ALL/K/S

   HIGHPTR     - Check users high pointer data
   WAITMAIL    - Check users waiting mail datafiles
   AREA        - Specify one area to act upon
   RANGE       - Specify a range of areas to act upon
   ALL         - Act upon all message areas
@rem </pre>

Running "MsgMaint" with the following command line will trim all the 
waiting mail files:

@rem <pre>
1> MsgMaint WAITMAIL ALL
@rem </pre>

Resetting user's high message pointers is also a good idea, else they will 
not know whether new messages have appeared in the area. Use this command 
line for this:

@rem <pre>
1> MsgMaint HIGHPTR ALL
@rem </pre>

These two commands are provided in an easy to use shortcut 
"Messages/TrimBases.zsc", see the Shortcuts section for more information.

Resetting a Message Area

Message Areas should not be reset by deleting the data files it uses. Some 
message base libraries (see Section Message Base Libraries) use specially 
formatted data files to contain message pointer information. To reset all 
the messages in an area and to reset the pointers to 0 you should use 
Bin/mbManager with the following command line:

@rem <pre>
1> mbManager INITIALISE AREA <AreaNumber>
@rem </pre>

You do not have to INITIALISE bases area by area, see the RANGE and ALL 
keywords accepted by mbManager.

Mail Processors which support FTS-1

A fair number of programs for the Amiga (generally found in 
AmiNet/comm/mail and AmiNet/comm/fido) support the FTS-1 format. This is a 
short list of programs that do:

 - Foozle
 - MailManager (set your message bases to Plain or Indexed mode)
 - TrapToss

@{"FTS-1 Config Picture" Link "MsgFTS1.gif/main"}


Setting up bases with mbstarmsg.library

Individual Bases@{uu}

To set an individual message base to the "mbstarmsg.library" you should 
load up the Message Area Configuration, double-click on the area, and press 
the "Sel" button next to the "Library" field.

This will bring up the "Select Message Base Library..." window.

@{"Message Base Libraries Picture" Link "MsgLibs.gif/main"}

Double-clicking on a message base library will assign it to this message 
area.

All Areas@{uu}

The "Zeus:Rexx/MsgAreas/ChangeBaseLib.rexx" script has been provided for 
changing all the areas on your BBS to a new message area. To change all the 
areas to "mbstarmsg.library" for instance you would execute the following 
command line:

@rem <pre>
1> rx Zeus:Rexx/MsgAreas/ChangeBaseLib.rexx mbstarmsg.library
@rem </pre>

NB: The message base filename is case-sensitive, entering 
"MBSTARMSG.LIBRARY" will cause adverse affects!

Installing new message base libraries@{uu}

From the "Select Message Base Library..." window you can add new message 
base library files by clicking on the "ADD" button and using the file 
requester to select the message base library file.


Prefs/Editors

Zeus:Prefs/Editors allows you to configure the Editors Configuration- the 
list of online editors that user's may use to write messages, edit file 
descriptions and create signature files with.

This is the main Editors Configuration window, which can be loaded from the 
Messages->Config->Editors menu option on the Control Window.

@{"Editors Picture" Link "Editors.gif/main"}

Editing an editor brings up the following window:

@{"Editors Config Picture" Link "EditorsCfg.gif/main"}

Name@{uu} - The name of the editor as seen by the user.

Low Access@{uu} - The minimum access level required to access this editor.

High Access@{uu} - The maximum access level required to access this editor.

Remote/Local/Both@{uu} - Whether the editor may be used on Local, Remote or 
either


Editors are configured in a rather different way from other parts of Zeus. 
For each Editor you have a text file which may contain "tags" and Arexx 
script segments. These tags split the file up into sections relating to 
what is being edited, and the Arexx segments are executed when a particular 
editor is run. To edit the text file press the Edit Scripts button. This 
will load up your text editor (as specified in ENV:FILEEDITOR) with the 
current editor's script segment. Here is an example script segment for 
CygnusEd:

The #FILE segment is related to file description editing. All file 
descriptions are edited in t:FileDesc.<line>, where <line> is the current 
line number.

The #MSG segment is related to message text editing. All message text is 
edited in t:MsgText.<line>, where <line> is the current line number.

@rem <pre>
#FILE
CLIDoor `"c:ED -keepio t:FileDesc.'Line'"'

#MSG
CLIDoor  `"c:ED -keepio t:FileDesc.'Line'"'
@rem </pre>


Netmail Mapper

@{"Message Mapper Picture" Link "MsgMapper.gif/main"}

The Netmail Remapper is called Prefs/Mapper and may be accessed from the 
Messages->Config->Netmail Remapper menu on the Control Window.

The Netmail remapper is particularly useful if you are feeding points. The 
basic theory is that messages for the names in the left-hand side box get 
sent to the addresses listed in the right-hand side box. This process is 
carried out during the ZMP Import phase.

In addition, nicknames may be set up: in the above example messages 
addressed to any of SysOp, Nick or Pinky will be renamed to "Nick Loman" 
and sent to his address. This is achieved by using the `@' sign as the 
first character of the name you wish to use as the real name of the user.

The name "Netmail Mapper" isn't too accurate for this program really.  It 
also serves as an Internet e-mail mapper.  When ZGate (see the @{"Internet" link "Manual.Internet"} 
chapter) gates an e-mail to the BBS, it will check the Mapper database.  If 
the person the e-mail is addressed to is not listed in the mapper (listed 
anywhere - as a nick name (described above) for example) then the e-mail 
will be sent to the user on the BBS.  Otherwise, ZGate will follow the 
mapping set out in the Mapper - it will address the e-mail "To:" the real 
name (as set by "@") and then send it to that user's FidoNet address.  This 
lets users have many names before the @ sign in e-mails, for example: sysop
@mybbs.com can point to your name via the mapper.

An additional feature used only by ZGate is the specifying of the outgoing 
e-mail name too.  By writing "^" before the nick name required, messages 
outbound will be remapped to that that nick name: for example, using "@Neil 
Williams,^neil,sysop" I can send e-mails as "neil@mybbs.com" and receive as 
"neil@mybbs.com", "sysop@mybbs.com" and "neil_williams@mybbs.com" which are 
all remapped to the name, "Neil Williams".  In addition to this, the "^" 
option can override the domain name (the bit after the @ sign in the e-mail 
address) which is defined in the Internet preferences.  You do this by 
writing the complete e-mail address the users wishes to use, but swap the @ 
sign for a &.  This is necessary as the "@" sign is already used in the 
Netmail Mapper.  Using our previous example we might use: "@Neil 
Williams,^neil&hotmail.com,sysop" to indicate that the user wishes all out-
going mail to appear that it came from "neil@hotmail.com".

Mapping is quite a powerful feature, and will become more useful as you 
progress to configuring the Internet features later.


Updating File and Message Databases

Upon quitting an Area Configuration program, the following window will pop 
up if any changes have been made:

@{"Update Picture" Link "MsgUpdate.gif/main"}

Save the Configuration@{uu} - You wish to save all the changes made.

Update Online Configuration@{uu} - You wish to update the active configuration.

Update Data Files@{uu} - You wish to make changes to the message data files to 
match any configuration changes made (deleting, renumbering and adding 
areas).



Message Tools

PostMsg

PostMsg is a command line only tool to post a text file into a specified 
message area. This can be used in any circumstances, such as easy posting 
of static messages in the ScriptSet or sending new files lists into echoes 
automatically using a Cron.

@rem <pre>
Offline message posting utility - written by Nick Loman and Neil Williams

Usage: PostMsg 
FROM/A,TO/A,SUBJECT/A,TEXTFILE/A,AREA/N,ADDRESS,PRIVATE/S,NOWRAP/S,TAG/K

   FROM         - Author of message
   TO           - Message recipient
   SUBJECT      - Message topic
   TEXTFILE     - File to retrieve message text from
   AREA         - Area number to save message in
   ADDRESS      - Destination FidoNet address (netmails)
   PRIVATE      - Mark message as private
   NOWRAP       - Don't wrap message lines
   TAG          - If AREA is not given, this Fido Echo name will be looked 
up.
@rem </pre>

As you can see from the @{"Template" link "Manual.CLI_Templates"}, From, To, Subject and TextFile are 
required.  It is obvious what their purposes are: the header and body of 
the message.  You should enclose them in quotes ("") in case they contain 
spaces, which some almost certainly will.

To specify what area to place the message into, you must provide either 
AREA or TAG.  In order to retain backward compatibility, TAG must be 
specified as a keyword (see the @{"Templates" link "Manual.CLI_Templates"} appendix for more details):

Example: to post a welcome message in area 1:

@rem <pre>
PostMsg "The SysOp" "New User" "Welcome!" Zeus:Misc/Welcome.txt 1
@rem </pre>

Note, none of the arguments were /K keywords, so they could be written 
straight in.

Example 2: to post an announcement message in FidoNet area AMY_NEWFILES:

@rem <pre>
PostMsg "The SysOp" "All" "Announcement!" Zeus:Misc/Ann.txt 
TAG=AMY_NEWFILES
@rem </pre>

Since TAG is a keyword, you must write "TAG=<option>" or "TAG <option>".

In order to write netmail using the Area number, it is quite simple.  The 
only thing to be made aware of is that you must, of course, include a 
destination address for the netmail (even if it's your own address):

@rem <pre>
PostMsg "User 1" "User 2" "AutoMessage" "RAM:Msg.txt" 900 1:2/3.4 PRIVATE
@rem </pre>

With 900 being the message area.  Of course, since Netmail should remain 
private, we include the Private switch also (this is not automatically 
set).

To use the Area TAG, it becomes more difficult due to compatibility 
'kludges'.  If you omit AREA, but include ADDRESS, PostMsg sees the address 
as the area number!  Not what we want..  So, since PostMsg will always use 
TAG over AREA, we give it a fake AREA number:

@rem <pre>
PostMsg "User 1" "User 2" "AutoMessage" "RAM:Msg.txt" 0 1:2/3.4 PRIVATE 
TAG=MATRIX
@rem </pre>

So, the Netmail area has the default tag of MATRIX, and I've told it to use 
0 as the area number - which is ignored.

Finally, the NoWrap option has the effect of stopping PostMsg wrapping 
messages at column 75.  In 99% of cases messages need not be wrapped at all 
(in FidoNet, ALL viewing software correctly wraps lines), and in some cases 
(e.g. Newfile listings or ASCII pictures) you certainly don't want to wrap 
the messages lines!


@endnode



@node "Manual.FidoNet" "FidoNet"

Chapter 8 - FidoNet

Introduction

This part of the manual covers the use of the message processing tools 
supplied with Zeus such as ZMP (fidonet mail import and export utility), 
ZMailer (fidonet packet transfering utility) and nodelisting documentation 
such as creating your own point net and using Traplist for parsing 
nodelists. Nodelists are like a telephone book listing the names and 
numbers of all the systems connected to FidoNet.

Also covered are other mail applicable aspects such as file hatching with 
fidonet technology.

About Fidonet and other Networks.

FidoNet is the largest amateur network in the world. It was started in 1984 
by Tom Jennings. Currently more than 20000 nodes worldwide are connected. 
The network has a hierarchic tree (or star) topology:

@{"FidoNet Picture" Link "FidoTopology.gif/main"}

Zones are divided in Regions, which are divided in Nets. The Nets consist 
of Nodes, which are usually (but not necessarily) Bulletin Board Systems. 
Each node has a unique address which consists of four parts: Zone, Net, 
Node and Point, in text form expressed as "zone:net/node.point". Zone 
numbers 1 up to and including 6 are used by FidoNet:

@rem <pre>
    1 = North America (United States of America and Canada)
    2 = Europe and Commonwealth of Independent States
    3 = Oceania (Australia and New Zealand)
    4 = Latin America
    5 = Africa
    6 = Asia
@rem </pre>

There are several other networks that use FidoNet Technology. Networks such 
as AmigaNet use higher zone numbers which are allocated by a central body, 
39-41 for example.

Many nodes have one or more points. A point is a user who gets mail from a 
node (it's "boss"), usually as compressed mail files. This way they can 
read and write messages offline saving time and money. The point address of 
the boss is 0 (e.g. 2:441/58.0) but the ".0" is often omitted from the 
address.

To operate successfully as a FidoNet node a variety of tools are needed. As 
you would expect Zeus provides these tools built-in but also has extensive 
support for third-party programs.


Mailer (also known as a FrontDoor)

The mailer is a program that waits for incoming calls from users and 
FidoNet systems. Users calling are passed through to the BBS. FidoNet calls 
are intercepted and a handshaking procedure is started. This handshaking 
establishes a common protocol between the two mailers, exchanges 
information about each other's system and makes sure that the connection is 
secure (i.e. the passwords match and mail is not being received by a 
foreign source).

Zeus's internal mailer is called ZMailer and is stored in Zeus:Bin. Its 
configuration program is Zeus:Prefs/Mailer.

Other Amiga mailers are available and compatible with Zeus. They are all 
available on the AmiNet.

@rem <pre>
Mailer                   AmiNet FileName

TrapDoor                 from most BBS's as TD_1_84.lzh
Gotcha                   Gotcha12b1.lha
GMS                      comm/fido/GMS_11b64.lha
JamMail                  comm/fido/jm960227.lha
"The Shelter Mailers"    comm/fido/SHELTER275.lha
@rem </pre>


Mail Processor (also known as a tosser, scanner or packer)

This program is used to "import" and "export" mail. Most mail processors 
work on a two phase basis. Exporting scans the message bases on the BBS 
searching for mail that has not been seen by all the systems who take an 
area (or 'echo') and gathers it into "bundles" stored in the "outbound" 
directory ready for collecting or sending by the Mailer. Importing scans 
the "inbound" directory for new mail bundles received by the Mailer and 
unpacks them into the message bases ready for reading by the BBS users and 
the system's points.

Zeus's internal mail processor is called ZMP and is stored in Zeus:Bin. Its 
configuration is stored and changed using a text file called 
Zeus:Config/ZMP.cfg.

Other Amiga mail processors are available and compatible with Zeus (using 
the mbstarmsg.library, see chapter Message Subsystem, section Message Base 
Libraries).

@rem <pre>
Mail Processor                  AmiNet FileName

EMS                             ems_1_0_exec.lha
Foozle                          comm/fido/foozle102.lha
MailManager                     comm/fido/mm_1_2.lha
JS_Toss                         comm/fido/JS_Toss102b.lha
TrapToss                        comm/fido/TrapToss_1_50.lzh
@rem </pre>


Tick Processor

FidoNet also provides support for "file echoes". These are similar in setup 
to a message echo, except they carry binary files rather than ASCII message 
text. A tick processor reads in TIC files from the inbound directory and 
adds their associated files to the correct file areas. Tick often has a 
companion called "Hatch" which allows FidoNet systems to send files to the 
file echo for distribution.

Zeus's Tick processor is built into the Zeus Mail Processor (ZMP) and can 
be run by specifying the TICK keyword on operation.

Other Tick processors for the Amiga are available, but not recommended for 
use as they cannot integrate with the Zeus file catalogue which renders 
them only partially useful.  It is still possible to use them if they set 
the file comment/note of the file, in which case files can be added to the 
Zeus file catalogue by running "FileManager AddNew FileNote Areas <areas>".


Areafix

Areafix is a relatively new FidoNet utility designed to take the pain out 
of running a busy node system for the SysOp. Before Areafix was used 
FidoNet administrators had to "fix" people into message echoes manually by 
editing their configurations. This was time wasting when users wished to 
add a large number of echoes. In addition to this, Areafix allows user's to 
connect to echoes not taken by the node. This is done by sending a chain of 
Areafix requests back up the FidoNet hierachy until a system is found which 
taks the echo that the user requries. Areafix could cause huge space 
wastage and gigantic mail packets if abused, so most Areafix programs allow 
strict access restriction systems to be implemented to stop some systems 
fixing into private echoes, too many, or any at all.

Zeus's Areafix is built into the Zeus Mail Processor (ZMP) and is 
automatically run when an import is in progress.


Nodelists

To function in a proper environment the Zeus specific mailer (ZMailer) 
requires you to have configured nodelists for every FidoNet technology 
network you carry on your bulletin board. This may include a pointlist, as 
nodelists only carry information about zones, networks and nodes.

The 'TrapList' utilities you will need to compile and update nodelists can 
be found on disk #4 of the Zeus distribution set as `NODELIST.LHA'.

Please take the time to read the relevant documentation for traplist and 
the nodelist tools within that archive for a more verbose description of 
nodelist technology than covered here.

To cover briefly setting a system up for a link with your BBS system then 
it's just a matter of using the SetPasswd tool within the Nodelist archive 
like this:

 SetPassWd <Session Password> Address_1 Address_2 etc....

These passwords can also be set within the traplist configuration file 
which will be explained later on, along with an example configuration file. 
This is also the recommended route since the nodelist data can be damaged 
and lose all the password information, and you can backup the traplist 
configuration file very easily.

Needless to say both you, and the system(s) you are exchanging mail with 
will have to use the same passwords, else the link will be considered 
insecure and mail will not be transferred.

Here is an example traplist configuration which will give you an idea on 
setting up to recieve the Zeus support echos.

@rem <pre>
ZONE 85

NODELISTPATH "NODELIST:"
BUFFERSIZE 65536
REPORT "Ram:Traplist.log"
NOCHECKCRC
NODELIST "ZeusNet"
PASSWORD "SECRET" 85:1/1.0
DIAL "44" "0"
@rem </pre>

The entries broken down individually are :-

Zone@{uu} - This is the network number. 85 is a unique number used by the 
ZeusNet. While more than one nodelist can be parsed with this single config 
file, you must use only one Zone line which should be the lowest network 
number you are on.

NodelistPath@{uu} - This is the directory where the nodelists and nodediff files 
are present. You must assign Nodelist: to a directory and use that path 
here as in the example above.

BufferSize@{uu} - The bigger the buffer, the quicker traplist can process the 
nodelists. Obviously this depends on memory restrictions with your 
computer.

Report@{uu} - Path to a file to output any messages from the processing. A 
logfile you can refer to to check if it all worked okay.

NoCheckCRC@{uu} - If this line is ommited then Traplist will run a crc check on 
the nodelists listed in the following lines and compare it to the number 
within the nodelist itself. This is really only of use to sysops using 
nodediff files for updating nodelists and for nets as small as Zeus, it's 
easier to just release the full nodelist and so crc checksums within the 
nodelist are rarely needed.

Nodelist@{uu} - As many of these entries as you like can be configured. Each 
name will be checked by traplist for a nodelist <name>.082 for example and 
processing will take place if it finds a valid file. You don't need to 
specify an extension number on the name as traplist will do it for you, and 
automatically makes sure it uses the highest number.

 For example if ZeusNet.024 and ZeusNet.046 were found, *.046 would be 
processed.

Password@{uu} - This is how you configure session password between systems 
linked to your bbs for security checks and to prevent any system polling 
your Mailer with the right address and stealing someone elses mail.

 For example

 PASSWORD "Backyard" 85:1/13.0

 This means that if system 85:1/13.0 calls your mailer then no files will 
be transferred unless it also sent a session password of Backyard.

DIAL@{uu} - This is a modifier string for changing phone number formats within 
the nodelists as they are processed.

 For example

 DIAL "44" "0"

means any instances of 44 at the start of a phone number for a system in 
the nodelist would be replaced with 0, used by UK sysops as the 44 is the 
international dialling code used to dial into the the UK from outside. A 
system with the number "44-1423-123456" in their nodelist entry would be 
replaced with "01423-123456" when dialling out. You should check what your 
county's international dialling code is (you can find examples in the 
nodelist) and replace it for the 44 here.


NB: This is a very brief breakdown of usage regarding Traplist and mailer 
technology. Refer to the full documentation within the Trapdoor archive for 
more detailed explanations as the above is a quick starting guide only.

Here is an example pointlist file which you can customise to generate a 
list of points from which ZMailer will get their system information when a 
call is recieved from their address. It's not actually necessary to use a 
pointlist with Zeus but you will still need a password line for each point 
listing their session password and addresses or they will not be able to 
send or recieve mail. You set up a points password details within the 
TrapList config file (as detailed above) as normal, or with the SetPasswd 
tool within the Trapdoor archive. Setting passwords in the TrapList 
configuration is recommended as passwords set with SetPasswd can easily be 
lost if your compiled NodeList becomes corrupt.

@rem <pre>
;A My Pointlist/Fakenet list -- fake crc 0 : 0<Ctrl M>
;A^M
Zone,2,->^M
Region,254,->^M
,255,BackYard,North_Harrow,Anthony_Brice,44-0-181-1234567,9600,HST,MO^M
Point,1,Hell_On_Earth,North_Harrow,Anthony_Brice,UNLISTED,9600,MO^M
Point,3,The_Garage,Rayners_Lane,Gary_Pearse,UNLISTED,9600,MO^M
^Z
@rem </pre>

Note: The information within the < > brackets are control codes which need 
to be used when generating your own pointlist. <Ctrl M> means press Control 
and M at that point. I recommend using an editor such as CygnusEd when 
creating a nodelist, as that allows the insertion of such control codes, 
and nodelists MUST have these codes to process properly.

The Point lines are defined as below:

Point,<PointNumber>,<SiteText>,<Location>,<Point 
Name>,<PhoneNumber>,<Baud>,<Flags>


PointNumber@{uu} - Their unique number as a point.

Site Name@{uu} - Some text describing their system.

Location@{uu} - Their town / state.

Phone number@{uu} - Can be set to UNLISTED as above, which means you can't use a 
mailer to dial them up by using their fido address as they are points and 
wouldn't have a mailer to answer the phone.

Baud@{uu} - A maximum rate of 9600 due to some old nodelist processors not 
handling handle a five digit number.

Flags@{uu} - Usually set to MO (Mail Only).


Setting up a simple node system

Most bulletin board's operate as "nodes" within the FidoNet hierarchy.

Here is an example configuration for a FidoNet node "2:888/43.0" who polls 
an IBM system called "2:888/1.0".


Addressing

A note on addressing:

The address defined in the NODE keyword is used as the base for all the 
AREA, SYSTEM and ROUTE addresses so you only need to specify the parts 
which are different.

Example:

@rem <pre>
NODE 85:1/1.0@zeusnet

85:1/100.0@zeusnet
85:1/100.0
85:1/100
1/100
100
@rem </pre>

Are all valid ways of writing '85:1/100.0@zeusnet'

@rem <pre>
85:1/100.1@zeusnet
85:1/100.1
1/100.1
100.1
@rem </pre>

Are all valid ways of writing '85:1/100.1@zeusnet'

@rem <pre>
85:1/1.1@zeusnet
85:1/1.1
1/1.1
1.1
.1
@rem </pre>

Are all valid ways of writing '85:1/1.1@zeusnet'


Structure of ZMP.cfg

This is the order of the items required for a ZMP configuration:

@rem <pre>
; Comment Lines

Main Configuration Tags

-> Network
NODE
AKA
POINTNET
ZONES

SYSTEM Lines
AREA lines
TICK Lines
ROUTE Lines
-> Network

Next Network...
@rem </pre>


Configuration files are stored in Zeus:Config/ZMP.cfg

This is an example:

@rem <pre>

MSGBUFFER 32768
IDXSTORE  1024

; CRCDUPE
; NODUPECHECK

FLOCTRL
4DFLPO
CTRLCHAR ^
NOCHKNETDOMAIN
NOCHKPKTDOMAIN
BOUNCEBADADDR
BOUNCENOROUTE
BOUNCENOUSER
UNPACKDIR T:
PACKDIR T:

; FidoNet setup

NODE           2:888/43.0
AKA            0
POINTNET       8843
ZONES          1 2 3 4 5 6
CREATENEW      1
CREATELINK     1

SYSTEM     1   U2YYNNNBY   ZIP   PASS   PASS   PASS   0

AREA       NET888          0     1 43
AREA       AMIGA_UK        0     1 43
AREA       AMIGASOFT       0     1 43
AREA       CONTROV         0     1 43

ROUTE      *:*/*.*         1
@rem </pre>

For a better and more fully commented ZMP.cfg example, please see the 
example files installed in Zeus:Config/


Explanation of configuration tags.

MSGBUFFER@{uu} <bytes>

defines the largest FTN message ZMP will be able to import. <Number> is the 
amount of bytes ZMP allocates and must be a minimum of 32768 bytes.

IDXSTORE@{uu} <records>

defines the amount of index records in ZMP.index. The more indexes, the 
better the dupe checking and reply linking but ZMP will use more memory, 
disk space and time. Each index record is 36 bytes long.

CRCDUPE@{uu}

if a message has no `ID' with which to uniquely identify it, ZMP will check 
if the message is a dupe by ensuring no two messages have exactly the same 
header (to, from, subject, date, etc.).  It is remotely possible for two 
messages to have the exact same header, however this isn't very common.

NODUPECHECK@{uu}

disables Dupe checking completely.

FLOCTRL@{uu}

makes ZMP use special characters in the .?LO flow files. Most Amiga mailers 
support this flag (including ZMailer), but some older ones may require 
NOFLOCTRL to be set.

4DFLO@{uu}

makes ZMP use 4 dimensional filenames in the Outbound directories. Again 
most Amiga mailers (including ZMailer) support this format.

CTRLCHAR@{uu} <character>

indicates that mail packet files should be prefixed with <character> in 
outgoing mail packets. The default is a ^ which means that the file should 
be deleted. Most Amiga mailers support these characters. Other acceptable 
values are:

# - Truncate this file to zero length

^ - Delete this file (with logging)

- - Delete this file (without logging)

~ - Don't send this file

NOCHKNETDOMAIN@{uu}

When a netmail's origin and destination addresses are not in the same 
domain, ZMP will attempt to bounce the message back to the sender. 
Specifying this keyword disables this checking.

NOCHKPKTDOMAIN@{uu}

If ZMP detects that a message packet's origin and destination addresses are 
not in the same domain, it will rename it *.BAD and not process it. 
Specifying this keyword disables this check.

BOUNCEBADADDR@{uu}

If you use netmail domain checking (by not specifying NOCHKNETDOMAIN in 
your config) then badly addressed messages will be bounced back to the node 
which sent them (if possible).

BOUNCENOROUTE@{uu}

If a netmail is processed which is not destined for your system but ZMP is 
unable to route the message to another system, a message will be sent back 
to the sender telling them about it.

BOUNCENOUSER@{uu}

Specifying this keyword causes ZMP to check the To: field of every netmail 
that is destined for your system against your current list of users. If the 
recipient does not have a user record, ZMP will bounce the message with the 
message 'User not known at this address'.

UNPACKDIR@{uu} <directory>

The temporary directory ZMP will unpack mail bundles into. Specifying a 
directory in the Ram Disk (RAM:) will make importing quicker, but will 
cause problems if trying to import large sized message packets on a system 
without much free memory.

PACKDIR@{uu} <directory>

The temporary directory ZMP will pack the mail bundles from before they are 
put in the outbound directory. The same limitations apply here as with 
UNPACKDIR.

ATTACHDIR@{uu} <directory>

The directory to place any files that arrive attached to messages. If you 
do not use this keyword, the inbound directory will be used.


Also..

INBOUND@{uu} <directory>
OUTBOUND@{uu} <directory>

These force ZMP to use different directories than those set in the General 
Paths configuration (in the Prefs/System program).  Useful if you don't 
want ZMP to import packets/bundles received via ZMailer (if you use another 
message processor such as MailManager), but do still want ZMP for other 
work.



Each network you have is defined under a NODE entry

NODE@{uu} <network address>

NODE defines your address in a FTN network. The address must be at least 4d 
(Zone:Net/Node.Point) and preferably 5d (Zone:Net/Node.Point@Domain).

AKA@{uu} <network address>

Defines one or more AKA ("also known as") addresses. These extra addresses 
allow you to, for example, run a multi-node system with each node having 
its own network address. Mail can then be sent to any of your addresses and 
still be correctly processed without the need for additional, complete, 
NODE entries.

POINTNET@{uu} <number>

The net number defined by POINTNET will be stripped out of incoming 
messages and replaced with 3d control lines.

ZONES@{uu} <zone number, zone number [...]>

Defines all valid zone numbers for this domain. These are used when 
incoming netmail messages have no domain information. A maximum of twenty 
may be defined.

CREATENEW@{uu} <area>

When an echomail comes in with an unknown tag and the sending system has 
ALLOWNEW capabilities, ZMP will also create a message area on your board. 
If the given message base number is already used, ZMP will increment it 
until it finds an unused one.

CREATELINK@{uu} <area>

If you want to link into a pass-through area you would usually have to 
create the area on the board and then add your address to the appropriate 
AREA line. By specifying this keyword (BEFORE your AREA lines) you only 
need to add your address to the AREA line and ZMP will create a new area on 
the board while it loads its config.

The Message Base Number works in the same way as CREATENEW.


Under each NODE (network) entry, you then list your SYSTEMs - those nodes 
and points with which you will be sharing mail.

SYSTEM@{uu} <address> <flag array> <archiver> <packet password> <areafix 
password> <tick pw> <groups>

defines a known system for the current network. The arguments are as 
follows:

Address - The FTN address of this system.

Flag Array - Nine characters together (i.e. 'U2YYNNNBY') as follows:

1. AreaFix Access - One of the following characters:

 N No AreaFix access. All requests from this system will be ignored.
 Y May use %LIST, %QUERY, %UNLINKED, %HELP, %PAUSE, %RESUME, %RESCAN, 
%FORCERESCAN and link/unlink into echos.
 U As for 'Y' but AreaFix enables uplink requests. (See AreaFix section).
 C Full AreaFix control. May create, delete and rename echos.  May also 
request for other systems.

2. Control Type
 Specifies the number of dimensions used on SEEN-BY and PATH lines.  Type 2 
must only be used if the calling point needs to use a "pointnet", this is 
only necessary if they use extremely old software.

 2 Net/Node for nodes. PointNet/Point for points.
 3 Net/Node for nodes. Net/Node.Point for points.

3. Packet Change
 Specify 'Y' to make ZMP generate the same packet header types for this 
system
 as it receives. 'N' to disable.

4. Archiver
 Change Specify 'Y' to make ZMP change the archiver it uses on this systems 
mail
 packets to match the ones it receives.  'N' to disable.

5. Netmail Flow
 Specifies the 'flavour' of netmail packets.  This is how the messages will 
be sent by the FidoNet mailer.  The changes are visible in the Outbound 
mail directory as <address>.FLO, <address>.HLO, etc.

 N  Normal  -  You must call the destination to deliver.
 H  Hold    -  The destination must call you to deliver.
 D  Direct  -  Mail will be sent directly to the SYSTEM in question.
 C  Crash   -  All netmail will be 'crashed' - it will be sent directly to 
the SYSTEM in question via a direct call, even if there is a routing entry 
which affects this address.

6. Echomail Flow
 Specifies the 'flavour' of echomail packets. Same options as the netmail 
flow
 flag.

7. Tick FLO
 Specifies the 'flavour' of *.TIC files. Same options as the netmail flow 
flag.

8. Packet header
 Specifies the packet header type used on outgoing mail packets. May be one 
of:

 A Basic FTS-0001 revision 15 header. No point information - not a 
recommended format.
 B FSC-0039 header. Uses four dimensional addressing.
 C FSC-0048 header. Uses four dimensional addressing. Some programs cannot 
use this format, but it is slightly more advanced. At least some Xenolink 
versions require FSC-0039 and will reject these.
 D FSC-0045 (Packet 2.2) header. Has full five dimensional addresses.

9. Archive Netmail.
 If this SYSTEM can handle archived netmail, set this flag to 'Y', 
otherwise set to 'N' and the netmails will be delivered as an uncompressed 
PKT file. Often, Amiga programs can handle compressed netmail while PC ones 
cannot.


Archiver

 The name of an archiver as defined with Zeus:Prefs/Archivers. All echomail 
(and optionally netmail) for this system will be archived using the 
specified type.

Packet Password

 For extra security, you can specify a password which will be placed in the 
packet header. If you do not want to use a packet password (or the system 
does not support them) use '-NONE-' without the quotes.

AreaFix Password

 If the system has AreaFix access then you can specify a password which the 
system must put in the subject field of all AreaFix requests. If you don't 
want to use an AreaFix password then specify '-NONE-'. If you want to use 
the packet password for AreaFix as well, specify two quote marks like "".

Tick Password

 The password to be used in *.TIC files sent and received to/from this 
system. You can disable the password by specifying '-NONE-', you can 
duplicate the AreaFix password by using "".

Group Number(s)

 The area group numbers that this system has access to. AreaFix uses this 
information when deciding what echoes this system can link into. You can 
place more than one group number here, simply put a space between each one 
and ZMP will recognise them all. Learn more about groups below, in the AREA 
section.



After having defined which SYSTEMs are in this network, we can now define 
the AREAs which are available to them.

AREA@{uu} <tagname> <group> <address address [...]>

This keyword defines an echomail area. The arguments are:

TagName

 The tagname for the echo.

Group

 A number between 0-65535. By grouping certain echos together you can 
easily restrict access for a system. Each AREA can belong to one group (NB: 
please don't confuse ZMP groups with Special Interest Group on the BBS) and 
each SYSTEM can belong to more than one group. For a SYSTEM to be allowed 
access to an AREA via AreaFix, the SYSTEM must belong to the group of which 
the AREA is a member.

Linked Systems

 A list of system addresses which are linked into this echo. If you do not 
specify your address, the messages will not be stored in any message base 
on your BBS, but be 'passed through' to the other connected systems. This 
PassThough option is used when you don't wish to read an AREA for yourself 
(or your BBS users) but you do allow other people who area Linked to the 
AREA to be able to still receive it. Note: if no other SYSTEMs are linked 
to the AREA, imported messages are simply deleted on import. Therefore you 
should watch these areas and disconnect from your uplink when nobody else 
is linked in.
 The address may be prefixed (i.e. =12/3 #15:44/123) with one of the 
following characters:

 '-' The system has read-only access to this echo.

 '=' The system is not linked in but if they link in using AreaFix, they 
will have read-only access.

 '|' The system is linked in but their feed is currently paused (using the 
%PAUSE AreaFix command). On issuing the %RESUME command, they will have 
normal access.

 '#' The system is linked in but their feed is paused and when they resume 
the area, they will have read-only access.

 Advanced usage:

 If your BBS is a hub or other distribution system in a network, you may 
have several AKA addresses in addition to the main NODE address.  While ZMP 
will import messages addressed to an AKA without problems, it is not 
immediately possible to export messages using these AKAs. To make this 
possible, there is a special command which can be embedded inside the list 
of linked addresses of an AREA or TICK (see below) line.

 Take this fragment of ZMP.cfg as an example:

       NODE 2:442/107.0
       AKA  2:442/110.0

       AREA AMIGA_UK 0 .1 .2 .3 USEAKA 2:442/110 .4 .5

 Downlink .1, .2, .3 AND .5 receive Fido from 2:442/107.  Point 4, however, 
receives Fido mail from the AKA 2:442/110.  Noteworthy points: any system 
linking in via AreaFix automatically receives mail from the NODE (main) 
address and netmails always come from the NODE address.

 The `scope' of UseAKA is therefore only one address - only the next one 
listed (here, .4) is exported to as 2:442/110.  The next after that, .5, is 
exported to as normal using the NODE address, 2:442/107.


After defining the AREAs, we must provide one or more ROUTE lines in order 
that ZMP knows where to send Netmails written or received.

ROUTE@{uu} <from address> <to address>

Specifies Netmail routing information. When Netmail's are imported to the 
system, their to address is compared against the "from address" entry of 
each ROUTE statement in the applicable network. If a route statement 
matches, then the Netmail will be routed to the "to address", e.g., if a 
netmail addressed to 2:888/67.0 was imported to the system and the 
following ROUTE lines existed:

@rem <pre>
ROUTE 40      56
ROUTE *:*/*.* 1
@rem </pre>

The first entry would be ignored as the to node of the message is 67, not 
40. The next entry is a wildcard and all addresses will match it. Therefore 
the message would be routed to node 1 (2:888/1.0).



There are more configuration options which ZMP can use in each NODE 
section. The following keywords must appear after a NODE definition 
(preferably, after the ROUTE lines):

NEWTICKAREA@{uu} <File Area Number>

When ZMP creates a new TICK area, it uses the given number as the file area 
for the new TICK line. ZMP does NOT create new file areas on your BBS.

TICK@{uu} <TagName> <Group> <File Area> [Linked System Addresses]

Defines a TICK file echo.  The format and usage is the same as the AREA 
line but you need to specify a file area number so ZMP knows where to save 
the files.  If you wish to have a pass-through area, set the <File Area> to 
zero.  "UseAKA", as demonstrated in the AREA documentation above, is 
equally valid in the TICK lines.

FILEECHO@{uu} <TagName> <FileAreaNumber>

Echomail messages may also have file attaches. This keyword links an echo 
tag to a file area on your BBS. Files that come attached to messages in 
<TagName> will be uploaded to area <FileAreaNumber>automatically.

UPLINK@{uu} <Address> <Password>

Specifies an uplink address for AreaFix to automatically request known 
areas. See AreaFix section for more information. The password should be the 
one you use when accessing the given systems AreaFix.

ALLOWNEW@{uu} <Address> [Address...]

When a message is received with an unknown tag ZMP will create the AREA in 
your ZMP.cfg only if the message came from an address defined with the 
ALLOWNEW keyword.

AUTOECHOADD@{uu} <Address> [Address] ...

Whenever ZMP creates a new AREA, it automatically adds the addresses 
specified with this keyword to the export to list. Very useful if you are a 
hub for a small network!

AUTOTICKADD@{uu} <Address> [Address] ...

Whenever ZMP creates a new TICK, it automatically adds the addresses 
specified with this keyword to the export to list.

TWITUSER@{uu} <User Name>

Any messages being imported that are from <User Name> will be deleted.

TWITADDR@{uu} <Address>

Any messages being imported that are addressed from <Address> will be 
deleted. <Address> may include wildcards.

MAXPKTSIZE@{uu} <Bytes> <Address> [Address] ...

Some systems cannot handle mail packets over a certain size. This keyword 
specifies the maximum number of bytes per packet to create for the listed 
systems (wildcards accepted).

MAXBUNDLESIZE@{uu} <Bytes> <Address> [Address] ...

More importantly, some systems cannot handle mail bundles (archived 
packets) over a certain size, usually due to memory or disk limitations. 
This keyword specifies the maximum number of bytes per bundle to create for 
the listed systems (wildcards accepted). Remember that as the packets are 
compressed to about a third of their usual size, so if MAXPKTSIZE is set at 
256000 bytes, MAXBUNDLESIZE at 512000 is plenty!



Here are two special commands which can appear during the AREA lines:

ITRANSLATION@{uu} <Table1> [Table2]

Translation tables are stored in Zeus:Config/Translation. On import, the 
text is translated through <Table1> and then, if specified, through 
<Table2>.  The convert the character set of messages from one type to 
another.  Defined tables affect all areas after the keywords, e.g.

@rem <pre>
ITRANSLATION ISO IBM
OTRANSLATION IBM ISO

AREA ...
AREA ...

These areas are affected by the ISO-IBM translations.

ITRANSLATION SE7 ISO
OTRANSLATION ISO SE7

AREA ...
AREA...

These areas are affected by the SE7-ISO translation tables.
@rem </pre>

OTRANSLATION@{uu} <Table1> [Table2]

On export, the text is translated through <Table1> and then, if specified, 
through <Table2>. It is then exported to all connected nodes.
The translation tables can be found as seperate files in the directory 
Zeus:Config/Translation/



Other ZMP configuration tags.

These are some very useful (sometimes necessary) configuration options 
which should appear at the top of the ZMP.cfg file, before any NODE 
sections.


DELFILECHAR@{uu} <Character>

This character will be prefixed to netmail attached files in the 
destinations .FLO file if you have turned on FLOCTRL and DELNETATTACHED.

DELNETATTACHED@{uu}

When an incoming netmail, which has attached files, is not destined for 
your system the files will be added to the destinations FLO file with the 
DELFILECHAR prefixed to the file name so your mailer will deleted the file 
once sent. This only works in conjunction with the FLOCTRL keyword.

DELNETMAIL@{uu}

ZMP always imports netmails into your message base but if this keyword is 
used, ones that are not from or to your system will be deleted during 
export.

ATTACHDIR@{uu} <Directory>

Files that come in attached to messages will be put in the ATTACHDIR. When 
the messages are exported, ZMP will look here for attached files.

AREAFMT@{uu} <Format String>

When AreaFix adds a new area to your ZMP.cfg it uses the specified format 
string to make it match to your other AREA lines. You need one '%s' (for 
the tagname) and one '%lu' for the group number. Both are standard C 
formatting codes. There are two other special codes which are:

@rem <pre>
       \\t  Tab (single backslash followed by t)
       \\s  Space (single backslash followed by s)
@rem </pre>

TICKFMT@{uu} <Format String>

As for AREAFMT but this specifies the format of TICK lines. You need an 
extra '%lu' in the format for the file area number, but apart from that, it 
is the same as AREAFMT.

NOAREAFIX@{uu}

Disables ZMP's internal AreaFix.  Should you use ZMP on a point or use a 
3rd party AreaFix program, set this to completely disable AreaFix in ZMP.

MINMSGS@{uu} <Number>

Specifies the number of new messages that must be in a message base before 
ZMP will try to export the area. By running ZMP with the arguments `EXPORT 
FORCE', ZMP will ignore the MINMSGS setting.  You should run ZMP with FORCE 
at least once a day (on a Cron) and after major mail polls in order that 
messages in low-flow areas are not left unexported for days or weeks.
   The purpose of this option is to assist those SysOps running a busy 
system on a slower computer.  If you had to pack messages into 50 downlink 
bundles every time a point delivered their mail, you could be waiting for a 
long time with a 68000 or 68020. Using this, you can accumulate single 
posted messages over several calls until there is enough to make the export 
`session' worthwhile.

NOUSERBOUNCE@{uu} <User Name>

If you have user bouncing enabled (with BOUNCENOUSER - don't get confused!) 
there are some names that you might not want to cause a bounce with, 
i.e.'SysOp'. This keyword gives you this option.  One name per entry, but 
as many entries as you wish.

NETEXEC@{uu} <Name> <Password> <Executable>

If a netmail is received addressed to <Name> the given executable will be 
run. <Name> must have quotes around it if there are spaces.

Password should be a maximum of ten characters long but may be pecified as 
'-NONE-' which will disable the password checking. Using NETEXEC before any 
NODE statements defines a global entry, you can also limit the entry to a 
single network by putting it after the appropriate NODE entry.

Various control codes can be passed to the "Executable" line of NETEXEC to 
expand into data relating to the message:

%f
 Who the message is from

%t
 Who the message is to

%s
 The subject of the message (Usually password but could be other data if 
your password is -NONE-!)

%A
 The from address of the message (Zone:Net/Node.Point)

%D
 The dest address of the message (Zone:Net/Node.Point)

%n
 The number of the saved message

%a
 The area the message was saved into

%F
 Who the message is from (with underscores)

%T
 Who the message is to (with underscores)

%S
 The subject of the message (with underscores)

%%
 A '%' character

DELBADMSGID@{uu}

If a message comes in with a non-FTN standard MSGID kludge it will usually 
be imported. Specify this flag if you want these messages to be deleted 
during import. Not really recommended, as there are a lot of PC packages 
which really mess it up, and gated Internet messages often don't use FTN 
style MSGID lines.

ALLOWRESCAN@{uu}

By specifying this keyword, you allow systems that have AreaFix access to 
use the %RESCAN and %FORCERESCAN commands. See the AreaFix help 
documentation for more information.

ALLOWMIXEDPKT@{uu}

Special kludge which causes echomail packets to be processed in a slightly 
different way to support some brain-dead PC tosser software!

NOECHOCREATE@{uu}

Disables ZMP's auto echomail creation function.

NOTICKCREATE@{uu}

Disables ZMP's auto tick area creation function.

TWITUSER@{uu} <User Name>

Any messages being imported that come from <User Name> will be deleted. 
This can be a global setting for all domains, but can also be restricted to 
a single network by placing in a NODE section. Don't worry about this 
breaking FidoNet's golden rule of not interfering with in transit mail - 
mail from twits will still be passed on to linked SYSTEM's or routed in 
netmail. You'll just never see it on your BBS.

HIDEARCS@{uu}

When (de)archiving mail packets, the archivers output will be redirected to 
NIL: if this keyword is set.

ANNOUNCEAREA@{uu} <Message Area Number>

When a new Message or Tick echo is created automatically, a message will be 
posted in this message area to inform yourself and/or your users.  Placed 
in the global section, this will affect all areas. In the NODE section, it 
will affect only that network.  You can, of course, also have a global 
entry and still have messages for some networks go to unique areas by 
placing an ANNOUNCEAREA in any NODE section.

NORETRECREQ@{uu}

From Zeus version 1.2, ZMP can send "Receipts". These are messages sent to 
a user when their message reaches your system and is addressed to a user 
here: think of them as confirmation that a message has got to it's 
destination.  Programs such as Spot and MailManager can request these be 
sent, and in any case a receipt will only be sent if requested by whoever 
sent the message.  This switch stops ZMP sending receipts for received 
netmail.

NOAUDITREQ@{uu}

Also from Zeus version 1.2, ZMP can send "Audits". These are messages sent 
to a user when their message reaches your system and NOT is addressed to a 
user here - the audit message sent to the original sender tells them where 
the message was routed to after passing through your system.  They can be 
thought of as being like the PATH: or Via lines which is are every message, 
only in this case Audit trails can be used to check where a message is 
failing to be correctly routed on from.  This switch stops ZMP sending 
audits for received netmail.

NOAREAFIXFUZZY@{uu}

Disable fuzzy string matching on incorrect Areafix echo requests (where an 
echo specified doesn't exist). This is on by default.

QUICKDISPLAY@{uu}

Updates the display in the shell every second rather than for every 
message.



Uplinks

Uplinks are systems which you poll for mail, have a wider range of echos, 
and you have AreaFix access to.

To setup an uplink, you need to specify them in the config with a SYSTEM 
line and an UPLINK line. You also need a list of echo tagnames that they 
carry and you have access to. This list needs to be saved in the 
Zeus:Config/AreaFix/ directory and the filename format is z.n.n.p.LST

An example:

I have an uplink at 1:234/56.0 and they carry three echos which I don't. 
Their names are ECHO1, ECHO2 and ECHO3. So I create a file called 
Zeus:Config/AreaFix/1.234.56.0.LST and in it I have the echo tagnames 
listed one per line, like so:

@rem <pre>
ECHO1         Example Echo #1
ECHO2         Example Echo #2
ECHO3         Example Echo #3
@rem </pre>

If the echo tagname is followed by a description (as in the above example) 
then it will be used to describe the area if a system asks for an %UPLINK 
list.

When a system (with uplink access) requests ECHO2, AreaFix scans the 
echolist file, finds ECHO2 and sends an AreaFix request to 1:234/56.0 
asking it for the echo.

Meanwhile, the original requesting system is told that the echo has been 
requested from the uplink and ZMP adds a new AREA line into ZMP.cfg which 
sets up ECHO2 as a pass-through area. When the new echo starts flowing from 
the uplink, it is passed automatically to the original system and you don't 
need to change your configs at all!

The uplink lists are AREAS.BBS format, if your uplink can supply them. 
Otherwise, an edited %LIST response could be used.


The AreaFix Help Command

The following help file is sent to the user on receipt of a %HELP command 
in an AreaFix request - if you would like to edit it for your own needs, it 
is stored in Zeus:Config/AreaFix/AreaFix.hlp


1. What is an AreaFix?

   Running an AreaFix saves the SysOp of a node from the annoying business
   of adding/removing systems to/from their echomail distribution list.


2. Formatting your AreaFix request

   Requests are made via netmail messages which contain specific
   information in some of the fields.  In particular, the addressee is
   always 'AreaFix' and the subject is the password assigned to you by the
   SysOp of this system.

   For example:

      From:     Jon Dough    (1:234/56.7)
      To:       AreaFix      (1:234/56.0)
      Subject:  Password

   The text of the message contains one or more request commands (one per
   line) which are detailed below.


3. Information requests

   You can request information from AreaFix about what echos you have
   access to and which ones are already being exported to you.

   These are the currently supported commands:

@rem <PRE>
      %HELP      Sends this file to your system as a netmail.

      %LIST      Lists all the echomail and file areas currently available
                 from this system.  The list also shows which areas you are
                 currently linked into.

      %QUERY     Lists all the areas you are currently linked into.

      %UNLINKED  Lists all the areas you are _not_ currently linked into 
but
                 are available to you.

      %UPLINK    Lists all the echomail areas available to you from this
                 systems uplinks (if any).
@rem </PRE>


4. Linking and Unlinking to areas

   You can link into an echo by specifying it's tagname in your AreaFix
   request.

   For example:

      ECHONAME

   Will link you into the echo with a tagname of 'ECHONAME'.  If the echo
   doesn't exist at this system, and you have the relevent access, the
   request will be passed to this systems uplink.

   You can unlink from an echo by specifying a '-' before the tagname.

   For example:

      -ECHONAME

   As an extension to this, you can request to be linked/unlinked to/from
   all the available echomail areas at this system.

      %ALL
      %-ALL

   The first command would link your system into all the echos available to
   you and the second unlinks them all.  These commands do not apply to 
file
   echos.

   After any echomail link changes, AreaFix will send you a message with
   the results of the request.


5. %PAUSE and %RESUME

   If you wish to temporarily disable the flow of all echomail areas
   without actually linking out of them, then send the %PAUSE command.

   When you want to enable the echomail flow send a %RESUME command.

   Netmails for your system are unaffected by these commands.

   Messages in echomails areas addressed to you are also unaffected by
   these commands if you are listed in the SysOp's 'Netmail remapper.'
   Ask the SysOp to be listed if you wish to ensure that you receive
   personal mail.


6. Rescanning

   Sending a %RESCAN command to AreaFix will cause it to export all the
   messages you haven't seen yet from all the echos you are linked into.

   You can rescan a single area by using:

      %RESCAN <Echo Name>

   ... which will also link you into that area if you are not already.

   Specifying ALL for the echoname will have the same effect as having
   no arguments.

   If the BBS holds a large number of messages, you could easily get far
   more than you originally intended. To get around this, you can specify
   a maximum number of messages to rescan:

      %RESCAN <Echo Name> <Number of Msgs>

   e.g

      %RESCAN WORKBENCH 100

   A %RESCAN will only send you messages you have not already seen. In
   order to also receive messages that have /already/ been exported to
   your address, you can use the %FORCERESCAN command. This operates in
   the same way as %RESCAN.


7. Remote maintainance

   If the SysOp of this system has granted you full AreaFix control then
   you have access to the following commands.

@rem <PRE>
      &ECHONAME               Create a new echo with the tag name
                              'ECHONAME'.  Your system will not be linked
                              into the new echo.

      ~ECHONAME               Delete the echo with a tag name of 'ECHONAME'

      # OLD_NAME : NEW_NAME   Change an echo's tagname from 'OLD_NAME' to
                              'NEW_NAME'.  The spaces are optional.

      %FROM: 1:234/56.0       Specify that the request comes from
                              1:234/56.0 instead of your system.  Note that
                              the password in the subject line must be
                              correct for your system.
@rem </PRE>


Using AreaFix

AreaFix can be thought of as a robot, waiting on your system for requests 
from users. Each network has its own AreaFix robot. If a user wanted to 
receive a list of echoes which you carry in the FidoNet network, for 
example, they would send a netmail to the `user' "AreaFix" at your FidoNet 
address. In this message, they must put the command "%LIST". The AreaFix is 
password protected however, to ensure that nobody else attaches them to any 
areas they don't want (imagine suddenly one day receiving several megabytes 
of mail when normally you'd only get a few kilobytes - this is the abuse 
that passwords are designed to stop). The users password must be in the 
subject line.

Using AreaFix for remote SysOping

If you are away from your BBS computer, but can stay in touch with it 
(using a laptop with some terminal and pointing software and a mobile phone 
for example), then you can still manage your mail system!

You must first make sure the point address you have set for yourself has 
SysOp access to AreaFix.  Check the relevant section in this chapter on the 
SYSTEM keyword.  Once done, you can perform the changes mentioned in the 
AreaFix help file.

This system may also be useful if your BBS is run by many Co-SysOps. A co-
SysOp can be in charge of a single network's (or more, remember that SysOp 
access can be set per-network) management while not actually needing to be 
at the BBS computer.

Of particular note is the "%FROM:" keyword.  If a point or other downlink 
of the BBS is having trouble fixing into an echo (for example), then you, 
as a SysOp also pointing, can send an AreaFix with that users password from 
your address if you specify "%FROM <their address>".



Setting up Archivers

ZMP reads the Archivers configuration file so it can pack and unpack 
message bundles, which are traditionally archived to save transmission time 
with the Mailer.

When setting up your ZMP configuration you must make sure that all the 
archiver entries have the correct paths to the binaries. These archivers 
are all available from the AmiNet.

@rem <pre>
Archiver Name             AmiNet File

ARC                       util/arc/arc.lha
LHA & LZH                 util/arc/LhA_e138.run
LZX                       util/arc/lzx121.lha
UUENCODE                  util/arc/uuxt31.lha
ZIP                       util/arc/Unzip512x.lha,
                          util/arc/zip201x.lha
ZOO                       util/arc/zoo2-0.lzh
@rem </pre>

The Archivers configuration program can be accessed from the Message-
>Config->Archivers menu on the Control screen, or from the Zeus:Prefs 
drawer.

@{"Archivers Config Picture" Link "ArchiversCfg.gif/main"}

Each archiver has various fields to be configured:

Name@{uu} - The descriptive name of the archiver. This field is the one used in 
your ZMP.cfg to define what archiver a particular node uses. It should not 
have spaces in it.

Compress@{uu} - The line is expanded to make the command line used to compress 
one or more files into this archive type. This line takes expansion codes 
~a and ~f, e.g. if we were packing a file called 00003445.pkt into a file 
called OUTBOUND:2.888.1.0.LHA, the compress line would be expanded to 
"c:LHA -2 -a a OUTBOUND:2.881.1.0.LHA 00003445.pkt".

Extract@{uu} - This line is expanded to make the archiver extract one or more 
files from an archive. This line takes expansion codes ~a and ~f, where ~a 
is the archive name to extract and ~f is an optional argument to extract 
one or more single files. If all the files are to be extracted then the 
program will not use the ~f argument.

Test@{uu} - This line is expanded to make the archiver test the archive. This 
line only takes the ~a expansion code. Zeus detects whether the file tested 
OK by checking the result code of the archiver. If the result code is 0 
then it is assumed that the archive is not corrupted.

Recog@{uu} - This line is a hex string used to detect whether an archive to 
extract is of this type. For example all LHA archives start with two random 
characters and then the string "-lh5-". We represent this with the line "** 
** 2D 6C 68 35 2D", where ** compares to any single character.

Show User@{uu} - This flag determines whether the archiver will be able to be 
viewed by an on-line user accessing the archivers configuration, for 
example within Phoenix.  Archivers such as "UUENCODE" and "ZGATE" should 
not have this flag set off.



Using ZMP

Finally, once ZMP is configured, you can use the program.

@rem <pre>
Zeus Bulletin Board System (c)1992-1999 Zeus Developments
Zeus FTN Net/EchoMail, Tick & Hatch Processor v1.2

Usage: ZMP IMPORT/S,EXPORT/S,FORCE/S,TICK/S,HATCH/S,AREA/K,
           FILE/K,DESC/K,REPLACE/K,DELETE/S,BATCH/K

   IMPORT  - Dearchive and import mail
   EXPORT  - Scan message areas and pack new messages
   TICK    - Process *.TIC files
   HATCH   - Hatch a file into a TICK echo
   BATCH   - Perform a batch file hatch

   FORCE   - Force packing of all messages

HATCH options:

   AREA    - Tick tag name to hatch file to
   FILE    - Filename of file to hatch
   DESC    - File description
   REPLACE - File to be replaced
   DELETE  - Delete original file

BATCH options:

   DELETE  - Delete batch file once done
@rem </pre>


Most of the commands are quite descriptive themselves; to import your 
echomail using the option "Import" in the command line, to export use 
"Export".  Many of the option can be combined on one command line, so "ZMP 
Import Export" is perfectly valid and actually recommended, as it means the 
configuration needs to be read and saved only once.

The Hatch command (to create and export a Tick file) behaves a little 
differently.  If you specify just "ZMP Hatch", ZMP will go into interactive 
mode and request from you the information it needs to build a Tick file and 
export it.
Alternately, you can use the "Hatch options" as shown above: in this case, 
you specify the options required by ZMP on the command line and ZMP does 
not ask for them interactively.  Since these are keywords (/K), you might 
do this:

@rem <pre>
ZMP Hatch Area="NEW_GAMES" File="Inbound:3D_Fighter.lzx" Desc="New 3D 
fighting game" Replace="" Delete
@rem </pre>

Since in this case since I'm pointing to a file in my Inbound directory, I 
wish to delete it.  ZMP will be add the file to my file base in the area 
which attached to the NEW_GAMES Tick echo, so I have no need to keep it in 
the Inbound: directory.


The ZMP Batch command lets you specify much the same thing, except the 
Hatch options are fetched from a file, and there can be more than one file 
Hatched at a time.

The Batch file (which you specify after the Batch command, i.e. "ZMP 
Batch=RAM:Batches.txt") is of the format:

@rem <pre>
Tick Area1
File1
Description1
File to Replace1
Delete1 (Y/N)
Tick Area2
File2
Description2
File to Replace2
Delete2 (Y/N)
@rem </pre>

So, each Hatch entry follows directly on from the last, and each element is 
placed on a new line.  If any entry has no option, like the Replace option 
for example, leave a blank line.  The Delete option is a single character, 
either Y for Yes or N for No.

For example, the Batch file may look like:

@rem <pre>
NEW_GAMES
Inbound:3D_Fighter.lzx
New 3D fighting game

Y
@rem </pre>

And the command line used for ZMP would be:

@rem <pre>
ZMP Batch=RAM:Batches.txt Delete
@rem </pre>

In this case, Delete on the ZMP command line means to delete the 
Batches.txt file.


In the most simplistic usage, you merely call ZMP to import and export your 
echo mail, and would have this in an "After session" script for ZMailer:

@rem <pre>
ZMP Import Export
@rem </pre>

If you also receive Tick files (such as nodelists) then you should also add 
Tick to the options.

@rem <pre>
ZMP Import Export Tick
@rem </pre>



ZMP's Construction of a Tick file

It may be useful for you to know how ZMP refers to and treats the file when 
making a Tick.

If the Tick area is passthrough: ZMP will make a copy of the file given, 
and place the copy in the Outbound directory.  This copy in the Outbound is 
the file ZMP's Tick (for every downlink) refers to.
If the Tick area is a file area on the BBS: ZMP will make a copy of the 
file given, and place the copy in the path of the file area.  This is the 
copy which is refered to in the Tick file.

In both cases, the original of the file is no longer required by Zeus. In 
the case of the non-passthrough area, ZMP should respect the MultiPaths of 
the file area.



ZMP Statistics

ZMP keeps a file which contains statistics on what has been imported from 
networks.  The file contains:

· The total number of items imported into that area since the area was 
first created.
· The number of items imported each day for the last week.
· The size of items imported each day for the last week.

The statistics can be accessed with the Bin/ZMPStat program.

@rem <PRE>
Zeus Bulletin Board System (c)1992-1999 Zeus Developments
Network Statistics Utility - Written by Neil Williams

Usage: ZMPStat 
AREA/N/K,RANGE/K,ALL/S,FILES/S,ITEMS/S,BYTES/S,GEN=GENERAL/S,TAG/K,SUM=SUMMARY/S

   AREA        - Specify one area to act upon
   RANGE       - Specify a range of areas to act upon
   ALL         - Act upon all message areas
@rem </PRE>

You run ZMPStat on areas in much the same way as mbManager, using the 
familiar Area, Range and All parameters.  To run ZMPStat on a single area, 
you can also specify the area by its tag name using the TAG keyword: 
"ZMPStat Tag=AMIGA_UK"

Running ZMPStat on an area now will output something similar to:

@rem <PRE>
Zeus Bulletin Board System (c)1992-1999 Zeus Developments
Network Statistics Utility - Written by Neil Williams

.--------------------------------------------------------------------------
.
|        Thu   |   Fri   |   Sat   |   Sun   |   Mon   |   Tue   |  Today  
|
+--------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
| AMIGA_UK                    Amiga UK                       [   409] Msg  
|
|                                                                          
|
`--------------------------------------------------------------------------
'
@rem </PRE>

This doesn't show any statistics however.  We must use other options to 
tell ZMPStat what we want to see.

Files@{uu}

Specify this option to see Tick echoes not message echoes.  You cannot show 
both message and tick echoes at the same time.

Items@{uu}

Show the number of items (messages or tick files) imported in this area 
over the last 7 days.

@rem <PRE>
| AMIGA_UK                    Amiga UK                       [   409] Msg  
|
|                                                                          
|
|Items       4 |       6 |      16 |       9 |       3 |       6 |      11 
|
@rem </PRE>

Bytes@{uu}

Shows the bytes (of messages or files) imported over the last 7 days.

@rem <PRE>
| AMIGA_UK                    Amiga UK                       [   409] Msg  
|
|                                                                          
|
|Bytes    4134 |   12152 |   18824 |   12049 |    2720 |    6906 |   16468 
|
@rem </PRE>

General@{uu}

Shows the total number of items imported ever, and the total size in bytes 
of items imported this week only.

@rem <PRE>
| AMIGA_UK                    Amiga UK                       [   409] Msg  
|
| Total message imported:       99   -   Total bytes this week:    73253   
|
@rem </PRE>


Items, Bytes and General can be combined:

@rem <PRE>
> ZMPStat Area 409 Gen Items Bytes

.--------------------------------------------------------------------------
.
|        Thu   |   Fri   |   Sat   |   Sun   |   Mon   |   Tue   |  Today  
|
+--------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
| AMIGA_UK                    Amiga UK                       [   409] Msg  
|
| Total message imported:       99   -   Total bytes this week:    73253   
|
|                                                                          
|
|Items       4 |       6 |      16 |       9 |       3 |       6 |      11 
|
|Bytes    4134 |   12152 |   18824 |   12049 |    2720 |    6906 |   16468 
|
`--------------------------------------------------------------------------
'
@rem </PRE>


Note that ZMPStat will only show the header (day titles) once when a range 
of areas is shown.

Summary@{uu}

When a range of areas are being displayed, it can be useful to see them in 
summary.  This option will add extra lines on the end of the statistics 
output showing the totals of all areas listed.



Error Handling

Should something go wrong while importing or exporting mail, ZMP will 
attempt to rescue data and, if possible, carry on working.  It will also 
'bail out' safely if it cannot continue working.  This is a detailed, if 
somewhat incomplete description of what ZMP may do in case of an error:

· An inbound mail bundle (archived) fails to extract:
ZMP will rename the bundle with a ".BAD" extension and set its file 
note/comment to the error which was encountered.  It will then attempt to 
import whatever packets were extracted, dealing with any that may be 
corrupted as normal.

· An inbound packet is corrupted:
ZMP will import the messages contained in the packet up to the point of 
corruption.  If the corruption is easily detectable (e.g., a mangled 
message header) ZMP will stop importing that packet, and rename it with a 
".BAD" extension.  The file comment will be set to a description of the 
error encountered.

· A crash occours during export, leaving packets and bundles partially 
filled:
ZMP will export all the messages again, on the idea that it's better to 
have some duplicate messages go out (which will most likely be filtered out 
by the downlinks) than for mail to go missing.  Partially completed bundles 
(perhaps caused by a crash during or after archiving) may have an extension 
representing the archiver's normal extension.  In case this is a valid 
bundle, Zeus will rename it (so other archiving can take place) and place a 
message in the log file to notify you of the presence of the 'unattached' 
(to a FLO, HLO, etc.) bundle - "WARNING: Unattached bundle found in 
Outbound; renamed to <new name>".

· An inbound packet arrives which is not addressed to any of your 
addresses/AKAs:
The packet is treated as corrupt; it is renamed with a ".BAD" extension and 
the file comment set to describe the fault.



@endnode



@node "Manual.Internet" "Internet"


Chapter 9 - The Internet

Introduction

Types of InterNet account

There are currently two widely used types of InterNet account: static and 
dynamic.  Static accounts provide you with your own IP address, domain name 
and multiple mail boxes.  Dynamic accounts allocate a new IP address for 
you every time you connect to your provider, have only one mailbox and 
usually a 'firewall' stopping outside access to your system using Telnet, 
FTP and other InterNet protocols.

Zeus and the InterNet

Zeus comes with six utilities to help interface your BBS to the InterNet:

@rem <PRE>
* Zeus:Bin/System/NetStart
@rem </PRE>

Telnet is a very simple InterNet protocol which sends and receives text 
between a host and a client, much like a normal BBS session, except the 
data is divided up into packets and transfered over the net instead of a 
normal dialup connection.

Because of this similarity, several Telnet daemons have been written which 
allow Amiga terminal and BBS packages to interface to Telnet by opening a 
faked serial.device.

Some Telnet daemons allow multiple incoming sessions by allocating a new 
unit number to every connection and spawning an external program such as 
NetStart.

Telnet daemons will normally only work well with static InterNet accounts, 
although so called "Dynamic DNS" services are available which will assign 
you a hostname which you can update immediatly with whatever IP address you 
have been assigned for that particular call.

@rem <PRE>
* Zeus:Bin/ZGate
@rem </PRE>

ZGate converts Email into Netmail, UseNet newsgroups into Echomail and vice 
versa.  You will need to have a working FidoNet-technology mail processor 
setup (preferably ZMP) although you do not need to be a member of any 
external network if you only wish the mail and news to appear on your BBS.

ZGate will work fine with dynamic InterNet accounts but, if you want to 
give your users E-Mail access or run a private/public gate, a static 
account is preferred.  Recently, ISPs have begun offering dynamic IPs with 
subdomains.  For the purposes of this manual, these can be considered 
static accounts.

@rem <PRE>
* Zeus:Rexx/AmiNetZAmiMirror.rexx
@rem </PRE>

The Aminet archive is the biggest collection of non-commercial Amiga 
software and information in the world.  It is accessable on the InterNet 
using FTP from several sites and is constantly being added to by the Amiga 
community.

ZAmiMirror is an ARexx script that downloads the latest files from the 
archive and uploads them to file areas on your BBS.

ZAmiMirror will work with both static and dynamic InterNet accounts.

@rem <PRE>
* ZPOP3Xfer
@rem </PRE>

ZPOP3Xfer transfers messages using the POP3 Internet mailbox format from 
the Internet to your hard drive, ready for processing with ZGate.

@rem <PRE>
* ZSMTPXfer
@rem </PRE>

ZSMTPXfer transfers messages using SMTP to and from the Internet.

@rem <PRE>
* ZNewsXfer
@rem </PRE>

ZNewsXfer transfers messages with the NNTP protocol from the Internet to a 
file on your hard drive, ready for use with ZGate.


InterNet Configuration

This is where Zeus's Internet support is set up.  Most options relate to 
ZGate, but can be used by other programs so please fill it in even if you 
use another gateway program.

@{"Internet Configuration Window" Link "InternetConfig.gif/main"}

User Name@{uu} - If you have a "dynamic" InterNet account you must enter your 
mailbox name. Example If your mail address is zeusbbs@net.com then set this 
field to zeusbbs. If you have a static account, this field MUST contain 
nothing.

Address@{uu} - Your InterNet address.  If you have a dynamic account this will 
be the address of your InterNet provider. Example If your mail address is 
zeusbbs@net.com, you would enter net.com.  If you have a "static" account, 
this must be the full domain assigned to you,for example: with <anything>@t
kgbbs.freeserve.co.uk you would put "tkgbbs.freeserve.co.uk" without the 
quotes.

Organization@{uu} - Your organization, if you have one, or your BBS name if you 
wish.  If this field is left blank, it will not be used.

News Receipt@{uu} - Some InterNet news systems require that new articles be 
submitted by Email.  If your provider works like this then enter the 
destination e-mail address for newsgroup messages here.

Save Config@{uu} - Saves the configuration.  If Zeus is running, it will also be 
updated.

Close Gadget@{uu} - Quits the program. If changes have been made and you have 
not saved them, a requester will appear asking if you wish to save.



Options Window

@{"Internet Options Window" Link "InternetOptions.gif/main"}

Gate Type@{uu} - Sets the kind of gate you wish to run

Local@{uu} - Only messages written on your BBS will be gated.  Any messages 
comming from other systems will be bounced back informing them of the 
situation.

Private@{uu} - Messages will only be gated if they came from your BBS or from a 
system listed in the 'Gate Access' list AND the user has be specified in 
the 'User Mapping' configuration.

Indexes@{uu} - ZGate keeps an index file of information about each mail and 
newsgroup article that it processes.  By specifying a low number into this 
field (minimum of 100) you will save disk space but ZGate's duplicate 
message detection and message linking functions will not work very well.  
As a guide, you should set this field to the approximate number of InterNet 
messages you process in one week, but if this is a low number (less than 
1000 is low) it is perfectly okay to increase it.  Many of ZGate's advanced 
features depend on this database.


Approximate Disk Usage@{uu}:

@rem <PRE>
100 Indexes (Minimum)
1600 bytes (1.5kb)

1000 Indexes
16000 bytes (15.6kb)

10000 Indexes
160000 bytes (156.2kb)

1000000 Indexes
16000000 bytes (1.5mb)
@rem </PRE>

Your Addr@{uu} - Your FidoNet address.

Gate Addr@{uu} - The FidoNet address you have given to your InterNet gate.  This 
must also be set up in ZMP.cfg (as detailed below) or whatever FidoNet 
message processor you are using.  Usually this is a point on your BBS, not 
a fake node, to simplifiy things.

Domain@{uu} - Usually this should be set to 'fidonet.org'.  If your ISP will 
send you 'subdomained' e-mails, this can be your address.  Please leave as 
fidonet.org if you are not completely sure of the Internet or gating 
technology involved.

Time Zone@{uu} - Your time zone.

GMT Offset@{uu} - The difference in hours from GMT to your time zone.

Gate Access@{uu} - If 'Gate Type' is set to Private or Public then this list 
becomes active.  It specifies the systems that may access your gate.  
Entries are of the form [+ or -]zone:net/node.point :  + to give access or -
 to deny, followed by a standard FidoNet address.  You can use the wildcard 
character '*' for any of Zone, Net, Node or Point to allow multiple systems 
access without having to type all the addresses in.



Examples:

+2:441/58.72

 - Allow this point access.

+2:441/58.*

 - Allow all my points access.

+2:441/*.*

 - Allow all the nodes and points in net 441.

+2:441/*.*

 - Allow all nodes/points in net 441

-2:441/59.*

 - except node 59 and all his points.


By default, no systems have access so you don't need a entry of '-*:*/*.*'.

Add@{uu}: Add a new gate access entry.

Del@{uu}: Deletes the currently selected access entry.



Paths & Commands Window

@{"Internet Commands Window" Link "InternetCommands.gif/main"}

News Dir@{uu} -  The directory which contains your newsgroup hierachy. This is 
usually 'UUNEWS:'.

Temp Dir@{uu} -  A directory where temporary files will be stored.  You should 
set this to 'T:' to speed up program execution.

Spool Dir@{uu} -  The directory where spool files are written.  Usually 
'UUSPOOL:'.

Mail Dir@{uu} -  The directory which contains your mail boxes.  Usually 
'UUMAIL:'.



The next three fields are used by ZGate to interface to your InterNet email 
and news setup.  See further on for example configurations.

Send News@{uu} -  Full path and command line of the program you use to post news 
articles to the InterNet.

Send Mail@{uu} -  Full path and command line of the program you use to post 
email to the InterNet.

RNews@{uu} -  Full path and command line of the program you use to process 
incoming news articles.



Headers Window

@{"Internet Headers Window" Link "InternetHeaders.gif/main"}

Email and news articles contain many header lines of which only a few are 
actually needed by ZGate.  By default, ZGate reads its information from the 
header lines and then discards them.  If you would like certain header 
lines to be included in the message text then you can specify which ones in 
this window.

Add@{uu} - Adds a new header line entry.  If you only enter the beginning of a 
header line, ZGate will include all lines which begin with what you have 
entered.

@rem <PRE>
Examples:

From:
 - Will match 'From:'

From
 - Will match From:, Fromage:, etc..

X-
 - Will match all lines beginning with 'X-
@rem </PRE>

Delete@{uu} - Deletes the currently selected entry.

Port/Kludge@{uu} - Specifies how the currently selected header line will be 
added to the message text.

 Port@{uu} - The line will appear at the top of the message text

 Kludge@{uu} - The line will be hidden behind a FidoNet ^A kludge.


ZGate

ZGate is basically a conversion utility that sits between your current 
InterNet setup and your BBS mail processor.  It was designed like this to 
give a lot of flexibility in how email and newsgroup articles are 
processed.  For example:

The simplest use of ZGate is to convert email and articles to a form which 
can be imported into your BBS message areas using any FidoNet mail 
processor (although ZMP is preferred!).  This gives read only access to the 
InterNet for you and your users.

ZGate can of course work the other way: taking new messages from the BBS 
and converting them to emails and articles ready to be sent onto the 
InterNet.

Because ZGate converts messages to and from FidoNet packets, you can also 
link your points or other node systems into the areas, thus giving them 
access as well.

Please note that some InterNet providers will charge you extra if you want 
to start gating to other systems.

From version 1.5, ZGate also includes the ability to act as a simple 
mailing list server. Any messages in specified Fido echoes are gated to 
email and sent to a list of Internet email addresses.

And finally, if you wanted to keep emails and articles in InterNet format, 
maybe for feeding to a local network over NNTP or SMTP, ZGate can convert 
the messages to FidoNet format but won't delete the original files.

ZGate keeps two index files containing information from every email and 
newsgroup article it processes.  Because of this, it has duplicate message 
detection, advanced message linking abilities and the unique ability to 
provide UseNet with correct To: and From: fields, just like FidoNet echoes!


Before continuing, please make sure you have now read about and understand 
the "Netmail Mapper" in Chapter 7, @{"Messages" link "Manual.Messages"}, as this is also the heart of 
ZGate's e-mail address mapping system.


@rem <PRE>
Zeus Bulletin Board System (c)1992-2002 Zeus Developments
InterNet <-> Zeus <-> FidoNet News/Email Gating Utility v1.5
Written by Alex May and Neil Williams

Usage: ZGate 
GATEMAIL/K,MAILBOX/K,GATEPKT/K,GATENEWS/K,GATEBATCH/K,POSTNEWS/S,DELETE/S,MBHDR/K,NWHDR/K,XTRACT/S

   GATEMAIL  - Convert single Email to .PKT
   MAILBOX   - Convert mailbox to .PKT
   GATEPKT   - Convert .PKT to Email/News
   GATENEWS  - Convert single article to .PKT
   GATEBATCH - Convert RNews news batch file to .PKT
   POSTNEWS  - Convert article on stdin to .PKT

   DELETE    - Delete original files after conversion
@rem <PRE>


InterNet E-Mail to Zeus

When emails are transfered from your InterNet mail host to your system, 
they end up as either mailbox files or individual messages depending on 
what software you use.  ZGate takes either format and converts them to 
FidoNet netmail packets in your inbound directory ready to be imported onto 
your BBS.

To convert a single email message, you use the GATEMAIL option with ZGate:

@rem <PRE>
CLI> ZGate GATEMAIL T:NewMsg.tmp [DELETE]
@rem </PRE>

The DELETE option tells ZGate to delete the email message if it was 
successful in converting it.

Converting a mailbox is equally as simple: just use the MAILBOX option:

@rem <PRE>
CLI> ZGate MAILBOX UUMail:zeus [DELETE]
@rem </PRE>


Zeus -> InterNet E-Mail

To send an email from your BBS, you need to write a netmail with the To: 
field set to the receipients email address (OR *IMPORTANT* - the e-mail 
address sometimes will not fit in the To: field, so instead write their 
name in To: and write the first line of your message as "To: <address>" - 
you can automate this in your scriptset), and the destination FidoNet 
address set to the gate address you specified in the InterNet config.  When 
you export the message, the mail processor will create a FidoNet packet in 
your outbound which can then be imported by ZGate using the GATEPKT option:

@rem <PRE>
CLI> ZGate GATEPKT Outbound:00000000.PKT [DELETE]
@rem </PRE>

This process can (and in all honesty, must) be automated by setting up 
ZGate as an archiver!  If you are using ZMP then add the following entry 
into Prefs/Archivers:

@rem <PRE>
   Name    : ZGATE
   Compress: Zeus:Bin/ZGate GATEPKT ~f
   Extract : <Leave blank>
   Recog   : <Leave blank>
@rem </PRE>

In your ZMP.cfg, setup the gate SYSTEM like so:

@rem <PRE>
   SYSTEM .1 N3NNNNNBY GATE -NONE- -NONE- -NONE- <Group Numbers...>
@rem </PRE>

This example sets the gate address to .1, the archive netmail flag on, and 
the archiver type to GATE.  Now, when ZMP comes to archive some messages 
destined for the gate address, it automatically calls ZGate which processes 
the packet!

If you now let users know that you have an Internet gateway on (in this 
example) your address at point 1, they can then send and receive e-mail, 
using the same methods as other gateway programs.  Ensure they know to 
write the e-mail address on the first line of their message using "To: 
<address>".  Many FidoNet programs can take care of this themselves, since 
it is the standard used all over FidoNet.


Newsgroup Configuration

The InterNet configuration gives ZGate enough information to import and 
export email but, if you want it to process newsgroups, it needs a text 
config file, named Zeus:Config/ZGate.cfg, which defines the groups you want 
to gate and how they should be handled. ZGate.CFG also allows you to 
seperate Mailing Lists from your using E-Mail. The mailing lists appear on 
the BBS in their own area, just like a gated newsgroup.

Since newsgroup tag names are usually quite long, ZGate supports mapping 
between newsgroup tag names and FidoNet tag names.  ZMP will happily 
process tags up to sixty characters long but other mail processing software 
may have problems.  If you will be gating newsgroups to other systems, or 
using a mail processor other than ZMP, you will probably need to define a 
shorter FidoNet tag for all your newsgroups.

A number of newsgroups have restrictions on who may post articles to them.  
Some require that all new articles be sent to a moderator as email. If the 
moderator thinks that the article is relevant to the newsgroup, they will 
post it for you.

Other newsgroups have a matching discussion group where people reply and 
talk about the articles in the main group.  These newsgroups use the same 
tag name with '.d' appended to signify a discussion group.

ZGate supports both of these restriction systems automatically once the 
relevant information has been entered into the ZGate.cfg file.  Here is an 
example of what the config looks like:

@rem <PRE>
;
; An example Zeus:Config/ZGate.cfg
;

;     <InterNet Tag> <FidoNet Tag>  [Moderator/Followup]

AREA  zeus.announce  ZEUS_ANNOUNCE  zeus@pavilion.co.uk   ; Moderated
AREA  zeus.news      ZEUS_NEWS      zeus.news.d           ; Followup
AREA  zeus.news.d    ZEUS_NEWSD                           ; Normal Group


; Generic Newsgroups list

; List of newsgroups, like above AREA definitions, but in an external file 
in
; either 'active' format, or one group name per line. As such, no mapping 
of
; newsgroups to different echo names or setting different 
moderator/followup
; addresses.

;         <Filename>

XGROUPS   UULIB:active

; Mailing lists

; For the 'origin of list', you should put the address which his in the
; To: or Reply-To: field of the E-Mails.

; It will also match on the From: address, allowing for strange mailing
; list server configurations and also allowing you to 'filter' mails
; from specific addresses into seperate echoes even if they are not
; mailing lists.

; You can have more than one LIST entry if it is necessary to match
; several different addresses as being the same mailing list. But,
; the first LIST entry for a particular echo name will be where new
; messages from the BBS are sent to.

;    <Address>                <Our echo>      <Name subscribed with>

LIST amterm@vapor.com         TKG_ML_AMTERM   neil

; Hosted mailing lists - effectively, ZGate is a mailing list server
; like a primitive majordomo. "File" is either a file in Zeus:Config/
; or you can give a complete path.
; "Name of List" is <name>@your.bbs.com, where subscribers send messages
; to and messages come from.
; add PUBLIC to the end if you want to allow anyone to post there, if they
; know the list name/address. Recommended, as this eliminates expensive
; checking of the subscribers file, for _every_ message received for this
; list.

;    <File with List of Subscribers> <Echo to Gate to> <Name of List> 
[PUBLIC]

HOSTEDLIST ZGate_testlist.txt        ZGATE_TEST        zgate.test     
PUBLIC
HOSTEDLIST ZGate_TKG.txt             TKG               tkg

; End of example
@rem </PRE>

As the example shows, you can place comments in the config by preceding 
them with a semi-colon ';'.  ZGate will also ignore blank lines.

The AREA keyword describes a single newsgroup and has the template:

@rem <PRE>
AREA <InterNet Tag> <FidoNet Tag> [Moderator/Followup]
@rem </PRE>

InterNet Tag@{uu} - The tag name of the newsgroup

FidoNet Tag@{uu}  - The tag name to use in FidoNet packets

Moderator/Followup@{uu} - If this argument is present, it specifies either a 
moderators email address to which all new articles will be posted, or a 
follow-up discussion newsgroup tag name.  ZGate decides which type of 
argument you have given by checking for a '@' character.  If it finds one, 
it assumes an email address.



To save adding all your newsgroups in by hand, you can use the XGROUPS 
keyword to specify a file which contains the groups you want ZGate to 
process.  The format of this file is simple:  one newsgroup tag per line 
like this:

@rem <PRE>
   zeus.announce
   zeus.news
   zeus.news.d
   <etc. etc.>
@rem </PRE>

Several external newsgroup processing utilities use a similar file format 
(called the "active" file, often in an assign called "uunews:") which, 
since ZGate doesn't change the file in any way, you can 'share' it between 
them.

Some utilities append other information after the newsgroup name in these 
files, like high and low message pointers, which makes the file look like 
this:

@rem <PRE>
   zeus.announce 1482 1266 n
   zeus.news 8273 6350 n
   zeus.news.d 17623 8232 n
   <etc. etc.>
@rem </PRE>

ZGate can still read this format since it only takes the first part of each 
line, stopping at any space or tab character.

The disadvantage of using the XGROUPS keyword is that no FidoNet tag 
mapping or moderator/followup information is present.  For the example, it 
is equivilent to the following AREA lines:

@rem <PRE>
   AREA zeus.announce zeus.announce
   AREA zeus.news     zeus.news
   AREA zeus.news.d   zeus.news.d
@rem </PRE>

So, if you want tag name mapping for all your newsgroups, you will still 
have to enter them all in as AREA lines.  If, however, you don't want to 
use tag name mapping, then you only need to use AREA lines for groups that 
have a moderator or follow-up newsgroup.


Mailing Lists - as a subscriber

Using LIST, any e-mail which arrives can be checked to see if it came from 
a mailing list you have subscribed to, and if it has, to seperate those 
messages into a specific EchoMail Area for either just yourself or your 
users.

The format is:
LIST <Address>   <Our echo>   <Name subscribed with>

Address@{uu} -- The address to which e-mail for the mailing list will be sent, 
and also the address which tells ZGate that a certain message has arrived 
from a particular mailing list.
On incoming mail, this address is compared to that which is in the "To:" or 
"Reply-To:" line of incoming e-mail.

Echo@{uu} -- The echo tag on the BBS to place the messages. If ZGate's SYSTEM 
line in the ZMP configuration is set to create areas, it will be auto-
created on the first message processed.

Name@{uu} -- This is the name you subscribed with.  It is useful to subscribe 
with a unique name if you have a static account, but not necessary.  The 
rest of the address (name@<address>) must be what is configured in the 
Internet preferences - you cannot, for example, run list1@hotmail.com, list2
@bigfoot.com.  This is the same elsewhere in the gateway system.

Sometimes, you may find the "To:" line of mailing lists changes sometimes, 
depending on which server is used at the mailing list home.  This is the 
case with the Miami mailing lists.  To get around this, it is perfectly 
possible to use two near identical LIST lines:

@rem <pre>
LIST miami-announce-ml@nordicglobal.com      TKG_ML_MIAMI_ANN     neil
LIST miami-announce-ml@h1.nordicglobal.com   TKG_ML_MIAMI_ANN     neil
@rem </pre>

In this case, all out going messages are correctly addressed to "miami-
announce-ml@nordicglobal.com", but incoming messages can also come from 
"miami-announce-ml@h1.nordicglobal.com" and correctly arrive in the MIAMI 
echo.


Mailing Lists - as a server

The HOSTEDLIST line in ZGate.CFG allows us to create a more powerful form 
of mailing list - one where we server emails out to users. In this case, a 
FidoNet echo is assigned an email address in our domain (you must have the 
ability to have multiple addresses under your domain).

For example, area ZGATE_TEST has the email address zgate.test in the 
example configuration - when conbined with our domain this might give 
zgate.test@bbs.com. Now, whenever ZGate imports an email addressed to 
zgate.test, it will send it to this echo. Much like with a standard LIST, 
but in this case we don't filter on the "To:" line of the email.

@rem <pre>
HOSTEDLIST ZGate_testlist.txt        ZGATE_TEST        zgate.test     
PUBLIC
HOSTEDLIST ZGate_TKG.txt             TKG               tkg
@rem </pre>

However, we can filter on the "From:" line - in the ZGATE_TEST example, 
anyone can send a message to zgate.test and it will be gated. In the TKG 
example above, you'll notice it is missing the PUBLIC argument. This means 
that only the email addresses listed in the file 
"Zeus:Config/ZGate_TKG.txt" will be allowed to email messages into the 
echo.

This also works in the other direction; the more useful aspect of this. 
When a message is posted in ZGATE_TEST, it is sent to every email address 
in ZGate_testlist.txt. Similarly for the TKG echo.


Newsgroups to Zeus

When transfering newsgroup articles from the InterNet to your machine, some 
software will create a batch file containing all the articles (often called 
the "RNews" format) whereas others store them as individual files 
(ZNewsXfer, below, uses the batch file format).  The batch method is faster 
and less system intensive, but ZGate will handle both formats.

To convert a batch file you use the GATEBATCH option:

@rem <PRE>
CLI> ZGate GATEBATCH T:News.batch [DELETE]
@rem </PRE>

To convert a single newsgroup article, you use the GATENEWS option:

@rem <PRE>
CLI> ZGate GATENEWS T:News.article [DELETE]
@rem </PRE>

Some InterNet software doesn't store the article in a file but instead, 
'feeds' it over stdio.  ZGate has the option POSTNEWS for this type of 
situation.

@rem <PRE>
CLI> ZGate POSTNEWS <T:News.article
@rem </PRE>



Zeus to Newsgroups

Because Zeus generates the new articles as echomail, the setup for 
processing is the same for new email.  See section 3.2.



Example Configurations

This section will explain in detail how to setup ZGate with several of the 
available email and newsgroup transfering packages around. It assumes that 
you already have the InterNet software installed and working.

INetUtils 1.4 for AmiTCP/AS225r2



ZGate to InterNet

Although email will be stored in a temporary spool directory, news will 
not.  If you have a direct link to the InterNet or if you are running ZGate 
manually while connected, use these options:

@rem <PRE>
   Prefs/InterNet - Paths:

      Send News: NNTPpost ~f
      Send Mail: SMTPpost ~f
@rem </PRE>

Otherwise you can implement a simple version of news spooling by creating 
the directory 'Mail:NewsSpool' and using the following script:

@rem <PRE>
;
; AmiTCP:SendNews/StoreNews.script
;

.key NEWSFILE/A
.bra {
.ket }

Copy {NEWSFILE} Mail:NewsSpool/
Echo >>Mail:NewsSpool/SendNews.script "NNTPpost Mail:NewsSpool/{NEWSFILE} -
x Mail:NewsSpool/{NEWSFILE}"
Delete {NEWSFILE}

; End of script
@rem </PRE>

Then you need to set the following in the InterNet configuration:

Prefs/InterNet - Paths:

@rem <PRE>
      Send News: Execute Mail:NewsSpool/StoreNews.script ~f
      Send Mail: SMTPpost -Q ~f
@rem </PRE>

This will create a script called 'Mail:NewsSpool/SendNews.script' which you 
should execute when you are next connected to the InterNet.  It will call 
NNTPpost for each spooled news article which in turn will post the message 
and then delete it if everything went well.



InterNet to ZGate

To make NNTPxfer use ZGate to process incoming news articles, add this line 
to your S:User-Startup

@rem <PRE>
   SetEnv NNTPRNews Zeus:Bin/ZGate POSTNEWS
@rem </PRE>

Incoming email is best processed with SMTPpost to deliver it to the mailbox 
directory.  Once all mail has been transfered, run this script to make 
ZGate convert and delete all the mailboxes:

@rem <PRE>
.key MAILBOXDIR/A
.bra {
.ket }

List {MAILBOXDIR} FILES LFORMAT="Zeus:Bin/ZGate MAILBOX %s%s DELETE" 
>T:Temp
Execute T:Temp
Delete T:Temp
@rem </PRE>

Gate Access:  If 'Gate Type' is set to Private or Public then this list 
becomes active.  It specifies the systems that may access your gate.  
Entries are of the form:


ZGate's Return Codes

ZGate sends a return code to the CLI to indicate how well the gating 
process went, so that you can handle any special situation in a calling CLI 
script.

@rem <PRE>
   0  -  All went well.
  20  -  Failure loading ZGate indexes - mostly likely out of memory.
@rem </PRE>

In the case of 20, you can set your script to delete the indexes to make 
them small enough to again fit in memory, or launch a program to analyse 
memory fragmentation in case the computer requires a reboot.



ZAmiMirror


What is Aminet?

Only the largest collection of Amiga software, data and information in the 
world!  It lives on the Internet, so, if you don't have an InterNet 
account, this program will not be of much use to you.

There are a wide range of files which are stored in a hierarchical 
directory tree by the (nice) people who administrate the Aminet.  These are 
available for free and, since it usually contains all the latest public 
domain and shareware products, it is very popular with Amiga owners.


What is an Aminet Mirror?

The Aminet exists on several systems throughout the InterNet.  These are 
called Mirrors since each machine has an identical copy of all the files 
contained on all the other Aminet mirror systems.  Since so many people 
access the Aminet everyday, one single system would not be able to cope.

Another advantage to having several sites is that you can connect to a 
system 'near' you and get faster TCP/IP responses than if you were having 
to drag data from the other side of the world.

Using this program, your Zeus system can become an Aminet 'mini-mirror', 
taking in only a few directories, or a full blown mirror, taking in every 
directory.  Obviously the choice comes down to storage space and how much 
time you actually want to spend logged into the InterNet.  If you have a 
64k ISDN line linked to the InterNet 24 hours a day and a 15Gb hard drive, 
then you might want to go the whole hog...


How does ZAmiMirror.rexx work?

It interfaces to the InterNet using the File Transfer Protocol (FTP) client 
NcFTP which comes as part of the AmiTCP package, but is available from the 
Aminet (of course).  ZAmiMirror has not been tried with any other FTP 
packages as they do not have the features needed by this program.

Basically, it works like this:

 - Download a list of the new files available
 - Scan through the list, noting which files are wanted.
 - Download the files.
 - If the files have been downloaded successfully, upload them to the 
matching Zeus BBS file areas.


Setting up ZAmiMirror

1. This is not a simple task but by following the steps below you should 
not have any problems.  If you do encounter problems with the FTP side of 
things and are not confident with NcFTP, you should seek help from someone 
who is.

2. First of all you need a working InterNet setup.  If you haven't got it 
working properly yet then stop reading this and go sort it out!

3. Next you need to find the closest reliable Aminet mirror.  For the 
purposes of this document we shall use the Imperial College's site in the 
UK - its address is sunsite.doc.ic.ac.uk.

4. Decide where on your system you want to store your Aminet mirror.  Make 
a directory there called 'Aminet'.  We shall use 'DH3:' as an example.

5. Load up Zeus:Rexx/ZAmiMirror.rexx into your text editor and take a look 
at the user variable section.  It looks something like this:

@rem <PRE>
 Base     = 'DH3:Aminet/'
 AmiAddr  = 'sunsite.doc.ic.ac.uk'
 SysAddr  = 'phoenix.doc.ic.ac.uk'
 AmiDir   = 'pub/aminet'
 MaxSize  = 1024000
 RecName  = 'ADT_RECENT_7'
 LogFile  = 'Logs:ZAmiMirror.log'
 FullTree = 'No'
@rem </PRE>

6. Edit the 'Base' line to reflect where your Aminet directory is.  Don't 
forget the slash '/' at the end.

7. Put the address of your chosen Aminet mirror site into 'AmiAddr'.

 'SysAddr' and 'AmiDir' will be covered later...

8. Set the 'MaxSize' variable.  Files that are larger than 'MaxSize' will 
not be downloaded.

 RecName' is the filename of the recent files list on your chosen Aminet 
mirror site.  It comes in two flavours:

@rem <PRE>
 ADT_RECENT_7  - New files in the past seven days
 ADT_RECENT_14 - New files in the past forteen days
@rem </PRE>

9. Choose a name and location for the ZAmiMirror log file and set 'LogFile' 
accordingly.

10. If you want to mirror all of the Aminets directories then set 
'FullTree' to 'Yes'

11. Save the script at this point.

12. Log into the InterNet and get a AmigaDOS shell ready.

13. Type 'CD DH3:Aminet' (or wherever your Aminet directory is).

14. Type 'NcFTP sunsite.doc.ic.ac.uk' (or whatever your Aminet site is).

15. When connected and the NcFTP prompt appears it should look something 
like this:

@rem <PRE>
 phoenix.doc.ic.ac.uk:/
 ncftp>
@rem </PRE>

 Although you connected to 'sunsite.doc.ic.ac.uk', the site name is 
actually 'phoenix.doc.ic.ac.uk' and this must be entered into the 'SysAddr' 
variable.

 If the site name is the same as the address in 'AmiAddr', you still need 
to set 'SysAddr'.

16. Type 'cd pub/aminet'

 If the prompt now looks like this:

@rem <PRE>
 phoenix.doc.ic.ac.uk:/pub/aminet
 ncftp>
@rem </PRE>

 Then 'AmiDir' is correct.  If the site complains that the directory does 
not exist then you will have to find the 'aminet' directory and set 
'AmiDir' accordingly.

17. Type 'cd info/adt' and then 'get TREE' which will download a list of 
all the current Aminet directories to a file called 'TREE' in your Aminet 
directory.

18. Type 'dir ADT_RECENT_*'

19. If you see 'ADT_RECENT_7.Z' then this Aminet site supports 'GZip' 
compressed files.  If you have GZip on your system then add the '.Z' 
extension to 'RecName'.  (Example: RecName = 'ADT_RECENT_7.Z')

20. If you want to mirror all the Aminet directories then skip to step 27

21. If the file transfered successfully then type 'exit' and shut down your 
InterNet connection.

22. Load the TREE file into your text editor.  It should look something 
like this:

@rem <PRE>
 new           Upload area
 priv          Private uploads
 recent        Files uploaded the last seven days
 biz           Business software
 biz/dbase     Data base software
 biz/demo      Demo versions of commercial software
 biz/misc      Misc business type soft
 biz/patch     Commercial software patches
 comm          Communications
 comm/bbs      BBS programs
 :   :          :    :
 [Lots more directories]
 :   :          :    :
@rem </PRE>

23. Remove the 'new', 'priv' and 'recent' lines.

24. Go through the list and delete all the directories you do not want to 
mirror. Remember that often many megabytes of files are sent to some 
directories, so choose carefully.

25. Save the file.

 If you do not want the files automatically uploaded to your Zeus file 
areas then skip to step 30.

26. You need to create a file area for every Aminet directory you wish to 
mirror.  Fortunately, this can be automated with the included 
'TreeToCfg.rexx' script.

27. In the shell, type 'rx Zeus:Rexx/TreeToCfg <Dir> <Area>' where <Dir> is 
your Aminet directory (Same as the 'Base' variable) and <Area> is the 
starting file area number.

28. If all went well, type 'Zeus:Bin/UpdateConfigs FA' to reload the file 
area configuration.  You will also need to edit your SIG and Template 
configs at some point to give your users access to the new areas.

29. Make sure you have saved all the changes to ZAmiMirror.rexx.

30. Thats it!  To start the program, log into the InterNet, and, once 
connected, type 'rx Zeus:Rexx/AmiNet/ZAmiMirror' into a shell.



ZNewsXfer

@rem <PRE>
Zeus Bulletin Board System (c)1992-2002 Zeus Developments
Network News Transfer Protocol for TCP/IP - Written by Nick Loman and Neil 
Williams

Usage: ZNewsXfer NEWSHOST/A,PORT/N,BATCHFILE,AREASFILE,SOCKETS/N,APPEND/S,
                 
LASTDATE,DEBUG/S,KILLFILE/K,GROUPLIST/K,NEWGROUPS/K,TEMPDIR/K

   Upon first invocation, the LASTDATE argument must be specified in
   date format YYMMDD HHMMSS.
@rem </PRE>

NEWSHOST@{uu} - NNTP host to get news from, e.g. "news.demon.co.uk", 
"pubnews.demon.co.uk", "news.mistral.co.uk"

PORT@{uu} - Port number to connect to. Defaults to NNTP's port, 119 (usually no 
need to change).

BATCHFILE@{uu} - Output file for "RNews" formatted batch files containing 
messages, defaults to "Ram:Batch".

AREASFILE@{uu} - "Active" file, file containing list of newsgroups, one per 
line. Can be shared with an NNTPd's Active file, the extra data used by 
NNTPd is ignored. See ZGate's XGROUP configuration item description.

SOCKETS <num>@{uu} - Number of sockets/connections to open to the server, to 
increase performance by ensuring that while one connection waits the next 
is downloading something. Best results with a modem appear to be with 4 
sockets - experiment with you connection to find the best performance. 
Defaults to 1.

APPEND@{uu} - Append instead of overwriting BATCHFILE.

LASTDATE <date>@{uu} - Specify the oldest news messages to retreive in YYMMDD 
HHMMSS format, if not specified uses a "Zeus:Config/LastNNTP_<servername>" 
file for data, e.g. "030116 000000" (include quotes). Only specify the data 
on the first invocation; afterwards ZNewsXfer collects messages from the 
date it has stored through to the present.

KILLFILE <file>@{uu} - Name of kill file. A kill file contains one or more 
patterns to match and kill on Message-IDs you do not wish to receive.

GROUPLIST <file>@{uu} - Retrieve entire group listing from server into <file>. 
Use sparingly - these lists are massive.

NEWGROUPS <file>@{uu} - Retrieve all new groups since LASTDATE from server into 
<file>. This allows you to use GROUPLIST once, then use NEWGROUPS to keep 
it up to date with new additions at the server.

TEMPDIR <file>@{uu} - Where to store temporary files. Defaults to T:.


Example Lines

1> ZNewsXfer news.mistral.co.uk PORT 119 BATCHFILE uunews:batch
      AREASFILE Zeus:Config/Msg/NewsGroups.Txt SOCKETS 4 APPEND
      LASTDATE "951117 000000"  TEMPDIR "ram:"

1> ZNewsXfer news.mistral.co.uk SOCKETS 2

1> ZNewsXfer news.freeserve.co.uk BatchFile Mail:BatchFile
      AreasFile uunews:active Append
      Newgroups Zeus:Config/NewNewsGroups.txt
      KillFile Zeus:Config/NewsKillFile Sockets 4
2> ZGate GateBatch Mail:BatchFile DELETE


---Example KILLFILE---
*scala*
*nazi.com*
---Example KILLFILE---

---Example GROUPLIST---
comp.sys.amiga.datacomm
comp.sys.amiga.games
comp.sys.amiga.misc
---Example GROUPLIST---


You can use ZGate to gate the news, for importing with ZMP, using the 
"GATEBATCH" option.



@endnode




@node Manual.SIG "10 - Special Interest Groups"

Chapter 10 - Special Interest Groups

Special Interest Groups allow the SysOp to separate the message and file 
areas on the BBS into distinct topics which may include both message and 
file areas.This feature is useful when you have more file or message areas 
and want to group them together to make finding the right area easier for 
your areas.

Let's imagine our BBS is split up into three distinct groups of interest.

  AmiNet files for download

  Information about herbal remedies

  Support for the YARGLE(tm) software.

For each SIG's there may be some file areas or message areas, and our

Message Area configuration looks like this:

@rem <pre>
[  100] AmiNet New Files
[  200] Herbal Remedy Recipes
[  201] Discussion about innovations in the Herbal
        Remedy field.
[  202] Herbalists General Chat.
[  300] YARGLE(tm) betatesters echo
[  301] YARGLE(tm) users echo
[  302] YARGLE(tm) general chat
@rem </pre>

And the File Area Configuration may look like this:

@rem <pre>
[  100] AmiNet comm/bbs
[  101] AmiNet comm/fido
[  102] AmiNet comm/mail
    ::    ::    ::   ::   more AmiNet areas.

[  200] Herbal recipes (archived with .ZIP)
[  300] YARGLE(tm) betatester updates
[  301] YARGLE(tm) support files
@rem </pre>

These are not huge lists, but it makes more sense to be split up into 
separate
groups:

@rem <pre>
AmiNet
 AmiNet New Files
 AmiNet comm/bbs
 AmiNet comm/fido
 AmiNet comm/mail

Herbal Remedies
 Herbal Remedy Recipes
 Discussion about innovations...
 Herbalists General Chat
 Herbal recipes (archived with .ZIP)

YARGLE(tm)
 YARGLE(tm) betatesters echo
 YARGLE(tm) users echo
 YARGLE(tm) general chat
 YARGLE(tm) betatester updates
 YARGLE(tm) support files
@rem </pre>

This setup makes it easier for the user to pinpoint exactly what they want 
and resembles a directory structure system familiar to all computer users.

In a similar way to a file system, Zeus allows multi-levels of directories.

Configuring SIGs

@{"SIG Configuration Window" Link "SIGConfig.gif/main"}

Each SIG has various items of configuration data to be set:

Tag@{uu} - The "tagname" of the SIG. This name will be the "key" for user's to 
type when selecting which SIG they wish to access, rather than by a 
conventional numbering system. This should be less than 8 characters long. 
For multi-levelled SIG's they can be represented in a way similar to 
directories "e.g. AMINET/BBS" would access the SIG named BBS located within 
the AMINET SIG.

Description@{uu} - The description of the SIG. This should be less than 40 
characters.

Low@{uu} - The lowest access level necessary to access this SIG. This value has 
no bearing upon the access to the areas within it.

High@{uu} - The highest access level necessary to access this SIG.

Even if you have not configured any SIG's you will already have one set up 
on the BBS. It's name is "GLOBAL" and contains all your file and message 
areas.Load up the SIG Editor (Special Interest Groups from the Config-
>Messages and Config->Files menu or Prefs/SigEditor) and select "GLOBAL". 
You will see the "Default" checkbox is set on. This means that whenever a 
new area is added to the BBS it will automatically be added to this SIG 
definition. A new user logging into the BBS will have the GLOBAL SIG 
attributed to their user account by default.

How do SIG's work?

Each user has two SIG settings, one for files and one for messages. 
However, a user selecting a SIG which contains both file and message areas 
will have both their File SIG and their Message SIG change.

Conversely, a user selecting a SIG with only message areas attributed to it 
will only have their Message SIG changed.

Attributing an Area to a SIG

After selecting the SIG you wish to edit, the two listviews displaying the 
complete list of file areas and the list of message areas are 
activated.Clicking upon the area will attribute the area to a SIG when the 
check icon is displayed, when the check icon disappears the area is not 
attributed to the area. If you wish to add or remove more than one area at 
one time menu items are provided for this:

@rem <pre>
File ->     Add all Areas
            Remove all Areas
            -------------------
            Copy range...
            Match string...

Messages -> Add all Areas
            Remove all Areas
            -------------------
            Copy range...
            Match string...
@rem </pre>

Add all Areas -Attributes all the file or message areas on the BBS to the 
currently selected SIG.

Remove all Areas - De-attributed all the file or message areas on the BBS 
to the currently selected SIG.

Copy range - Attributes a certain "range" of areas to the current SIG. 
Input to this function may contain the standard range formatting commands 
(1-39,4,6 etc.)

Match string - Attributes all areas which descriptive name match the 
wildcard pattern entered. This function accepts all AmigaDOS wildcards and 
is not case-sensitive, e.g. #?Zeus#? would match "Zeus Updates", "Zeus 
Doors" and "comm/zeus"

You should set up your SIG's once you have set up your message and file 
areas correctly. Firstly split up the different catagories of your BBS into 
separate sections linked together by a subject or topic.

Setting up example SIGs

Here is an example file area and message area configuration:

@rem <pre>
 File Areas                  Message Areas

 [100] AmiNet comm/bbs    [100] Latest AmiNet uploads
 [101] AmiNet comm/dlg
 [102] AmiNet comm/fido   [200] Ancient Greek history
 [103] AmiNet comm/mail         discussion
 [104] AmiNet comm/tcp    [201] Ancient Norweigan
 [105] AmiNet comm/zeus         mythology
                          [202] The Bronze Age era
                          [203] Henry VIII - artist
                                or fat bloke?
 [110] AmiNet dev/asm
 [110] AmiNet dev/c       [300] Neighbours discussion
 [111] AmiNet dev/e       [301] Home & Away discussion
 [112] AmiNet dev/gui     [302] Eastenders plot
 [113] AmiNet dev/misc          giveaways!
 [200] Pictures of Amiga Tech
       employees nude.
 [300] Pictures of Coronation
       Street stars nude.
 [900] Pictures of rare birds.
 [500] Uploads
@rem </pre>

From this list we can pick out four distinct catagories: AmiNet, Pictures, 
History and TV Soaps.

Load up the SIG Configuration and click ADD four times.

Replace the (new) entries with:

@rem <pre>
   Name: AMINET
   Desc: The AmiNet Software Archive
    Low: 0
   High: 255

Default: No
   Name: PICTURES
   Desc: Pictures of rare and exotic things!
    Low: 0
   High: 255

Default: No
   Name: HISTORY
   Desc: Discussion of all things history!
    Low: 0
   High: 255

Default: No
   Name: TV
   Desc: Television- view of the world or boring tosh?
    Low: 0
   High: 255
Default: No
@rem </pre>

Select the "AMINET" SIG. Select "Copy range..." from the Files menu and 
enter 100-113 in the box to add all the AmiNet areas.

Select the "PICTURES" SIG. Select "Match string..." from the Files menu and 
enter "Picture#?" to add all the Picture related areas.

Keep going for the other two SIGs...

We may think that file area 300 is worthy of being in two SIGs, PICTURES 
and TV. Select the "TV" SIG and select area 300 from the file area listview 
to add this in. It now belongs to both SIGs.


Access Restrictions

This section is concerned with restricting access of the BBS and the file 
and message data contained within to users. Information on how to restrict 
login access to the BBS can be found in Chapter 3, Configuring your Lines, 
Section Line Access.

Access Levels

Every user on the BBS is assigned an access level in the range of 0 to 255. 
 Conventions dictate that 0 refers to a new user, where 255 is reserved for 
the local SysOp. When a new user logs into the BBS they are automatically 
assigned level 0.

The Design of the Access Level System

This is the process that Zeus goes through to determine whether a user has
access to a file or message area:

@rem <PRE>
Is the users access greater than the low access definition?

If not, no access to area.

Is the users access less than the high access definition?

If not, no access to area.

Is the message/file area defined in the ranges in the Template 
Configuration for
this particular access level?

If not, no access to area.

Is the Access flag set?

If not, no access to area.

Is the user old enough to access this area?

If not, no access to area.

Is the user the right gender to access this area?

If yes - Access granted to area.
@rem </PRE>

Low and High Access Definitions

These fields have the highest priority override all over the other access 
definitions. If you set a file area's low access level field to 10 then new 
user's may not access level even if the area has been defined in the 
Template Configuration ranges.


@endnode



@node Manual.Templates "11 - Templates"

Chapter 11 - Template Configuration

The Template Configuration allows the most precise restrictions to be 
placed on the BBS. There are three distinct parts to the Template 
Configuration:

 - User Limits

 - Message Area Definitions

 - File Area Definitions

The Template Configuration may be accessed by running Prefs/Templates, or 
selecting Users->Configuration->Templates menu on the Control Screen.

@{"Template Window" Link "TemplateConfig.gif/main"}

User Limits

User Limits allow the SysOp to limit the amount of time spent on the BBS 
each
day, regarding the number of files that may be downloaded each day; the 
number
they may download in ratio to the number of files that they upload, and the
maximum number of messages the user may write per day.

For each access level defined in the Template Configuration they may be 
another
set of user limits. You may edit the user limits by pressing the

"Edit User Limits" button:

@{"Template Limits Window" Link "TemplateLimits.gif/main"}

Calls/Day@{uu}
 The maximum number of calls a user may make per day. "0" indicates the 
user may log on as many times as desired.

Mins/Call@{uu}
 The maximum amount of time in minutes a user may spend online per call. 
"1440" would give the user access to the BBS for a full 24hrs a day.

Mins/Day@{uu}
 The maximum amount of time in minutes a user may spend online every day. 
"1440" would give the user access to the BBS for a full 24hrs a day.

File Ratio@{uu}
 The number of files a user may download for every one they upload. A value 
of "10" would set the ratio at 1:10 meaning that they may download 10 files 
for every one they upload. "0" gives them unlimited File Access.

Byte Ratio@{uu}
 The number of bytes a user may download for every byte they upload. "0" 
gives them unlimited Byte Access.

Max Bytes@{uu}
 The maximum number of bytes they may download every day. "0" means there 
is no restriction on download.

Max Files@{uu}
 The maximum number of files they may download every day. "0" means there 
is no restriction on download.

Free Files@{uu}
 The number of "free" files they are allowed to download before the File 
Ratio field becomes active. A typical value would be "10" with a file ratio 
of 1:10. Giving a user 0 free files means they must upload before they can 
download.

Free Bytes@{uu}
 The number of "free" bytes they are allowed to download before the Byte 
Ratio field becomes active.

Max Msgs@{uu}
 The number of messages a user may write per day. "0" indicated unlimited 
write access.


Simply changing the values in the User Limits Value will not affect the 
user's account data. If you wish to update all the users' access levels to 
new levels then press the "Update User Limits" button. A window will pop-up 
showing the user names as they are being processed.

@{"Template Updating Window" Link "TemplateUpdate.gif/main"}

This function can also be achieved through the ARexx script 
"Zeus:Rexx/SetLimits.rexx"


Area Definitions

The main window of the Template Configuration is concerned with attributing 
"access types" to ranges of areas. This means that different "access types" 
(allow access, allow download etc.) can be set for whole ranges of areas at 
a time, making it easy for you to partition your file and message area 
list.

Once you have created an access level, or selected an existing one you can 
add and delete ranges of areas to the "access list", which is represented 
by the large listview in the middle of the window. Each area range defined 
for the currently selected access level will be shown here, along with the 
"access types" assigned to it.

Access Types

There are 12 access flags for message areas and file areas.

A '-' character as an access type indicates that the area is set off, where 
a "access letter" shows shows that the access flag is set on. Pressing the 
according buttons will toggle the flag of the currently selected access 
range.

Ranges defined do not necessarily need to cover existing message areas. The 
range "1-1000" is a legitimate range even if only area 4, 5 and 50 exist 
currently. The ranges will apply to new areas defined after the template 
configuration was set.

For file areas the following tags are available to be set:

@{"File Tags Window" Link "TemplateFileTags.gif/main"}

Descriptor in Window
 Letter Tag
 Description

A
 A
 The user may access this area.

V
 V
 The user may view files in this area.

D
 D
 The user may download files in this area. Providing the user's access 
allows them enough file ratio and credits to access the file they may 
transfer files from this area to their local storage.

U
 U
 The user may upload files in this area providing the area definition 
supports uploading to this area (see Section File Area Configuration) and 
there is enough space left on the BBS hard drive.

EO
 O
 The user may edit the description of files they have uploaded.

EA
 A
 The user may edit all files. The user may change the description of any 
files in this file area regardless of whether they uploaded it. This flag 
should only be granted to trusted user's and SysOp's in most cases

UV
 u
 The user may access unvalidated files and the user may list, download, 
edit and delete (providing the correct flags are set) files that have not 
be validated by the SysOp after uploading.

D
 d
 The user may delete the file catalogue entry and the physical files in 
this area.

VA
 v
 The user may validate files. The user may validate files uploaded to this 
area.This flag is generally coupled with the UV flag.

S
 S
 The user has SysOp privileges in this area, useful for defining a specific 
access level for SigOp's (special SysOp's that manage and control a 
particular area/SIG of the BBS)

M
 M
 The user is masked out of the BBS by default.

P
 P
 The user has pack access in this area. When using an offline grabber they 
will by default have this area selected for downloading.


For message areas the following tags are available to be set:

@{"Template Message Tags Window" Link "TemplateMsgTags.gif/main"}


Descriptor in Window
 Letter Tag
 Description

A
 A
 The user may access this area. This area will show up when the user lists 
file areas.

R
 R
 The user may read and scan public messages and private messages addressed 
to them, or writing by them in this area

W
 W
 The user may write messages in this area. Under the constraints of the 
Message Area Configuration (see Section Message Area Configuration) the 
user may post messages to other users or "ALL" in this area.

WP
 P
 The user may write private messages in this area. This flag overrides the 
"Private Base" flag defined in the Message Area Configuration and is 
generally used only as a privelidged access type.

PR
 V
 The user may read private messages not addressed to, or written by 
themselves. This flag is generally only granted to SysOp's for monitoring 
of the nature of the conversations taken place. User's do not take kindly 
to their private messages being read so this flag should be kept for the 
exclusive use of the SysOp or SigOp in most cases.

KO
 K
 This flag allows the user to mark messages written by them as deleted (NB 
this does not remove the message from the storage physically).

KA
 L
 The user mark any message in the area as deleted. In general this flag 
should only be granted to SysOp's or SigOp's.

CN
 C
 The user may send crash netmails. This flag only applies in "Netmail 
bases" as defined in the Message Area Configuration. The definition of a 
Crash Netmail can be found in the Messages chapter.

FA
 F
 The user has file attach access. This flag allows the user to assign one 
or more physical files to the message for distribution over the BBS or mail 
network.This flag should only be granted "en masse" to local message areas 
and should be restricted to the SysOp for distributed networks.

S
 S
 The user has SysOp privelidges in this area, useful for defining a 
specific access level for SigOp's (special SysOp's that manage and control 
a particular area of the BBS)

M
 M
 The user is masked out of the BBS by default.

P
 P
 The user has pack set in this area, upon using an offline grabber they 
will by default have this area selected for downloading.

You may wish to use the [A-----------] flag set for new user's to the BBS, 
so they can see what is available on the BBS but not be able to interact 
with it.


Updating Template Configuration

Changes made in the Template Editor will only affect user's when the online 
configuration has been updated, or when Zeus has been reloaded.

Upon quitting the template editor a requester will pop-up asking whether 
you wish to update the online configuration. Selecting to update the online 
configuration has the same effect as "UpdateConfigs TEMPLATES" (see section 
Bin/UpdateConfigs).


Editing access user-by-user

More intricate configuration can be acheived upon certain users' access 
flags by using the UserEditor. Although the access level system affects the 
entire user base, any changes of the access flags from within the 
UserEditor will remain constant when changing the Template Configuration.

e.g. If we defined an Access Level 100 [Special User] from within the 
Template Configuration with the following access groups.

[M] [1-1000] [ARW----------]

[F] [1-1000] [AVDU---------]

Then we assigned user "Joe Normal" to this access group using the 
UserEditor.When asked if we wish to "Assign Template Limits" we select YES 
and the correct area definitions are copied to his account. We can verify 
this by looking in the "Messages" window of UserEditor.

Now if we wanted to give him Crash Netmail access to area 30 - FidoNet 
netmail but we did not want every user who had access 100 to be able to 
send Crash Netmails we could select area 30 from the UserEditor and click 
on the Crash Netmail checkbox.

Saving this user account and quitting the UserEditor would mean he could 
instantly write Crash Netmails now in that area. However, what if we 
changed the Template Configuration to have the access group:

[M] [1-1000] [ARW---O------]

Would Joe Normal lose his Crash Netmail setting? No, he wouldn't. All 
"changes" to his message or file area account are saved in his user data 
and respected after any Template changes.


Typical Access Flags

Typical flag accesses' for access levels are as follows:

New User:

Messages: [AR----------]

Files:    [AV----------]

Normal User:

Messages: [ARW---------]

Files:    [AVDU--------]

SigOp:

Messages: [ARWPVKL-F--P]

Files:    [AVDUOEudv---]

SysOp:

Messages: [ARWPVKLCFSMP]

Files:    [AVDUOEUdvSMP]


Resetting all Template Changes

In case you wish to flush out any changes you have made to user's, the 
ARexx script "PurgeXData" is supplied. For more information on how user's 
XData works.

Usage: PurgeXData <XData> [Access Level]

To flush out all the changes to every user's message area account you would 
run it in the following manner:

1> rx Zeus:Rexx/Users/PurgeXData.rexx MCHG

To flush out all the file area changes to all users will access level 0 the 
following line would be run:

1> rx Zeus:Rexx/Users/PurgeXData.rexx FCHG 0


An example BBS Configuration

For a simple BBS, three access levels should suffice and are set up on the
distribution disks provided with Zeus.

Let's assume we have set up the file areas so they fall within the 
following bounds:

Area 1
 Local Announcement Area, all users should have PACK access to this 
automatically.

Areas 2-500
 All areas falling within this range are of unrestricted access to user's, 
new users can access and view these areas.

Areas 501-1000
 Only validated users should be able to access these areas (software 
support or privelidged information for example)

Areas 1001-2000
 For the SysOp's private use.

We would set up the Template Configuration in the following way.

@rem <pre>
[  0] - New User
                [M] [  2-500 ]  [AR----------]
                [F] [  2-500 ]  [AV----------]
                [M] [  1-1   ]  [AR---------P]

[ 10] - Validated User
                [M] [  2-1000]  [ARW---------]
                [F] [  2-1000]  [AVDU--------]
                [M] [  1-1   ]  [ARW--------P]

[255] - SysOp
                [M] [  2-2000]  [ARWPVKLCFSMP]
                [F] [  2-2000]  [AVDUOEUdvSMP]
@rem </pre>


@endnode


@node Manual.ARexx "12 - Getting started with ARexx and Zeus"

Chapter 12 - Getting started with ARexx and Zeus

Written by Andrew Rooney

What is Arexx?

ARexx is an very flexible high level scripting language used to manipulate 
and control other programmes. Communication is done by sending information 
though message ports. Many pieces of Amiga software have an ARexx port. By 
using ARexx with other software, you can automate or perform new tasks by 
using combinations of the ARexx functions the software supports.

ARexx is usually an optional extension to a program. Zeus, however, 
requires the use of ARexx to change the look and feel of your BBS.

Why Arexx with Zeus?

Zeus uses an extremely powerful set of extended ARexx commands. The way 
your BBS appears is dependant upon the code within your own set of Arexx 
scripts.The Zeus ARexx command set allows you to create your BBS according 
to your own tastes, due to the almost infinite combinations of commands and 
the powerful language structure ARexx provides. The "look and feel" of the 
BBS is entirely up to you. After becoming familiar with Zeus you will soon 
see how Zeus BBS breaks down the barriers created by old-generation BBS 
software.

Zeus allows you design your BBS the way YOU want it. You are only limited 
by your imagination!

How Do I Use Arexx?

Arexx is invisible. If you have installed it an introductory message when 
RexxMast is run is the closest you will come to seeing it running.RexxMast 
is a server that allows Arexx to function. Arexx scripts (usually ending in 
.rexx) can be started using the command Rx. However, Zeus runs internal 
Zeus scripts automatically without any interaction on your part.

If you are looking for the files "Rexxmast" and "Rx" the paths for the 
files are "Sys:Rexxc/Rx" and "Sys:System/RexxMast".

In order to write your Arexx programmes you will require a good text 
editor. The type of editor can be important when it comes to writing BBS 
software you will want to add ANSI colour codes, CygnusEd is the best, but 
it is a commerical programme and not available as shareware. There are many 
available on the AmiNet in the text/edit directory.

Format of a Zeus Arexx Script

All Zeus Arexx scripts have a few specific similarities. A few simple 
guidelines will help you get on your way to creating your own scripts.

Zeus requires a few important keywords to be placed at the beginning of 
your scripts.

The first line must be a comment:

@rem <pre>
/* Comment string */
@rem </pre>

All Arexx scripts require a comment line at the beginning. Comment lines 
start with the characters /* and end with */. If they are not present the 
Arexx server will not recognize it as an Arexx programme and will report an 
error, such as "Command returned 5/1: Program not found". If the scripts 
was launched by Zeus the error will appear in the ZeusError.log file within 
your Log Directory.

Within the comment braces you should put a description of what the script 
does.

After the comment the next line should be:

@rem <pre>
Parse Arg Line
@rem </pre>

The above line of text is very important for the Zeus functions to operate 
properly. This line puts the current line number the script is running on 
in the variable Line. For most Zeus ARexx functions to work the 
Lineargument must be present.

The next line should be:

@rem <pre>
Options Results
@rem </pre>

This text, commonly the 3rd line in every Zeus Arexx script is extremely 
important. Without it, nearly all Zeus Arexx functions will fail. This 
allows functions to return information to you in a variable called "RESULT" 
and plays a key role in making your script work. This is explained later in 
the chapter "Results, Strings, Variables, and Return Codes".

To recap:

@rem <pre>
/* My First Zeus Arexx Script! */

Parse Arg Line

Options Results

Running your Zeus Arexx Script
@rem </pre>

The Arexx scripts that you write are not compiled such as a programme 
written in C or Assembler. Instead they are interpreted. A "standard" Arexx 
script, one which does not use any non-asynchronous Zeus Arexx commands 
(see Chapter Arexx Command Set) could be run with the command:

@rem <pre>
Rx HelloWorld.rexx
@rem </pre>

Where HelloWorld.rexx was:

@rem <pre>
/* HelloWorld.rexx */

say 'Hello, world'
@rem </pre>

When you are running a Zeus Arexx script you do not need to issue the Rx 
command as Zeus will interpret your script for you. Zeus will always begin 
and end it's script sequence with the scripts Zeus:Rexx/Local.zeus or 
Zeus:Rexx/Remote.zeus as explained later on.

"Hello World" Example

Load up your text editor and type the following into the window:

@rem <pre>
/* Example Script "Hello World.Zeus" */

Parse Arg Line

Options Results

ClrScr

Write '"Hello World!\n\n"'

WaitReturn

/* End Example Script */
@rem </pre>

Now Save it as "HelloWorld.zeus" in your Zeus:Scripts/ directory, and then 
add the following text to the end of Zeus:Scripts/Main/MainMenu.zeus

@rem <pre>
CMD_9:

CallScript 'HelloWorld'

Signal MenuLoop
@rem </pre>

Then when you select option '9' from your BBS menu it will then clear the 
screen and display something similiar to:

@rem <pre>
Hello World!

Press RETURN to continue
@rem </pre>

Why Your Arexx Script Will Not Work

Check the following first:

 - Is RexxMast Running?

 - Did you add the rexxsupport.library in your startup-sequence

 - Are you executing it from you BBS menu properly?

 - Check spelling and other annoying errors.

 - Are FileControl and MsgControl Running?

If these lines of enquiry prove unsuccessful then it is time to start going 
through your code. The best way to begin debugging is to remember where 
exactly in your programme that the error happened. Then you should check 
your logfile called Zeus:Logs/ZeusError.log. The error log is not a 100% 
accurate method of debugging, but it will give you an idea of what is going 
on.If the error log tells you you have an unmatched quote, then you must go 
through each line checking for quotes that are not completed.

Example:

@rem <pre>
'"This is a correctly quoted string"'

'"This is NOT a correctly quoted string"
@rem </pre>

There are many possible errors with Arexx, but the best way is to write 
code a few lines at a time and checking they work until you build up more 
confidence.It is important to read your Arexx script character by 
character.

Eventually Arexx scripting will become quite natural.

Another good way to check for errors and when the occur is to run 
Zeus:Prefs/Startup and remove the line that starts up the BBSLine on node 
0. Start Zeus again with a reboot. Line 0 will not be active (you will not 
be able to login locally). You can manually start the line from shell.

@rem <pre>
1> Zeus:Bin/BBSLine 0 local.zeus
@rem </pre>

While line 0 is in use, any Arexx errors encountered will be echoed to the 
shell you started BBSLine from in realitime.

Results, Strings, Variables, and Return Codes

Strings@{uu}

Strings are anything, they can contain numbers, letters or any printable 
character. They can be of any length.

@rem <pre>
MyString = 'Hello World!'
@rem </pre>

Would place the words Hello World! into a variable called MyString.

Variables@{uu}

In Arexx, if you would like to store the string "Hello World!" as a 
variable, you would do the following in your text editor:

@rem <pre>
/* Making a String! */

Parse Arg Line

Options Results

string = 'Hello World!'  /* Create the String  */
ClrScr                   /* Clear the Screen   */
Write '"'string'\n\n"'   /* Display the string */
WaitReturn               /* Wait for a Return  */

/* End Making a string */
@rem </pre>

Now add it to your Menu and try it with Zeus.

The result of this example does the same thing as the first Hello World 
Example script, only using a variable string, instead of a constant string.

Another type of variable you may find that is used quite frequently is an 
array variable. These variables are made up of two parts and will look 
similiar to VARIABLE.EXT

Where VARIABLE is the actual variable and EXT is the numbered extension.

Some Examples:

@rem <pre>
Variable.1 = 'Hello '
Variable.2 = 'World'

Do i = 1 to 2
 Write '"'Variable.i'"'
End

Write '"\n\n"'
WaitReturn
@rem </pre>

The result of this example would output:

@rem <pre>
Hello World

Press RETURN to continue
@rem </pre>

Results@{uu}

In order to use the RESULT from an Arexx function, you must add the line 
"Options Results" to the top of your programme. This is very important when 
writing Zeus Arexx programmes. RESULT is a special variable that is created 
every time you run a Zeus Arexx function and is used to return information 
you have requested.

Example:

@rem <pre>
/* Get the user's name */

Parse Arg Line

Options Results

GetUserData 'NAME'  /* The Zeus Arexx function! After
                       this function is used it will
                       store the user's name in the
                       variable called RESULT */

UserName = Result   /* Now we make the variable UserName equal to RESULT */

Write '"You are 'UserName'\n\n"'
WaitReturn

/* End Username Example */
@rem </pre>

After you run this example from Zeus, it will show you something similiar 
to:

@rem <pre>
You are Andrew Rooney

Press RETURN to Continue
@rem </pre>

Return Codes@{uu}

Return codes work exactly the same way that RESULT codes do, only this time 
the variable is called "RC" and not "RESULT". Once again, the variable RC 
is created automatically each time you run a Zeus Arexx function. If you 
look in the chapter ARexx Command Set you will see that GetUserData will 
return two different return codes (RCs), 0 if everything went OK, or a 
number greater than zero (>0) if something went wrong.

Example:

@rem <pre>
/* Get the user's name */

Parse Arg Line

Options Results

GetUserData 'NAME'
UserName = Result

/* Check if the RC is >0, if so, something went wrong! */

If RC > 0 then Write '"Something went wrong! Could not get your name!\n\n"'
/* If everything went OK, then write what we want to
   see */
Else Write '"You are 'UserName'\n\n"'

WaitReturn
@rem </pre>

If this programme was to fail (not likely), you would see:

@rem <pre>
Something went wrong! Could not get your name!

Press RETURN to continue
@rem </pre>

However, if everything went fine, you would see the original example's 
output of:

@rem <pre>
You are Andrew Rooney

Press RETURN to continue
@rem </pre>

Formatting Strings

Most Zeus functions require a certain format when strings are involved. 
Because the inputs to Zeus Arexx functions may contain strings or keywords, 
Zeus Arexx functions requires separate pieces of information sent to it to 
be all in one string and therefore enclosed in single quotes.

@rem <pre>
'This is a single quoted string'
@rem </pre>

Using the Zeus command Write if you used the string in it's present state 
you would only see the following:

@rem <pre>
Write 'This is a single quoted string'

Result: THIS
@rem </pre>

Placing the string in double quotes, the same result will occur:

@rem <pre>
Write "This is a single quoted string"

Result: THIS
@rem </pre>

In order to display the entire string, you must first close it within 
double quotes then single quotes:

@rem <pre>
Write '"This is a dual quoted string"'

Result: This is a dual quoted string
@rem </pre>

In many cases, you will want to display a variable created in your script 
and use it with other constant string information. When this instance 
arises, you must place the variable only within single quotes.

@rem <pre>
ExpandCC '"%50|"'
Variable = Result
Write '"Hello 'Variable', How are you today?"'

Result: Hello Nick Loman, How are you today?
@rem </pre>

Asynchronous Arexx Commands

An asynchronous Zeus Arexx command may be run from ARexx scripts executed 
by the Rx function, where the input and output are redirected to the CLI, 
rather than to the user on the Bulletin Board.

Not all Zeus ARexx commands are asynchronous.

In order to gain access to asynchronous commands, you must specifically 
address the Zeus ARexx port. This is acheived with the following line which 
must appear near the top of your ARexx script.

@rem <pre>
Address 'ZEUS'
@rem </pre>

Avoiding Variable Collisions

The best way to avoid variable collisions is to not create variable that 
will be the same as arguments to functions. Using the Zeus Arexx function 
GetUserData as above, you will see that it accepts the keyword NAME.

Examples:

@rem <pre>
Name="Nick Loman"

GetUserData NAME         /* GetUserData NICK LOMAN */

GetUserData 'NAME'       /* GetUserData NAME       */
@rem </pre>

The second example is the best method to use since 'NAME' will always be 
constant. If you had nothing assigned to the variable NAME, then the first 
example would work just as well as the second. You will have problems 
however, if you are using NAME as a variable.

example:

@rem <pre>
Name = 'HELLO'

GetUserData NAME
@rem </pre>

If you do this, then GetUserData will not work properly since what you are

truly asking the function for is:

@rem <pre>
GetUserData HELLO
@rem </pre>

And to avoid a variable collision you should use the following instead:

@rem <pre>
Name = 'HELLO'

GetUserData 'NAME'
@rem </pre>

In this instance, you are still asking GetUserData to use NAME rather than 
what the variable NAME contained. This will keep you from having problems 
along the way, making it easier to debug your software.

DO, WHILE, UNTIL and ELSE Loops

Loops are a very important element when writing your programmes. The loops 
will help you control the flow of your script without making jumps to 
subroutines within the programme. The combination of IF, DO, ELSE and END 
will allow you to perform most of the essential tasks on your Bulletin 
Board System.

@rem <pre>
/* Display the prompt               */

Write '"Please Enter the letter A: "'

/* Get a single letter in uppercase */

Read MAKEUPPER HOT

/* Store it as variable 'Letter'    */

Letter = Result

/* Check if the letter A was entered*/

If Letter = 'A' then do
 Write '"\nCongratulations you entered the letter A!"'

End

Else Do

 /* Do this if it was something else */
 Write '"\nThat's not the letter A!"'

End

Write '"\n\n"'

WaitReturn
@rem </pre>

REMEMBER:

Every loop, no matter what kind, requires an END statment to tell Arexx 
when your loop has been completed. If you do not place this statement at 
the end of each loop you will get an error returned in the Zeus ErrorLog. 
For every DO branch you use there must be a corresponding END statement.

The following error is commonly reported:

@rem <pre>
+++ Error 26 Missing or unexpected END
@rem </pre>

The same problem will occur if you place too many END statements as well 
where they are not required or expected.

WHILE Loops

A WHILE loop remains in operation until a condition is false. This change 
can be a number of things such as a change in a string or numeric value.

@rem <pre>
Var = 0

Do while var < 5  /* Do this loop while 'var' is less than 5 */

 Var = var+1

End
@rem </pre>

In this example, the variable 'var' will increase by 1 for each time the 
loop is executed. Eventually 'var' is no longer less than 5, and the loop 
exits and continues executing the commands after the 'End' statement.

UNTIL Loops

UNTIL loops are similiar to WHILE loops, except that the loop will remain 
in
operation until a condition is true.

@rem <pre>
Var = 0

Do until var > 5
 say Var
 Var = var+1
End
@rem </pre>

As you can see, the variable 'var' is being increased again, and the loop 
cycles each time and will exit once 'var' has become greater than 5.

Getting Better

There is no real way around it, getting better at Arexx requires time and 
effort on your part, as does learning any language. Jumping in at the deep 
end, experimenting with commands and reading the source of other Arexx 
programmes are the best ways to achieve a better understanding of the 
language. If you do not know how to program in any language Arexx is a 
great place to start since it can prepare you for other high-level 
languages such as C. The combination of Arexx and Zeus should provide you 
with all the flexibility you require for your Bulletin Board System.

Where to Obtain More Information

There are several disk based Arexx tutorials and documentation available to 
you on AmiNet. Books are also available.

AmiNet

ARexxGuide20rq.lha   util/rexx  343K+        ARexx ref & tutorial. ReqTools 
ver.

ARexxGuide2_0A.lha   util/rexx  343K+        Complete ARexx reference. 
AmigaGuide.

Books

"ARexx User's Reference Manual, The REXX Language for the Amiga" written by 
William S. Hawes, from Wishful Thinking Development, 1987.

"Using ARexx on the Amiga" written by by Chris Zamara & Nick Sullivan, from 
Abacus, 1992.


@endnode


@node "Manual.DataManager" "13 - DataManager"

Chapter 13 - DataManager

The DataManager is a program called by the MsgConfig and FileConfig 
preferences programs. It was used in Zeus 1.0, was excluded from Zeus 1.1 
but is back from Zeus 1.2.

It is designed to alter the data files on disk to match changes made to the 
configuration of message and file areas.  Currently, it can only handle the 
Zeus native libraries (those named fbzeus and mbzeus).  As an example, what 
if you had the following message areas and wanted to move WORKBENCH to a 
more favourable position (using the "Number" button the MsgControl->Areas 
window)?

From:@{uu}

10 - Amiga
11 - Amiga
12 - Workbench

To:@{uu}

10 - Amiga
11 - Workbench
12 - Amiga

Firstly, if you post messages using Zeus:Bin/PostMsg into either Workbench 
or Amiga using either's area number, the messages will now go into the 
wrong area. If you often renumber areas, it can be annoying to change your 
off-line message posting scripts continually. Get around this by using 
"PostMsg TAG=<tag name>" instead of the area number, where <tag name> is 
the external tag of the area, such as WORKBENCH for FidoNet or 
alt.amiga.workbench for UseNet.

Now you have the problem that after renumbering the areas like this you 
have messages for Amiga in your Workbench area and vice versa! This is 
where the configuration program's "Update data files" checkmark comes in 
(on the "Save.." window).  If it is left checked when saving, it will call 
the DataManager.

However, before using DataManager you must ensure you renumber the areas 
carefully:

@{"DataManager Bad Example Window" Link "datamgr.gif/main"}

In the above example picture, 11 is renumbered directly to 12 and 12 
renumbered directly to 11. This is bad. DataManager will first renumber 11 
to 12, and in doing so it will erase area 12's original data files - which 
still contain our Workbench messages!  Then, 12 is renumbered to 11 which 
mean our Amiga messages will end up back where they started but Workbench's 
will be deleted.

Instead, follow this slightly more round-about method of renumbering while 
in the MsgControl program:

Renumber 11 to 13 (or another unused area number)
Save and run DataManager (click "Go All").
Renumber 12 to 11
Renumber 13 to 12
Save and run DataManager (click "Go All" again).

Now no data files will clash at any time.


Running DataManager Separately

If you did not opt to update the data files when saving the configuration, 
you can still update afterward using DataManager itself.

@rem <pre>
Zeus:Utils/DataManager ?
PUBSCREEN,FE=FILEEVENTS/S,ME=MSGEVENTS/S,USAGE/S:

PUBSCREEN       -- Set the screen DataManager appears on. E.g, Zeus.
FE=FILEEVENTS/S -- Process file area change events.
ME=MSGEVENTS/S  -- Process message area change events.
@rem </pre>

This can be useful if you are writing a program to handle Zeus's 
configuration, and want to take advantage of DataManager to update areas 
afterwards.  The ZMF.library contains functions used to facilitate using 
this, so programmers can check the documentation for that.

@endnode



@node "Manual.EditingBBS" "Editing Your BBS"

Chapter 14 - Editing your BBS

The Greek God Zeus could come down to Earth in any form he chose. This myth
inspired the name of our BBS package. With a little work, your BBS can take 
any
form you wish to give it.

Introduction

This is possible through our ARexx host system. Every "script" may 
represent a menu, a sequence of events or a function script. A menu is a 
point within the BBS which takes input from the user (by means of the 
keyboard, mouse or appropriate input device) and acts upon it. A sequence 
of events is as it sounds, a list of "events" which the user is forced down 
(like a corridor), with input events separating each event. An example of a 
sequence of events would be the login sequence which is called immediately 
after a recognised user logs into the BBS. A function script is a script 
such as the "file listers", the "download option" and the "message read 
option".

The supplied script set

Supplied with Zeus is a "script set" - this controls the entire structure 
of the BBS that the user sees when they logon. Currently the "script set" 
provided is Wedlock BBS. However there are other "script sets" available 
with different structures which can be found on Zeus support sites. Space 
dictates that we can only document the default script set, and we recommend 
you get to grips with this first before attempting to use a different one 
or writing your own from scratch. All scripts adhere to a similar style, 
but are not interchangable, i.e. in most cases a script from one script set 
cannot be inserted into another script set!

The "Wedlock" script set is split into different directories relating to 
the function of the scripts within:

This is a list of the supplied scripts with Zeus. They are separated into 
menus (M), sequences (S) and functions (F), as defined above.

Scripts

The following table shows a list of the Zeus script files supplied with the 
default BBS setup.

Zeus:Scripts/@{uu}

@rem <pre>
   BootUser.zeus        F
@rem </pre>
 The script which is called when a user is "booted offline" (see 
Zeus:Bin/BootUser).

@rem <pre>
   Local.zeus           S
@rem </pre>
 The first script loaded up by BBSLine when on a local node.

@rem <pre>
   Remote.zeus          S
@rem </pre>
 The first script loaded up by BBSLine when on a remote node.

Bulletins@{uu}

@rem <pre>
   BulletinsList.zeus   F
@rem </pre>
 Displays the bulletin list.

@rem <pre>
   BulletinsMenu.zeus   M
@rem </pre>
 Prompts the user for a choice of bulletin to read.

Doors@{uu}

@rem <pre>
   DoorsMenu.zeus       M
@rem </pre>
 Prompts the user for a choice of doors to run.

Files@{uu}

@rem <pre>
   AddUploads.zeus      F
@rem </pre>
 Adds uploaded files to the file catalogue.

@rem <pre>
   AreaChange.zeus      F
@rem </pre>
 Prompts the user to change file area.

@rem <pre>
   ChangeSig.zeus       F
@rem </pre>
 Prompts the user to change their file Special Interest Group.

@rem <pre>
   DOSscroll.zeus       F
@rem </pre>
 Shows ANSI scroll list of files in a physical directory on your computer 
and prompts for marking/deleting.

@rem <pre>
   Download.zeus        F
@rem </pre>
 Prompts user to select files to download and sends them along with 
currently marked files.

@rem <pre>
   EditFile.zeus        F
@rem </pre>
 Prompts user to edit the file catalogue data of a file.

@rem <pre>
   FileAreaList.zeus    F
@rem </pre>
 Displays a list of file areas (usually used in conjunction with 
AreaChange.zeus).

@rem <pre>
   FileMasking.zeus     F
@rem </pre>
 Prompts user to mask in or out of areas.

@rem <pre>
   GetFile.zeus         F
@rem </pre>
 Asks the user for a file name and expands it to a full path and filename 
which is placed in the variable `FILENAME'.

@rem <pre>
   GlobalNew.zeus       F
@rem </pre>
 Prompts user for a scan date and lists all the files uploaded after this 
date in the current SIG or globally over the BBS.

@rem <pre>
   HuntFile.zeus        F
@rem </pre>
 Searches for a file using file catalogue data as criteria such as 
filename, file description etc.

@rem <pre>
   LongFileList.zeus    F
@rem </pre>
 Displays files in current directory one by one in "full format"

@rem <pre>
   Mark.zeus            F
@rem </pre>
 Marks the file names and numbers specified in the `MarkFiles' variable.

@rem <pre>
   MaskFileArea.zeus    F
@rem </pre>
 Toggles the mask setting of the current area.

@rem <pre>
   NewFileListMenu.zeus F
@rem </pre>
 Prompts users to select a "short file listing mode".

@rem <pre>
   NewList.zeus         F
@rem </pre>
 Lists the files in the area (generally used after a call to 
NewFileListMenu)

@rem <pre>
   PostXfer.zeus        F
@rem </pre>
 Displays the results of a file transfer and calls the "AddUploads" script 
if files have been uploaded.

@rem <pre>
   QueryMark.zeus       F
@rem </pre>
 Prompts users to keep their marked file list when logging off.

@rem <pre>
   SigChange.zeus       F
@rem </pre>
 Displays an ANSI list of Special Interest Groups and allows the user to 
change their current setting.

@rem <pre>
   Tagged.zeus          F
@rem </pre>
 Allows users to remove marks on one or all files in their marked file 
list.

@rem <pre>
   TestFile.zeus        F
@rem </pre>
 Tests a file for it's archive integrity and optionally extracts a 
FILE_ID.DIZ from it.

@rem <pre>
   Upload.zeus          F
@rem </pre>
 Allows the user to select a transfer protocol and upload one or more files 
to the file base. Calls PostXfer if successful.

@rem <pre>
   UploadToUser.zeus    F
@rem </pre>
 Prompts the user for a name to privately send a file to.

@rem <pre>
   ViewFile.zeus        F
@rem </pre>
 Displays the contents of an archived file, or the data of a text file to 
the screen.

@rem <pre>
   FilesMenu.zeus       M
@rem </pre>
 Menu interface to the file base.

Logon@{uu}

 Allows the user to select which items of information they wish to view 
when logging in.

@rem <pre>
   FrontDoorMenu.zeus   M
@rem </pre>
 Shows the logon options requested by the user from the "LogOn Options" 
script.

@rem <pre>
   CheckUser.zeus       F
@rem </pre>
 Checks whether or not the user may logon to the BBS (if they are banned, 
have enough calls or time, have exired or are already online).

@rem <pre>
   DisplayLogo.zeus     F
@rem </pre>
 Displays one of the two Zeus logos alternately.

@rem <pre>
   GetUserName.zeus     F
@rem </pre>
 Prompts the user for their name and password.

@rem <pre>
   LineAccess.zeus      F
@rem </pre>
 Checks the current state of the line they have logged in on and makes sure 
it is not closed, or that the current baud rate is not acceptable.

@rem <pre>
   LoadUserIEMSI.zeus   F
@rem </pre>
 Puts a user logging in with the IEMSI protocol online.

@rem <pre>
   Logon.zeus           S
@rem </pre>
 The main login script called by the "Remote" and "Local" scripts. Displays 
a welcome text, asks for the user name, checks the user and line, gets and 
checks the password and passes control through to PostLogon.

@rem <pre>
   NewUser.zeus         S
@rem </pre>
 Asks user information about themselves and saves a user account if 
successful.

@rem <pre>
   NewUserIEMSI.zeus    S
@rem </pre>
 Retrieves information about the new user from their IEMSI information and 
adds it to their user account.

@rem <pre>
   PostLogon.zeus       S
@rem </pre>
 Displays title screen, logos, news, prompts to scan for new files and 
shows any waiting mail.

@rem <pre>
   PostLogonIEMSI.zeus  S
@rem </pre>
 Displays the information the user has requested in their IEMSI 
configuration.

Main@{uu}

@rem <pre>
   MainMenu.zeus        M
@rem </pre>
 The Main Menu. All functions of the BBS are accessable from here.

@rem <pre>
   PageSysop.zeus       F
@rem </pre>
 Calls the SysOp for a chat. Hit `/' to break in.

@rem <pre>
   SendNodeMsg.zeus     F
@rem </pre>
 Sends a message to a user on another line.

@rem <pre>
   WhosOnline.zeus      F
@rem </pre>
 Displays a list of the users online and what they are doing.

Messages@{uu}

@rem <pre>
   AddSignature.zeus    F
@rem </pre>
 Adds the users' preferred signature for the current area to a message. 
Requires some manual editing.

@rem <pre>
