BBS Script Flow

This diagram shows how the scripts of the BBS link together at a logon:

@{"BBS Flow Diagram Picture" Link "BBSFlow.gif/main"}



The Text System

Introduction

Zeus offers a lot of flexibility when setting up text files for use in your 
BBS. The aim of the Text System is to send the text file which uses as many 
terminal options as the user supports, but does not send anything the user 
cannot understand.

There is a naming convention for text files which allows Zeus to pick the 
best match for the user online but with a fall-back option if that file 
does not exist.

  IBM/Amiga ANSI codes supported
  8 bit character translation
  RIPscrip graphics protocol supported
  Up to 256 Languages
  Up to 256 Menu Sets
  Menu Sets and Languages are totally independent
  Up to 256 Access levels
  Over 16 million possible filenames for each single text file!
  User has complete control over what is displayed on their screen


Basic text files

If a file ends in .TXT it is assumed to be an ANSI/Plain text file. If Zeus 
is displaying it to a user with RIP enabled, it will send the codes to set 
an ANSI palette and reset the text window to full size before displaying 
the file.

The user may choose to disable ANSI codes in which case all ANSI codes will 
be filtered out (or expanded if possible) to produce a plain text file.

The user may also choose to disable 8-Bit characters which causes Zeus to 
use it's internal conversion table to find the best 7-Bit matching 
character.

Zeus will write a screens worth of text and then stop and ask 'More? Y/N/C' 
(or the string specified in Languages) but this can also be disabled if the 
user so wishes.

If the users terminal automatically expands a CR (Carrage-Return) to a CR-
LF (Carrage-Return/Line Feed) pair, the user can ask for all line feeds to 
be filtered out.

Filename searching is performed like this:

Example Base name is: 'MainMenu'

   Search #1: 'MainMenu'      (Zeus always tries the base name first)
   Search #2: 'MainMenu.!0'   (Access level of the user online)
   Search #3: 'MainMenu.TXT'  (The default)

Note: If Zeus matches the base name then it always assumes it is an 
ANSI/Plain text file.


RIP files

If a file ends in .RIP it is assumed to contain RIPscrip protocol commands. 
It is sent to the user as normal but Zeus will not prompt for 'More?' if 
the number of lines in the file is greater than the users screen height. 
Apart from this they are treated exactly like .TXT files, except of course 
that the user's terminal program will interpret them as graphical menus.

Zeus will only look for .RIP files if the user has set the RIP flag, or 
there is no user online yet and Zeus has detected a RIPscrip terminal (See 
ARexx command: DETECTRIP)

Filename searching is performed like this:

Example Base name is: 'MainMenu'

   Search #1: 'MainMenu'      (Zeus always tries the base name first)
   Search #2: 'MainMenu.!0'   (Access level of the user online)
   Search #3: 'MainMenu.RIP'  If the user has RIP enabled)
   Search #4: 'MainMenu.TXT'  (The default)


Menu Sets

The default filename extension for menu sets is <Filename>.<MSNumber>

Zeus' Menu Sets are completely independant of Languages. A Menu Set is 
supposed to be a certain text style, format, or protocol. For example, you 
could set up five menu sets as:

   0 - Plain Text             (No flags selected)
   1 - IBM ANSI               (8 Bit Graphics, ANSI Codes)
   2 - Amiga ANSI             (8 Bit Graphics, ANSI Codes, Amiga ANSI)
   3 - RIPscrip Graphics      (8 Bit Graphics, ANSI Codes, RIP graphics)
   4 - Animated ANSI graphics (8 Bit Graphics, ANSI Codes, No More prompts)

NB: The flags settings are defined in the Prefs/System program.

By defining menu sets like this you let Zeus know that when it opens a file 
called 'MainMenu.3' it contains 8 Bit characters, ANSI codes and RIP 
graphics which will affect how the file is displayed to the user.

If you have no menu sets defined, Zeus will not look for them when 
searching for a file to send to the user. If you have defined 1+ then Zeus 
looks for files in this order:

Example Base name is: 'MainMenu' and user has selected Menu Set #3

   Search #1: 'MainMenu'      (Zeus always tries the base name first)
   Search #2: 'MainMenu.3!0'  (Menu Set and Access level)
   Search #3: 'MainMenu.3' (Tries the menu set extension)
   Search #4: `MainMenu.!0 (Tries the access level)
   Search #5: 'MainMenu.RIP'  (Only if the user wants RIP)
   Search #6: 'MainMenu.TXT'  (The default)


Languages

Zeus allows you to run a multi-lingual BBS by defining languages.  They are
totally independant of menu sets and the filenaming convention is:
<Filename>.-<LanguageNumber>. If you use this format then Zeus doesn't know 
what
is in the file and so it assumes it is ANSI/Plain text.If you want to 
define RIP
language files, see the section below on combining Menu Sets and Languages.

Example Base name is: 'MainMenu' and user has selected language #2

   Search #1: 'MainMenu'      (Zeus always tries the base name first)
   Search #2: 'MainMenu.-2!0' (Language and Access level)
   Search #3: 'MainMenu.-2'   (Tries the language extension)
   Search #4: 'MainMenu.RIP'  (Only if the user wants RIP)
   Search #4: 'MainMenu.!0'   (Tries the access level)
   Search #5: 'MainMenu.TXT'  (The default)


Menu Sets and Languages

If you have defined Menu Sets and Languages, Zeus looks for files with the 
format <FileName>.<MenuSet>-<Language> as well as all the other 
combinations.

Example Base name is: 'MainMenu' and user has selected Menu Set #1 and 
Language #3

   Search # 1: 'MainMenu'        (Zeus always tries the base name first)
   Search # 2: 'MainMenu.1-3!0'  (MenuSet, Language and Access level)
   Search # 3: 'MainMenu.1-3'    (MenuSet and Language)
   Search # 4: 'MainMenu.-3!0'   (Language and Access Level)
   Search # 5: 'MainMenu.-3'     (Language)
   Search # 6: 'MainMenu.3!0'    (Menu Set and Access Level)
   Search # 7: 'MainMenu.3'      (Menu Set)
   Search # 8: 'MainMenu.!0'     (Access level)
   Search # 9: 'MainMenu.RIP'    (Only if the user wants RIP)
   Search #10: 'MainMenu.TXT'    (The default)

It is advised to enable text file buffering if you use menusets and 
languages!


Script name conventions

Scripts also use the text file naming convention, except when the RIP 
protocol is enabled the extension is `zrip', and the default script name is 
`zeus', e.g:

Example Base name is: 'MainMenu' and user has selected Menu Set #1 and 
Language #3

   Search # 1: 'MainMenu'        (Zeus always tries the base name first)
   Search # 2: 'MainMenu.1-3!0'  (MenuSet, Language and Access level)
   Search # 3: 'MainMenu.1-3'    (MenuSet and Language)
   Search # 4: 'MainMenu.-3!0'   (Language and Access Level)
   Search # 5: 'MainMenu.-3'     (Language)
   Search # 6: 'MainMenu.3!0'    (Menu Set and Access Level)
   Search # 7: 'MainMenu.3'      (Menu Set)
   Search # 8: 'MainMenu.!0'     (Access level)
   Search # 9: 'MainMenu.zrip'      (Only if the user wants RIP)
   Search #10: 'MainMenu.zeus'      (The default)


