Structure of Menu Files

This chapter is concerned with editing menu files rather than function 
files. The structure of function files is very variable, and describing 
them all is out of the scope of this manual. Once you are confident with 
editing menu files to provide the options you wish to give your users, you 
should refer to the chapter ARexx Command Set and the AmigaGuide file 
Zeus:Docs/Manual/Rexx.guide. They contain descriptions of all the ARexx 
commands in Zeus, as well as example source of how they are used.

This is the source of a menu file.

Each menu file must contain the following segments:

Header Comment.@{uu} All scripts must have a comment to start with.

@rem <pre>
/*
** MainMenu.zeus -- The main menu.
**/
@rem </pre>

Header lines.@{uu} All scripts must have these lines in order to set the 
variable `Line' which must be set to use the Zeus ARexx commands.

@rem <pre>
Options Results

Parse Arg Line
@rem </pre>

Check that the user hasn't logged off while transferring scripts. 
ChkCarrier will return not equal to 0 if they have.

@rem <pre>
ChkCarrier

IF RC ~= 0 THEN Exit
@rem </pre>

The main "loop": user's will be stuck in this loop until they select an 
option which transfers them to another script. When a command has finished 
executing within this script it will "Signal Menuloop" to jump back to this 
point.

@rem <pre>
MenuLoop:

   Signal On Syntax
@rem </pre>

SetStatus sets the "Action" of the user, the line viewed from within the 
Voyeur program for the SysOp to keep tabs on the user and displayed to 
other users via "Who is online" doors.

@rem <pre>
   SetStatus '"At Main Menu"'
@rem </pre>

Clear the screen and display the menu file `Main/MainMenu'.

@rem <pre>
   ClrScr

   Display 'Main/MainMenu' IsMenu
@rem </pre>

Show any "events" such as node messages or 'new mail waiting' messages that 
have occured since the last check.

@rem <pre>
   ShowEvents
@rem </pre>

Loop waiting for a key, and signalling the correct `CMD_' option if the 
input is a valid option.

@rem <pre>
   DO FOREVER
      Prompt `MainMenu'
      Read IsMenu
      IF RC ~= 0 THEN Exit

      CmdString = RESULT
      Cmd = 'Signal CMD_'CmdString

      Interpret Cmd
   END
@rem </pre>

This tag is called if the user pressed the `?' key to request a redraw of 
the screen.

@rem <pre>
/*
** Redisplay menu
**/

CMD_?:
   GetUserFlag 'Expert'

   IF RC = 1 THEN Display 'Main/MainMenu'

   Signal MenuLoop
@rem </pre>

This tag is called if the user pressed the `L' key to request to logoff the 
BBS. The user is asked whether they are sure they wish to logoff, if they 
are the `Logoff/Logoff' text file is displayed to the user and the current 
script is "cleared". Clearing the script means that the `Exit' script will 
exit out of the BBS, rather than returning to the previous script.

@rem <pre>
/*
** Logoff
**/

CMD_L:
   CallScript 'File/QueryMark'

   Write '"[0;37m\nAre you sure you want to logoff? "'

   GetYesNo 'Yes'
   IF RC = 10 THEN Exit

   IF RESULT = 'Y' THEN DO
      ClrScr
      Display 'Logoff/Logout'
      ClearScript
      Exit
   END

   Signal MenuLoop
@rem </pre>

This tag is called if the user pressed the `C' key to request to leave a 
comment for the SysOp. The current "SysOp Name" settings from the 
Prefs/System program is retrieved via. ControlCode 115 and pushed onto the 
input stack.

@rem <pre>
/*
** Leave a comment to sysop...
**/

CMD_C:
   SetStatus '"Leaving feedback to Sysop"'
   RIPReset
   ChangeMsgArea ALL 1

   Write '"\n[0;36mLeaving feedback to Sysop...\n"'

   FlushInput

   ExpandCC '"%115|"'
   PushInput '"'RESULT'\n\r"'

   CallScript 'Messages/WriteMsg'

   Signal MenuLoop
@rem </pre>

This option calls the function script `Main/PageSysOp' to call the SysOp 
for a chat.

@rem <pre>
/*
** Page Sysop...
**/

CMD_P:
   SetStatus '"Paging SysOp ..."'
   SetVar ZP||Line '" "' GLOBAL
   CallScript 'Main/PageSysop'
   Signal MenuLoop
@rem </pre>

This option passes control to the `Zeus/ZeusMenu' script.

@rem <pre>
/*
** Zeus support menu...
**/

CMD_Z:
   NextScript 'Zeus/ZeusMenu'
   Exit
@rem </pre>

Through the use of the `Signal on Syntax' line used earlier we ensure that 
if none of the options above where selected then we match the keypress 
against the global command configuration and execute the ARexx segment that 
is returned. If the keypress does not match a global command then a `Signal 
Menuloop' occurs and transfers control back to the beginning of the script.

@rem <pre>
/*
** None found, check for global commands.
**/

Syntax:
   GetGlobalCmd '"'CmdString'"'
   IF RC = 0 THEN Interpret RESULT
   Signal MenuLoop
@rem </pre>

Adding a link to download your file list:

To make an option to download a particular file directly from the hard 
drive, we'd add an option such as this:

@rem <pre>
CMD_F: /* download file list */
   MarkDosFile `"Files:Filelist.lha"'
   if RC = 0 then
       CallScript `Files/Download'
   else begin
        Write `"File list not present!"'
        WaitReturn
   end
   Signal Menuloop
@rem </pre>

Removing the Zeus menu from the main menu:

If we wish to remove the option to visit the Zeus menu from the main menu 
we would simply remove the lines from CMD_Z: to Exit from the main menu 
script.


Global Commands

Global Commands are menu options available "system-wide", i.e. they can be 
accessed from any menu regardless of the code placed in the menu. Global 
Commands are configured in the file Zeus:Config/Zeus/GlobalCmds.cfg and 
take a similar format to the Editors configuration described in the 
"Message Subsystem" chapter. Pieces of Arexx are separated by a `#' 
character followed by the menu key option:

@rem <pre>
#0
NextScript 'Main/MainMenu'
Exit

#1
NextScript 'Files/FilesMenu'
Exit

#2
NextScript 'Messages/MessagesMenu'
Exit

#3
NextScript 'Doors/DoorsMenu'
Exit

#4
NextScript 'Statistics/StatisticsMenu'
Exit

#5
NextScript 'Options/EditTerminal'
Exit

#6
CallScript 'Bulletins/BulletinsMenu'
Signal MenuLoop

#Z
CallScript 'File/QueryMark'
Write '"\n[0;37mAre you sure you wish to logoff? "'

GetYesNo Yes
If RESULT = 'Y' Then Do
 Display 'LogOff/Logout'
 Exit
End
Signal MenuLoop

#$
GetUserData 'Access'
if RESULT = 255 then do
   NextScript 'SysOp/SysOpMenu'
   exit
end
Signal MenuLoop
@rem </pre>

To enable global commands in a menu you must have the line:

@rem <pre>
Signal on syntax
@rem </pre>

After the line:

@rem <pre>
Parse Arg Line
@rem </pre>

Then, at the bottom of the script you should have the lines:

@rem <pre>
Syntax:
   GetGlobalCmd '"'CmdString'"'
   IF RC = 0 THEN Interpret RESULT
   Signal MenuLoop
@rem </pre>



@endnode



@node "Manual.MultiPaths" "15 - File Area MultiPaths"

Chapter 15 - Multiple Paths for each File Area


How MultiPaths Work

File areas in Zeus are given one main path per area, defined in the main 
area edit window in Prefs/FileConfig:

@rem <PRE>

FileBase
   |
   |- Area
   |   |- Name
   |   |- Path
   |- Area
       |- Name
       |- Path

@rem </PRE>

MultiPaths allow you to give every area as many Path elements as you want:

@rem <PRE>

FileBase
   |
   |- Area
   |   |- Name
   |   |- Path
   |   |- MultiPaths
   |        |- Path
   |        |- Path
   |        |- Path
   |        |- Path
   |- Area
       |- Name
       |- Path
       |- MultiPaths
            |- Path
            |- Path

@rem </PRE>


Reading Files

When Zeus looks for a file (e.g., when a user requests it for download, or 
FileManager wants to update its offline state), it first checks the "main 
path", and if the file is not found there each MultiPath is checked for the 
file in turn.  Only the first file found is returned to the BBS, so you may 
have CD-ROM's with duplicate files (like AmiNet CD's, with the newest CD 
first) and the newest version is returned.



The Elements of a MultiPath

Each area may have as many MultiPath entries as you wish.

 · Path
The path from which to read and save files from/to.

 · R/W (Read/Write) or Read Only
Defines how Zeus sees the path.  All CD-ROMs should be set to Read Only, as 
well as any paths you don't wish Zeus to write to.  This is discussed 
further below.



Use of MultiPaths

Here several scenarios are presented in which you may find MultiPaths of 
use:

Scenario 1:@{uu}

You have AmiNet CDs online and wish to also download AmiNet from an FTP 
site to the same areas as those the CD take.
You configure the AmiNet mirror style file areas, and for each area point 
the main path to a directory on your hard disk and then one Read Only 
MultiPath for each CD.
You can now access all files from the BBS and also upload files to those 
same areas as if they were hard disk only.

@rem <PRE>
Area
 |- Path = HardDisk/Area/     <ReadWrite>
 |- MultiPaths
      |- CD1/Area/            <ReadOnly>
      |- CD2/Area/            <ReadOnly>
      |- CD3/Area/            <ReadOnly>
@rem </PRE>


Scenario 2:@{uu}

You have several hard disks, and wish to run your file base over them all.  
While with other BBS software you may have to define separate areas to each 
drive, we instead will create the same directory structure on each drive 
and in Zeus set one drive as the main path, then add the rest as read/write 
MultiPaths.
As each drive fills up, Zeus will automatically see this and begin saving 
new files to the next path which has enough disk space to spare, either the 
next new drive, or if the file is small enough to fit on a disk previously 
filled with a larger file, it will go there.

@rem <PRE>
Area
 |- Path = HardDisk1/Area/    <ReadWrite>
 |- MultiPaths
      |- CD1/Area/            <ReadOnly>
      |- HardDisk2/Area/      <ReadWrite>
      |- CD2/Area/            <ReadOnly>
@rem </PRE>


Scenario 3:@{uu}

You are running out of hard disk space, but are able to burn a CD-ROM of 
your file base and keep the CD available most of the time.
This is done in a way identical to scenario 1, but you may wish to run 
"FileManager OFFLINE ALL" when the CD is removed and reinserted, to make 
sure Zeus knows which files are currently available.



@endnode



@node "Manual.EMailGate" "16 - FidoNet over E-Mail Tunnelling"

Chapter 16 - FidoNet over E-Mail Tunnelling
or
             How to `Gate' to FidoNet Nodes via. Internet E-Mail
             ===================================================

What FidoNet over e-mail tunnelling is

"Tunnelling" is a bit of jargon from the science of computer networking. It 
is the idea of carrying one type of network over another type which is 
incompatible. In our case, we want to send FidoNet over Internet e-mail. 
This is being increasingly done elsewhere, where the new Internet address 
system, IPv6, is being `tunnelled' over older systems which only support 
the current IPv4 (which is the "192.168.1.34" type of address).

Fidonet via e-mail is an area of Fidonet which is growing daily. While 
having the disadvantage of being an indirect way of transferring mail, 
Fidonet via e-mail provides a way to cheaply distribute networks over long 
distances, such as international links.

Zeus:Bin/EMailGate is a program which allows the sending of normal Fidonet 
mail (the finished bundles and packets you see in your Outbound directory) 
over e-mail which, once at the destination, can be decoded back into real 
Fidonet bundles and packets and imported.

Zeus does not current provide facilities for decoding mail, although this 
is easily done by running a uudecoder and MIME decoder over a specifically 
named mailbox, or by using a 3rd party mail packet such as YAM and its 
import mailbox feature. We will endeavour to expand Zeus BBS's features to 
include a purpose built decoder soon (one which will allow easy integration 
with shared mailboxes, if you only have a single e-mail address, for 
example).



Usage

EMailGate can either place tunnelled mail in the Spool directory (as 
defined by Zeus's Internet prefs), ready for sending with tools like 
SMTPpost from the INetUtils package (please see the script provided below) 
or Zeus's own ZSMTPXfer which is specifically designed for the task, or it 
can use PutMail by Martin Lanza to directly send mail to your Internet 
provider's servers without using an intermediate Spool file. PutMail is 
available on AmiNet.

If the mail is placed in Spool, then it is in a raw e-mail format. This is 
suitable for sending via any other means, either by importing directly into 
a package such as YAM or even processed by some ARexx for sending by any 
method at all. As mentioned, Zeus's own ZSMTPXfer is designed to deal with 
this.

If you wish to use PutMail, you should have a directory PutMail in AmiTCP: 
in which PutMail is place (i.e., "AmiTCP:PutMail/PutMail" is the 
executable). If you use Miami or another TCP/IP stack, you will have to 
assign AmiTCP: to your Miami directory and place the PutMail directory in 
there. This is a problem which will be readdressed.



Configuration

Edit Zeus:Config/EmailTunnel.cfg@{uu}

The first line is an identification of the configuration being used, and is 
ideally used to put a short identifier to highlight that the messages being 
generated contain UUEncoded Fidonet-type mail. It is irrelevant as far as 
EMailGate is concerned.

To gate to three nodes, an example config might be:

@rem <pre>
----------------cut-----------------
FIDONET
85:1/467.0   someone@address.com
85:1/543.0   another@hello.com   UUENCODE
2:1/344.0    another@blah.com    MIME
----------------cut-----------------
@rem </pre>

The format of each line is:

@rem <pre>
<FTN address> <e-mail address> <encoding method>
@rem </pre>

(where FTN is Fido-Technology-Network, meaning anything using Fido 
software)

The encoding method allows you to send mail in either MIME Base64 or 
UUEncode formats. If you tunnel to a PC system, you might have to 
experiment to see which method will work, as some PC software used for 
tunnelling can be quite poor.

The default is UUEncode, and this is what you should use unless you have 
difficulties with it.


Load up Prefs/Archivers@{uu}

Add a new archiver "UUENCODE"

Enter data for your UUEncoder (UUxt is recommended) so that `Compress' 
uuencodes data, and `Decompress' uudecodes data. Since this is useless to 
general BBS use, deselect `Show User'.

For example:

@rem <pre>
   Name:      UUENCODE
   Compress:  c:uuxt a ~a ~f
   Extract:   c:uuxt x ~a
   Show User: NO
@rem </pre>

Leave the other fields blank.


Now add another new archiver, "MIMEENC"

This one will be used for encoding MIME data for EMailGate. There are many 
of these available on BBS's and AmiNet, I'll show you configurations for 
Base64Encoder and Base64:

@rem <pre>
   Name:      MIMEENC
   Compress:  Base64Encode ~f ~a
   Extract:   Base64Decode ~a
   Show User: NO
@rem </pre>

   or

@rem <pre>
   Name:      MIMEENC
   Compress:  Base64 ~f ~a E
   Extract:   Base64 ~a D
   Show User: NO
@rem </pre>

The Base64Encode/Decode pack should come with Zeus in the 3rd Party Utils 
archive.

Leave the other fields blank.

Run "UpdateConfigs ARC" to update this.


Using PUTMAIL:@{uu}

To use PutMail, include the PUTMAIL argument when running the program.

My email address for Fidonet is "fido@tkgbbs.freeserve.co.uk" and my 
SMTPHOST (the server to which I send e-mail) is "smtp.freeserve.co.uk" so I 
would use the following command line.

@rem <pre>
   Run Zeus:Bin/EMailGate CONFIGFILE Zeus:Config/EmailTunnel.cfg 
MAILADDRESS fido@tkgbbs.freeserve.co.uk SMTPHOST smtp.freeserve.co.uk 
ARCHIVER UUENCODE PUTMAIL COPYOLD
@rem </pre>

Demon configurations might use (for example):

@rem <pre>
Run Zeus:Bin/EMailGate CONFIGFILE Zeus:Config/EmailTunnel.cfg MAILADDRESS 
zeusnet@bleach.demon.co.uk SMTPHOST post.demon.co.uk ARCHIVER UUENCODE 
COPYOLD PUTMAIL
@rem </pre>


Using Spool:@{uu}

To spool mail to your configured Spool directory (often "UUSpool:"), simply 
leave off the PutMail argument.  You must still include all the details for 
address and SMTP host however.

@rem <pre>
Run Zeus:Bin/EMailGate CONFIGFILE Zeus:Config/EmailTunnel.cfg MAILADDRESS 
fido@tkgbbs.freeserve.co.uk SMTPHOST smtp.freeserve.co.uk ARCHIVER UUENCODE 
COPYOLD
@rem </pre>

This will leave files in Spool which can be sent using SMTPpost (from the 
INetUtils package on AmiNet) using a script such as:

@rem <pre>
------------ cut -------------------
; Send queue mail via SMTP

pcd uuspool:
List >T:SendMailQueue.temp D.#? lformat "SMTPPost -S %n*Nif not error*N 
delete %n*N delete X.%e*Nendif*N"
Execute T:SendMailQueue.temp
Delete T:SendMailQueue.temp
pcd
------------ cut -------------------
@rem </pre>

Or by running ZSMTPXfer on the directory, or processed however you wish.


COPYOLD Argument@{uu}

This argument, on the command line, will copy mail which has been sent into 
a directory called "Old" in your outbound directory. Should something go 
wrong and the mail is not delivered, you can resend it manually from here.

However no check is kept on the amount of mail kept in this directory, so 
it is up to you as SysOp to periodically clean it, perhaps on a Cron job.


ARCHIVER argument@{uu}

From Zeus 1.3, the two `archivers' used are named UUEncode and MimeEnc. 
MimeEnc is fixed, and cannot be changed; if you wish to use a different 
archiver name for the UUEncode program, it can be specified using this 
argument.


@endnode



@node "Manual.Phoenix" "17 - Phoenix: Bluewave and QWK Door"

Chapter 17 - Phoenix: The Bluewave and QWK Door


Offline Reading

An offline reader is a program which allows users of your BBS to collect 
messages, archive them and download for reading while not online.  It 
requires two programs: a client, the Offline reader program, and a server, 
the Offline reader door.  The user of the BBS will use both of these 
programs, however the Offline reader door is, as the name suggests, a door 
on your BBS rather than a program on the user's machine.

The most popular Offline formats (there are several) allow users to reply 
to messages and send these replies back up to the BBS.  Of these, there are 
two major ones which Zeus supports:  Bluewave and QWK.  Bluewave is the 
technically superior of the two, offering proper Netmail support (in which 
QWK is weak) and allowing the user to `fix' into areas while offline.  With 
QWK, the user must request areas to download while online, or send messages 
to a specific name on the BBS in a similar way to a point using AreaFix.


Configuration

All the ScriptSets supplied with Zeus include the Phoenix scripts and 
default text files.

To open the Phoenix preferences program, either open it via Workbench in 
the Prefs drawer or select QWK/Bluewave from the Messages menu in Control.

@{"Phoenix Preferences Picture" Link "phoenix.gif/main"}

BBS ID@{uu} - A short unique name for your BBS (3-5 alphanumeric characters with 
no spaces). This in effect allows users to collect mail from many BBS's and 
keep the accounts seperate.

Phone no@{uu} - The telephone number of your BBS.

Primary addr.@{uu} - The main FidoNet-style address of your BBS.

Msgs Packet@{uu} - The default maximum number of messages someone can receive in 
a packet.  If you have a large BBS, use this to stop users accidentally 
requesting huge amounts of mail.

Area@{uu} - Similar to the above, but set for each area requested not the whole 
packet.

Memory Usage@{uu} - Phoenix uses buffers to speed up message grabbing.  Set the 
size of the buffers here.


@{"Phoenix Paths Picture" Link "phoenixpaths.gif/main"}

The first four are files which will be included with the grabbed mail for 
information purposes.  The Work path is a path to store temporary files - 
it must be big enough to hold a decently sized download. Several megabytes 
at least.


@{"Phoenix Door ID Picture" Link "phoenixdoorid.gif/main"}

Set several options for Phoenix here, including the area in which it looks 
for QWK control messages.


@endnode




@node "Manual.Support" "Support"

Appendix A

Software Support


Technical support and help for your Zeus Bulletin Board System can be 
obtained in a variety of different ways. Support is available from your 
local distribution site and from the authors direct. A network feed for 
ZeusNet can be obtained from your local distribution site. If this proves 
to be too costly, or inconvenient, feeds can supplied via FidoNet to 
Internet email tunnelling, or via Internet email mailing lists.

The public ZeusNet echoes are currently:

@rem <PRE>
 TagName     | Description
 ------------+----------------------------------------------
 ZEUS_BETA   | Bug reports relating to Zeus
 ZEUS_SYSOP  | General Zeus-related chat
 ZEUS_UTIL   | Door/Utility programming for Zeus
 ZEUS_SCRIPT | ARexx script help and discussion
 ZEUS_USER   | Open for users of Zeus BBSs to chat
@rem </PRE>

To subscribe to the Internet mailing lists, please visit the Zeus 
Developments website.  In the Support section you will find details for 
subscribing.

When contacting us, please provide your Zeus serial number as obtained from 
the "About" option on the main Zeus control screen.


Support Sites


An up to date list of support sites is always available from the Zeus 
Developments BBS in the UK, and from the Internet WWW page:

 http://www.bleach.demon.co.uk/zeus/


@endnode


@node "Manual.HowDoI" "How do I..."

@rem <PRE>
 @{" Log-on to the local line                   " Link "Logon"}
 @{" Upgrade my access level?                   " Link "AccLevel"}
 @{" Make my BBS available to remote callers    " Link "Callers"}
 @{" Shut down Zeus                             " Link "Shutdown"}
 @{" Tell what all these funky icons do?        " Link "Icons"}
 @{" See what users are doing on the BBS        " Link "Watch"}
 @{" Edit the menu bars of programs?            " Link "MenuBars"}
 @{" Enable RIP Graphics                        " Link "RIP"}

 @{" Macro Keys available on line watchers      " Link "Macros"}
@rem </PRE>

@endnode

@Node "Icons" "What are all these icons for?"

Main Icons:

 Two men talking/Ignoring each other:

   This icon allows you to toggle whether you are available for paging.
When the men are ignoring each other it means you are not available. This
icon is created by a program called Utils/ChatStatus and the image is
taken from the file Zeus:Config/Icons/ChatStatus.info, which is a standard
icon file.

  ZEUS icon with a line number underneath:

    These icon(s) indicate that an ANSI watcher is loaded/iconified.
Clicking on one will pop up the watcher for the applicable line. If it
is a local line then pressing Return will log you into the BBS. A watcher 
allows you to see what the remote user is doing, chat with them or
navigate them round your BBS.

  FED (Filing cabinet and scissors)

     This icon is created by Bin/ZeusDock, Zeus' internal Dock program
which is configured with the Prefs/DockCfg program. Pressing this icon
will execute Utils/FileEditor.

  UED (Surgeon with patient)

     This icon is also created by Bin/ZeusDock Pressing this icon will
execute Utils/UserEditor.

  US style Mailbox 

      This icon is created with the Bin/PostMan program. The icon
images are taken from Zeus:Config/Icons/(No)Mail.info. This program
monitors the SysOp's mailbox (the SysOp name is taken from the IEMSI
Information settings as defined in Prefs/System and changes its
image when mail is written or imported for the SysOp.

  Help button 

       Another ZeusDock icon. Loads this help file using Multiview on
the ZEUS screen. Configure the viewer by loading up Prefs/DockCfg,
selecting "Dock/Help" and changing the "Execute" string gadget.

Voyeur Icons:

 Jack in the Box

       "Pops" up the selected line. This will uniconify the watcher
selected, or launch a new one by calling the "Zeus:Rexx/PopUpWatcher"
script.

 Boot

       Boots the user on the selected line off the BBS. This will
terminate their call!

 Puzzled Man

       Shows pertinent information about the user currently selected.

 Surgeon and Patient

       Loads up "Utils/UserEditor" with the current user already
selected.

 Broadcast Mast

       Sends a "node message" to the user currently onlien.

 Iconify

       Iconifies "Utils/Voyeur" into a small icon on the control screen.
This icon is configurable from the file "Zeus:Config/Icons/Voyeur.info"

@endnode

@Node "Macros" "Macro Keys available on line watchers"

   F1 -- Start/End Chat
   F2 -- View details of the user online
   F7 -- Edit personal details of the user online
   F8 -- Edit account details of the user online
   F9 -- Edit message details of the user online
   F0 -- Edit file details of the user online

 S-F1 -- Log actions of user in LOGS:Logger.<Line>
 S-F0 -- Kick current user

These are, of course, just defaults and can be easily modified from the 
ANSI watcher main screen.

@endnode

@Node "AccLevel" "How do I upgrade my access level?"

New user's on Zeus have an access level of 0 (New User). In the Zeus
demonstration, two access levels are defined (New User and SysOp). You
can add new access levels using the Prefs/Templates program.

If you wish to upgrade your access:

 1) @{"Logon" Link "LogOn"} to a BBS.
 2) Press F8 (see @{"Macros" Link "Macros"})
 3) Press the Sel button next to "Access Level"
 4) Double click on "255 - SysOp", select Yes to the prompt.
 5) Close the Window
 6) Press Save User
 7) You will be updated automatically and have SysOp privelidges.

@endnode

@Node "Callers" "Activating a remote line"

Zeus is not set up to receive calls by default. Callers may be accepted
using one of the following Zeus programs.

 - Connector     -- A simple phone answerer.

   To install "The Connector" on Line 1 of your Zeus BBS, load up
   "Prefs/Progs". This preferences program is a list of the programs
   that Zeus loads after the main executable has set up.

   1) Press "Add"
   2) Delete "(new)" from the string gadget and add the following line:
      "Zeus:Utils/Connector LINE 1", minus the double-quotes.
   3) Press "Save"
   4) Press "Quit"

   @{"Shutdown" Link "Shutdown"} Zeus and reload it.


 - ZMailer       -- Zeus' own FidoNet Mailer.

   The commercial version of Zeus comes with it's own fully-featured
   FidoNet mailer. This can be used to launch the BBS, but is not
   supplied with the demo version.


 - NetStart      -- Starts Zeus from a TCP/IP telnet session.

   This program makes setting Zeus up for Internet telnet access easy,
   meaning Internet users can access your BBS from anywhere in the
   world at local phone rates! NetStart is not included with the demo
   version of Zeus.


 - MailerStart   -- Start the BBS from a mailer such as Trapdoor.

   Zeus supports the use of most Amiga front-door mailers to start
   the BBS. 
   
   TrapDoor

   The following should be added to your MAIL:TrapDoor.cfg

@rem <PRE>
   --- cut ---
   BBSCOMMAND "ZEUS:Bin/System/MailerStart 1 %b %B"
   BBSINOUT ""
   BBSMODE SPAWN
   --- cut ---
@rem </PRE>

   GMS

   Add the following to GMS:Config/Mailer.1/MailerSettings

@rem <PRE>
   --- cut ---
   BBSMode=Spawn
   BBSCommand="ZEUS:Bin/System/MailerStart 1 %b %B"
   BBSCommandStack=50000
   --- cut ---
@rem </PRE>

   JamMail

   Configure JamMail for a standard bbs, and use this BBS launch command:

@rem <PRE>
   --- cut ---
   1. Command: zeus
   2. TextFile:""
   3. Execute: ZEUS:Bin/System/MailerStart $(line) $(baudlocked) $(baud)
   4. PostBBS:
   5. Check Error: Enabled
   --- cut ---
@rem </PRE>

@endnode

@Node "Shutdown" "Shut down Zeus"

Zeus is loaded as a commodity and can be shut down in a number of
different ways.


 1)  Use the hotkey shortcut CTRL-ESC.
 2)  Load up SYS:Tools/Commodities/Exchange, select Zeus BBS from the 
listview and press the Remove button.
 3)  Send a QuitZeus command to the ARexx port ZEUS. This can be acheived 
with the following script:

@rem <PRE>
      ---------------cut----------------
      /*
      ** Close Down Zeus
      */

      Options Results

      Address 'ZEUS'

      QuitZeus
      ---------------cut----------------
@rem </PRE>

 4)  Double click on the "Zeus:Shortcuts/BBS/ShutdownZeus" icon.

@endnode

@Node "Logon" "Logon to the local line"

Upon loading Zeus, the Watcher is automatically loaded. This starts up in 
iconified mode. To deiconify the watcher, click on the icon marked "Line 0" 
on the Control Screen.

This will bring up the watcher interface. Various aspects of the watcher 
configuration can be changed from this public screen. Simply hit Return to 
logon to the local line.

If you quit the watcher, it can be reactivated by selecting "ANSI 0" from 
the Progs menu on the Control Screen, or by running "Zeus:Bin/Watchers/ANSI 
<Line>" from a CLI, where Line is the line you wish to watch.

@endnode

@Node "MenuBars" "Configuration of MenuBars"

The menubars of many programs in Zeus may be configured. This is done 
through the Prefs/MenuEditor utility. This editor allows you to edit the 
Menu titles, items and sub-items of individual programs and assign 
executable programs to them.

For instance, to add an option to "Boot Users off" from the Watcher screen, 
follow these steps:

 1) Load up Prefs/MenuEditor
 2) Press Amiga-O (Open) and load up the file:

        Zeus:Config/Watchers/Menus.Dat

 2) Press the "Add" button in the "Menus" section.
 3) Replace "(New)" with "User" (the name of the menu we are creating).
 4) Select "User" from the listview.
 5) Press the "Add" button in the "Items" section.
 6) Replace "(New)" with "Boot User" (the description of the menu item).
 7) Place the following data in the gadgets:

         CommKey: B
      Executable: Zeus:Bin/BootUser %n
            Type: Executable
           Stack: 4096

    Note that the "%n" item in Executable will be expanded to the current 
line number.

 8) Close the window and choose Save.
 9) Select "Reload Menus" from the "Project" menu of any watcher.
10) Et voila! We have a new menu options.

Other programs that allow configurable menu bars are:

@rem <PRE>
 Program      File
------------+------------------------------------------
            | 
 FileEditor | Zeus:Config/File/FileEditorMenus.Dat
 UserEditor | Zeus:Config/Zeus/UserEditorMenus.Dat
 Control    | Zeus:Config/Control/Menus.Dat
 Mailer     | Zeus:Config/Msg/MailerMenu.Dat
 Watchers   | Zeus:Config/Watchers/Menus.Dat
            |
------------+------------------------------------------
@rem </PRE>

@endnode

@Node "Watch" "How do I watch the users"

 To find out who is online and what they are doing:

  Run "Utils/Voyeur" - this gives you a realtime view of what the current
user is doing online.

 To watch and interact with a remote user:

 If the "Line 1" icon is present on the Control Screen, press this.
Otherwise run "Zeus:Bin/Watchers/ANSI 1" to bring up the watcher screen,
alternatively in Voyeur, select the remote line and press the "PopUp"
icon.

 Chat with an online user:

 Press F1 on the remote watcher screen.

@endnode

@Node "RIP" "Enabling Remote Imaging Protocol"

To enable RIP graphics you must load the RIP server. This works in 
conjunction with the ANSI watcher (Zeus:Bin/Watchers/ANSI) to provide 16-
colour EGA graphics over a terminal.

To load RIP you should select "RIP Server 0" from the Progs menu on the 
Control screen. Then you should logon to the BBS and select "RIP graphics 
on" from the "edit" menu of any BBS.

The RIP public screen is called RIP.<Line>, where <Line> is the BBS line 
number.

@endnode


@node Manual.Overview "Zeus BBS Overview"

Appendix B

Overview of Zeus BBS

Zeus:
+---@{"Mail" link "ov.Mail"}
+---@{"Prefs" link "ov.Prefs"}
+---@{"Rexx" link "ov.Rexx"}
|   +---FileAreas
|   +---FileEditor
|   +---Files
|   +---InterNet
|   +---Mailer
|   +---Messages
|   +---MsgAreas
|   +---Stats
|   +---UserEditor
|   +---Users
|   +---Voyeur
|   +---AmiNet
|   \\---Config
