@node ov.Mail "Mail Directory"

 Mail Directory

This is the default directory set by Zeus for storing the message bases.

If you run a BBS without any FidoNet networks or Internet, you can probably 
leave this as the mail directory. However, if you intend to have a large 
amount of mail moving through your BBS, it is a good idea to put the mail 
directory on a different, perhaps dedicated partition to that on which the 
Zeus program is stored.

@endnode


@node ov.Prefs "Prefs Directory"

 Prefs Directory

This is where the programs for changing Zeus preferences are stored. They 
can be accessed via Workbench or the shell, but most are usable only 
graphically.

Most aspects of Zeus are set via these programs.  ZMP is one of the very 
few which require configuring via a text file.  However, even when a 
program is required to alter the configuration it is possible to generate a 
text configuration which can be recompiled by using programs in the Rexx 
directory.

@endnode


@node ov.Rexx "Rexx Directory"

 Rexx Directory

This contains many ARexx programs which complement Zeus BBS.

These programs are not required to run Zeus, the purpose of them is to make 
running Zeus easier.  Some of the programs included are:

 . ZAmiNet - A program to facilitate turning your BBS (or a section or SIG 
of it) into an AmiNet mirror.
 . Config
 . FileAreas
 . MessageAreas - These are some programs to allow you to configure parts 
of Zeus with the Prefs/ directory programs, and without using ARexx.  
Studying these programs might give you an insight into ARexx which could 
help you develop powerful, automated configuration scripts!

@endnode


@node ov.Scripts "Scripts Directory"

 Scripts Directory

The "power" behind your BBS.

These determine what the user will see, what they can do, and their whole 
online experience.  There is far too much to go into here, but this manual 
contains a whole chapter on it, and the Zeus ARexx Reference is dedicated 
to this.

@endnode


@node ov.Shortcuts "Shortcuts Directory"

 Shortcuts Directory

Short scripts to help you run the BBS from Workbench.

The scripts in here invoke Zeus shell programs, in order to perform some 
simple but necessary functions, such as trimming the message bases.

@endnode


@node ov.Text "Text Directory"

 Text Directory

This contains the text files (ANSI, RIP, and plain) which are shown to 
users when called by your ScriptSet.

@endnode


@node ov.Users "Users Directory"

 Users Directory

In here the user data files are held, and also this is where each user's 
personal directory is kept.

@endnode


@node ov.Utils "Utils Directory"

 Utils Directory

This contains many useful, and sometimes necessary programs to assist 
running your BBS.

@endnode


@node ov.Developers "Developers Directory"

 Developers Directory

In here is technical information on the Zeus system libraries. If you are 
developing doors or other utilities in C, E or assembler, these are the 
files you require.

If you are using ARexx exclusively, you don't require these files. See 
rexx.guide for information on Zeus' comprehensive ARexx interface.

@endnode

@node ov.Bin "Bin Directory"

 Bin Directory

Some programs can be run only from the shell and not Workbench. This 
directory contains those programs and also some `system' programs which 
Zeus itself makes use of.

@endnode


@node ov.Config "Config Directory"

 Config Directory

Here is where the configuration files for Zeus are kept.  In general, the 
files immediately in Zeus:Config/ are those which are modified "by hand" 
using a text editor.  Files in subdirectories are those modified by the 
programs in Zeus:Prefs/ and are generally not needed to be modified 
manually.

@endnode


@node ov.Conversion "Conversion Directory"

 Conversion Directory

These tools are helpfull in converting any configurations you may have from 
a previous BBS package.  There are also some shortcuts in Zeus:Shortcuts/ 
to assist you in using these.

@endnode


@node ov.Docs "Docs Directory"

 Docs Directory

All documentation to help you run Zeus is kept in this directory.

@endnode


@node ov.Doors "Doors Directory"

 Doors Directory

These are the programs which run over your BBS for users to use.  You may 
need to alter configurations in here from some doors.

Doors can be of several types:

ZDoors - special executable programs specifically for Zeus.
CLI - any command line interface (shell) program can be launched to run 
over the BBS.
Generic ARexx - run just like any ARexx script, like a shell program.
Zeus ARexx - an ARexx script which is run exactly like a script from the 
Zeus scriptset.


@endnode


@node ov.Logs "Logs Directory"

 Logs Directory

This is the default location for Zeus to store its log files.

@endnode


@node Manual.CLI_Templates "Command Line Interface Templates"

Appendix C

Command Line Interface Templates

Although this is covered in the AmigaDOS manual, for those who don't own a 
copy here is some information essential to using Zeus effectively (while 
the DOS manual isn't included with some Amiga computers, all copies of OS 
3.5 include it in HTML and PDF format).

When using the Zeus command line tools, you'll see templates such as this 
when you run the program with no arguments (or with arguments of "?"):

@rem <PRE>
Usage: PostMsg 
FROM/A,TO/A,SUBJECT/A,TEXTFILE/A,AREA/N,ADDRESS,PRIVATE/S,NOWRAP/S,TAG/K

   FROM         - Author of message
   TO           - Message recipient
   SUBJECT      - Message topic
   TEXTFILE     - File to retrieve message text from
   AREA         - Area number to save message in
   ADDRESS      - Destination FidoNet address (netmails)
   PRIVATE      - Mark message as private
   NOWRAP       - Don't wrap message lines
   TAG          - If AREA is not given, this Fido Echo name will be looked 
up.
@rem </PRE>

The table is displayed by the Zeus tools themselves to give verbose 
descriptions.  The template, however, is the long capitalised line near the 
top.  This is used by the Amiga Operating System to provide a uniform and 
powerful interface to all Amiga command line programs.  As such the same 
sort of things are seen in almost all Amiga programs runable from the Shell 
prompt.

At the end of each argument (i.e. each element you can input) there is a 
slash "/" followed by a single character, followed either by another slash 
and character or a comma which indicates another argument option is 
starting.  The character after the slash tells you exactly how the argument 
behaves:

 /A -- This is a required argument.  It must be given for the command to 
run.

 /S -- This is a switch.  If provided by entering its name in full, you 
will turn that item on.

 /K -- A "keyword".  These are used to remove any ambiguity by requiring 
you to enter both the argument name and the option you want.  For example, 
for TAG/K you must enter "TAG=name".  You can omit the equals sign, but 
your data must follow the keyword.

 /N -- A number.  Only a number is acceptable for this option.

 /F -- The full command line.  This means that everything entered after 
this argument is taken as being part of this argument, whether there are 
spaces or not.

 /M -- Multiple strings, not used much in Zeus.  It means you can enter 
more than one item for this argument, similar to /F but other options such 
as switches can be included.

 /T -- Toggle. Rarely used, since a switch performs a simiar function. This 
can toggle an option on and off each time a program is run, but the program 
must save the previous setting.


Often you will want to include an option which contains a space, such as 
the above example for the PostMsg command where the From argument might be 
Fred Bloggs, for example.  To do this, simply enclose the option in double 
quotes: "Fred Bloggs".

@endnode



@node Manual.FAQ "Frequently Asked Questions"

Appendix D

Frequently Asked Questions

 Q ZMP - I cannot import file attaches if they have a .TIC or .MO0, etc., 
file extension.

 A These file extensions are usually reserved for FidoNet bundles and tick 
echoes. ZMP ignores these files incase they are supposed to be imported 
normally. Some packages for the PC actually attach Tick data files to 
netmails, and if Zeus were to import this as an attachment then the Tick 
would not be processed!


 Q I am no good at ARexx or programming, what can I do?

 A You don't need to alter the Zeus ScriptSet. If you are happy with the 
provided scripts, then you don't need to change them. If you really wish to 
change something, ask for assistance in ZeusNet. If you're lucky somebody 
may be available to perform the change for you.


 Q My modem reports CONNECT strings like:
      CONNECT 57600/V34/LAPM/V42BIS/28800:TX/31200:RX
   where 57600 is only my computer to modem speed. Does Zeus correctly 
detect this? Or can I change my modem's results?

 A Zeus takes the receiving speed (":RX") of such strings. You can change 
how the modem reports the speed, but with V90 modems it's often not a good 
idea. At least, my modem is incapable of any decend speed report which 
includes modem to modem and compression information. Remember that many 
other programs you could use to answer the 'phone will most likely still 
take the modem speed as being 57600.


 Q ZMailer doesn't seem to load at all.

 A ZMailer must have a working Nodelist installation, using the TrapList 
library.  In addition to this, you must have "Nodelist:" assigned to the 
directory holding the nodelist's data.


 Q My system crashed while ZMP was running, and although it finished 
importing (it was in the export phase) none of the messages are on the BBS!

 A The new high message number wasn't written to disk - run "mbmanager 
update all" to fix this, then run "zmp export" to finish the mail run.


 Q When dialling out using a TelSer node and ZMailer, TelSer always reports 
that the IP or name address is wrong, even though I can telnet and ping to 
it.  Why?

 A The most likely reason is that ZMailer is sending the dial command to 
TelSer as "ATD<host>".  TelSer doesn't like this, it requires "ATD <host>" 
with space.  To fix this, simply open ZMailer Preferences from Control, and 
for your TelSer nodes set the dial command to "ATD ", with the extra space 
at the end.

@endnode



@node Manual.Glossary "Glossary of BBS terms"

Appendix E 

Glossary of BBS terms 


A

Area@{uu} - A section of your BBS which holds some kind of data: a file area or 
a message area for example.


B

Bulletin Board System (BBS)@{uu} - What Zeus is as a whole; a system to provide 
services such as files, messages, games, and interactive chatrooms to 
people who connect to it, usually via a direct modem connection but 
increasingly via TelNet over the Internet.


C

Cron@{uu} - A program which executes commands or scripts a set times of the day. 
A Cron program is usually very configurable in the times it can be set to 
execute commands, allowing you to set ranges of days, specific days, groups 
of hours or minutes, and much more. A freeware Cron program (proven to be 
extremely reliable) is supplied with Zeus in the 3rd party utilities 
archive, called "cRon". The Cron program originated on Unix systems so is 
usually configured via a text file.


D


E

Echo@{uu} - The method by which FidoNet-type networks move data.  Each BBS that 
receives a message (or file) will copy that data for itself, then produce 
copies (the data is "echoed") for other BBSs, which will repeat this.  It 
also applies to a FidoNet area, as they are called "Echoes" after the 
method by which they are distributed.

Export@{uu} - if an item is taken out of a collection of items for, e.g., 
sending to another computer, it has been exported.  FidoNet messages 
written on your BBS will be exported by ZMP for collection of sending to 
other BBS's.


F

FileControl@{uu} - The program which must be running for the Files sub-system of 
Zeus to be active.  So, to upload, download or even just view file 
statistics, FileControl must be running.  By default, Zeus launches it 
itself when starting, via the StartUp preferences.

FileConfig@{uu} - A program in the Prefs/ drawer with which you define what file 
areas are on your BBS.


G


H

Hatch@{uu} - When someone says that they `Hatch' a file, they mean they are 
putting a file and tick into a file/tick echo, and every BBS linked to that 
echo will get the file.  See also: Tick


I

Import@{uu} - Import means to put an item or items into a greater, often 
structured collection.  For example, we say that we Import FidoNet messages 
into the Zeus message base, where they are separated out into areas/echoes 
for reading.  See also: Export


J


K


L


M

MsgControl@{uu} - The program which must be running for the Messages sub-system 
of Zeus to be active.  So, to read, write or even just view messages 
statistics, MsgControl must be running.  By default, Zeus launches it 
itself when starting, via the StartUp preferences.

MsgConfig@{uu} - A program in the Prefs/ drawer with which you define what 
Messages areas are on your BBS.  This does not define which areas are 
available from FidoNet or the Internet, that is done in conjunction with 
the Config/ZMP.cfg text preferences file.


N

NodeList@{uu} - The phonebook of FidoNet or FidoNet-type networks.  These 
special files contain lists of every node in that particular network, and 
are usually distributed by tick echoes.


O


P


Q


R


S

Special Interest Group (SIG)@{uu} - A division of your BBS into which a number 
of message and/or file areas are grouped, in order to break a large BBS 
into more manageable parts.  See also: Areas


T

Tick@{uu} - A tick is a small file (with the extension .TIC) which is sent along 
with files over FidoNet.  The tick carries information about the file, such 
as what echo area it belongs to, where it came from, and what the 
description of the file is. A tick is used to move files around FidoNet-
type networks, in exactly the same way as messages move around in echo 
areas.  See also: Hatch

Tunnelling@{uu} - Transmitting one type of network through a virtual `tunnel' in 
another type of network.


U


V


W


X


Y


Z

Zeus@{uu} - The greatest bulletin board software the world has ever seen?  You 
decide!


@endnode



